[{"body":"Review all 35 incoming human GSDME annotations, preserving the original records, product declarations and one restored extension. The review includes independent source provenance and explicit consistency exceptions. No NEW annotations are added.\n\nGenuine native helpers completed 22 missing records and preserved 17 existing source records. Gene, reference, history, family and cache-order checks passed; HTML is current. Reference validation checked one excerpt and 29 titles with no issues. The actual 951-family corpus had zero conflicts, with unrelated unresolved entries retained.\n\nThe computed review status remains DRAFT with six advisory warnings: the table checker calls three exceptions stale, while the legacy same-term checker flags the corresponding three deliberately distinct contexts. These judgments were independently reviewed and were retained. This PR remains draft pending exact-head review and CI.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/clingen-gsdme-20261107","number":6187,"title":"Review human GSDME annotations and product context","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6187"},{"body":"QuickGO annotations with an ECO identifier and null `goEvidence` currently fail TSV export. The import now preserves the raw ECO value and empty GO shorthand, and uses the supplied ECO identifier when no shorthand exists. This allows the actual CACAO `ECO:0007744` annotation on A5FI22 to be seeded and matched without inventing IMP, ISS, EXP or ND evidence.\n\nThe schema explicitly accepts `ECO:0007744`; unknown evidence values still fail. Existing GO shorthands retain precedence, optional null display fields become empty TSV cells, and repeated seeding retains strict annotation identity. The change includes the generated model, source documentation and schema history. No gene review payloads are changed here.\n\nValidation: 120 focused tests passed (eight deselected), Ruff, LinkML and history checks passed. `just validate-all` passed on `2564a3fb412d7a561ca8fd165128294e2e496016`: 10,782 reviews, zero blocking errors, and 54 pathway reference checks. Existing advisory warnings remain. The committed validation receipt records the tested files and log hash.\n\nThis is a prerequisite for the Lol trafficking module audit, whose Flavobacterium Fjoh_2111 lipoprotein-carrier exemplar carries the ECO-only annotation.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-eco-only-goa-import","number":6186,"title":"Preserve ECO-only evidence in QuickGO imports","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6186"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nCurates **Tranche 4** (12 *Variovorax paradoxus* TBEA6 `VARPD` gene reviews) of the ***Variovorax*: Rhizosphere Plant–Microbe Interactions & Critical Metal Recovery (`VARIOVORAX`)** project, stacked on `cmungall/aigr-variovorax-tranche3` (PR #6137), bringing the total curated *Variovorax* corpus to **46 genes across 4 tranches**.\n\n### Batch 4A — Alkanesulfonate ABC Importer (`ssuA2`, `ssuB2`, `ssuC5`), NADPH-FMN Reductase (`ssuE`) & Taurine ABC Importer (`tauA`, `tauB1`, `tauC`)\n1. **`VARPD/ssuA2` (`A0A0H2M8H9`, `VPARA_29980`, 330 aa):** Periplasmic aliphatic sulfonate-binding protein of the `ssuB2-ssuC5-ssuA2` (`VPARA_29960-VPARA_29980`) ABC importer (`NCBIfam:TIGR01451`, `Pfam:PF09084`). `ACCEPT` `GO:0046306` (`alkanesulfonate transport`); `NEW` `GO:0030288` (`outer membrane-bounded periplasmic space`) and `GO:0055052` (`ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter complex, substrate-binding subunit-containing`), contributing to complex-level `GO:0015419` (`aliphatic sulfonate transmembrane transporter activity`).\n2. **`VARPD/ssuB2` (`A0A0H2MAS0`, `VPARA_29960`, 272 aa, EC 7.6.2.14):** Aliphatic sulfonates import ATP-binding protein (`HAMAP:MF_01723`, `PANTHER:PTHR42788:SF20`, `PROSITE:PS50893`) with conserved Walker A (`41-GPSGCGKT-48`) and Walker B (`153-LLLD-156`) motifs. `ACCEPT` `GO:0005524`, `GO:0015419`, `GO:0016887`, `GO:0046306`; `NEW` `GO:0005886` (`plasma membrane`) and `GO:0055052`.\n3. **`VARPD/ssuC5` (`A0A0H2MD44`, `VPARA_29970`, 298 aa):** Integral 6-TM inner-membrane permease subunit of SsuABC (`PANTHER:PTHR30409:SF6`, `PROSITE:PS50928`). `ACCEPT` `GO:0005886`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0016020`; `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` on standalone `GO:0022857` and `GO:0058024` (per multi-subunit ABC permease convention, with complex contribution to `GO:0015419` captured via `contributes_to_molecular_function`); `NEW` `GO:0046306` and `GO:0055052`.\n4. **`VARPD/ssuE` (`A0A0H2MC24`, `VPARA_36280`, 190 aa, EC 1.5.1.38):** Cytosolic homodimeric/homotetrameric NADPH-dependent FMN reductase (`NCBIfam:TIGR01727`, `Pfam:PF03358`, `PANTHER:PTHR30097:SF7`) supplying reduced $\\text{FMNH}_2$ to alkanesulfonate monooxygenase SsuD. `ACCEPT` `GO:0005829`, `GO:0010181`, `GO:0052591`, `GO:0071284`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0016491`, `GO:0016651`, `GO:0050661`.\n5. **`VARPD/tauA` (`A0A0H2M727`, `VPARA_03650`, 318 aa):** Periplasmic taurine-binding protein of the `tauA-tauB1-tauC` (`VPARA_03650-VPARA_03670`) ABC importer (`HAMAP:MF_01666`, `PANTHER:PTHR30024:SF47`, `NCBIfam:TIGR01728`). `ACCEPT` `GO:0015734` (`taurine transmembrane transport`) and `GO:0030288`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0042597`; `NEW` `GO:1902935` (`taurine binding`) and `GO:0055052`.\n6. **`VARPD/tauB1` (`A0A0H2MNF8`, `VPARA_03660`, 255 aa, EC 7.6.2.7):** Taurine import ATP-binding protein (`HAMAP:MF_01667`, `PANTHER:PTHR42788:SF18`, `PROSITE:PS50893`) with Walker A (`37-GPSGCGKT-44`) and Walker B (`156-LLAD-159`). `ACCEPT` `GO:0005524`, `GO:0005886`, `GO:0015411`, `GO:0015734`, `GO:0016887`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0058024` and `GO:0140359`; `NEW` `GO:0055052`.\n7. **`VARPD/tauC` (`A0A0H2M7Y1`, `VPARA_03670`, 294 aa):** 6-TM taurine ABC permease subunit (`HAMAP:MF_01668`, `PANTHER:PTHR30409:SF9`, `PROSITE:PS50928`). `ACCEPT` `GO:0005886` and `GO:0015734`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0016020`; `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` on standalone `GO:0022857` and `GO:0058024` (matching `PSEPK/tauC`); `NEW` `GO:0055052`.\n\n### Batch 4B — Heavy-Metal Resistance Island Completion (`czcI`, `czcR`, `cusS`, `cadA2`) & Ferric Uptake Regulator (`fur1`)\n8. **`VARPD/czcI` (`A0A0H2LVR9`, `VPARA_44920`, 123 aa):** Periplasmic heavy-metal resistance modulator (`NCBIfam:NF045614`, `Pfam:PF11162`, `InterPro:IPR055013`) containing a histidine-rich cluster (`36-HWGHHEHE-43`) and `71-CAVCH-75` motif encoded between `czcCBA` and `czcR-cusS`. `ACCEPT` `GO:0046686` (`response to cadmium ion`); `NEW` `GO:0010043` (`response to zinc ion`) and `GO:0046872` (`metal ion binding`).\n9. **`VARPD/czcR` (`A0A0H2LVW5`, `VPARA_44930`, 225 aa):** Two-component OmpR/PhoB-family response regulator (`NCBIfam:TIGR01387`, `PROSITE:PS50110` Asp51 receiver domain, `PROSITE:PS51755` winged-helix DNA-binding domain). `ACCEPT` `GO:0000156`, `GO:0000160`, `GO:0000976`, `GO:0005829`; `MODIFY` `GO:0006355` -> `GO:0045893` (`positive regulation of DNA-templated transcription`); `KEEP_AS_NON_CORE` `GO:0032993`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0003677`; `NEW` `GO:0001216` (`DNA-binding transcription activator activity`) and `GO:0010043`.\n10. **`VARPD/cusS` (`A0A0H2MBG2`, `VPARA_44940`, 443 aa, EC 2.7.13.3):** Inner-membrane heavy-metal sensor histidine kinase CzcS/CusS (`NCBIfam:TIGR01386`, `PROSITE:PS50885` HAMP domain, `PROSITE:PS50109` histidine kinase domain with autocatalytic `His233`). `ACCEPT` `GO:0000155`, `GO:0000160`, `GO:0005886`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0004673`, `GO:0007165`, `GO:0016020`, `GO:0016301`, `GO:0016740`, `GO:0016772`; `NEW` `GO:0005524` (`ATP binding`), `GO:0046777` (`protein autophosphorylation`), and `GO:0010043`.\n11. **`VARPD/cadA2` (`A0A0H2LVW9`, `VPARA_44980`, 759 aa, EC 7.2.2.12):** P1B-2-type $\\text{Zn}^{2+}$/$\\text{Cd}^{2+}$-translocating efflux ATPase (`NCBIfam:TIGR01512`, `PANTHER:PTHR48085:SF5`, `PROSITE:PS00154`) with N-terminal HMA domain (`26-CCGSTAC-32`, `57-MDC-59`), intramembrane `410-CPC-412` motif, `296-TGES-299` phosphatase loop, and `Asp454` (`454-DKTGTIT-460`) phosphorylation site. `ACCEPT` `GO:0005524`, `GO:0005886`, `GO:0016463` (`P-type zinc transporter activity`), `GO:0046872`, `GO:0071577`; `NEW` `GO:0015086` (`cadmium ion transmembrane transporter activity`), `GO:0070574` (`cadmium ion transmembrane transport`), `GO:0000287` (`magnesium ion binding`).\n12. **`VARPD/fur1` (`A0A0H2M8T9`, `VPARA_18200`, 139 aa):** Cytosolic ferric uptake transcriptional repressor (`NCBIfam:NF006999`, `Pfam:PF01475`, `PANTHER:PTHR33202:SF2`) with structural $\\text{Zn}^{2+}$ site (`Cys92, Cys95, Cys132, Cys135`) and regulatory $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$/$\\text{Mn}^{2+}$ site (`His86, Asp88, Glu107, His124`). `ACCEPT` `GO:0000976`, `GO:0005829`, `GO:0006879`, `GO:0045892`; `MODIFY` `GO:0001216` -> `GO:0001217` (`DNA-binding transcription repressor activity`) and `GO:0045893` -> `GO:0045892`; `KEEP_AS_NON_CORE` `GO:0008270`, `GO:0030145`, `GO:0032993`; `NEW` `GO:0008198` (`ferrous iron binding`).\n\n### Module & Project Updates\n- Updated `modules/taurine_uptake_and_desulfonation.yaml` (`tauA`, `tauB1`, `tauC`), `modules/variovorax_metallophore_metal_homeostasis.yaml` (`fur1`, `cadA2`, `czcI`, `czcR`, `cusS`), and `modules/bacterial_tonb_siderophore_import.yaml` (`fur1` evidence).\n- Updated `projects/VARIOVORAX.md`, `projects/VARIOVORAX/VARIOVORAX_MODULE_PLAN.md`, rendered HTML pages, and added history records under `history/`.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-variovorax-tranche4","number":6185,"title":"Curate Variovorax Tranche 4: SsuABC-SsuE, TauABC, CzcIRS, CadA2, and Fur1","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6185"},{"body":"Bacterial lipoprotein maturation now distinguishes the shared Lgt/LspA core from five optional terminal routes: Lnt, Lnb, LnsAB, Lit and LhaT. Eleven manually assessed references, including three reviews, ground 23 findings and individual reaction steps. Reviewed exemplars span Pseudomonadota, Bacillota and Bacteroidota; taxon Bacteria defines an occurrence envelope, not universal distribution or essentiality.\n\nFourteen complete gene reviews preserve 75 inherited annotation rows, including three historical NEW proposals, and add 13 supported annotations in five normally seeded genes. Four PANTHER family reviews preserve their inherited PAINT assertions and qualify broader activity transfer. Verified InterPro/NCBIfam descriptors cover alternative tailoring enzymes. Capacity checks pass 35 evidence-qualified cases and detect 10 mutations, with separate gates for compatible LnsAB partners, carrier requirements and terminal product identity.\n\nFull validation passed on science commit `06a98cd0530c1b0e462a053f68dd5cabec2bf306`: 10,771 reviews, zero blocking errors, 54 pathway checks and 341 frozen inputs verified. Advisory warnings are recorded in `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_lipoprotein_maturation/full-validation-proof.json`. Subsequent main integration preserves the scientific inputs; focused module checks and repository Ruff pass. Twelve frozen validation logs are included as supplemental provenance. Review, CI and actual merge remain pending.\n\nLhaT reuses the reviewed BACSU ypjA gene from #6167 without changing its bytes. This module-specific audit overlaps the broader module material in #5076; it does not subsume that PR's other work.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-lipoprotein-taxon-audit","number":6184,"title":"Audit cross-species bacterial lipoprotein maturation and tailoring","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6184"},{"body":"Reviews GRXCR2 with all 11 incoming contexts preserved and one explicitly inferred ISS process proposal. The follow-up retains the existing actions, adds the process to the single core, clarifies source boundaries, and supplies checkable quotations and independent evidence pointers.\n\nScoped validation, strict GOA matching, reference validation, status computation, history validation and rendering pass. All three snippets and three publication titles were checked with zero issues or skipped snippets. Computed status is COMPLETE with zero review warnings; runtime deprecation notices remain in the logs. The six normal sources and ontology cache are unchanged. This draft awaits substantive review and CI on the updated commit; no readiness or merge is claimed.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/clingen-grxcr2-20261107","number":6183,"title":"Review GRXCR2 ClinGen Mendelian annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6183"},{"body":"Audit bacterial L-carnitine catabolism through 3-dehydrocarnitine and betainyl-CoA, with optional CaiX–CbcWV uptake. The module distinguishes standalone Pseudomonas CdhB from the Sinorhizobium BcoEF fusion and uses Pseudomonadota as an occurrence envelope. Eleven curated references, including five reviews, ground individual reactions and cross-species limits. Screening signals, clone-to-reference identity and the distinct anaerobic CJ25 comparator remain explicitly qualified.\n\nFourteen complete gene reviews preserve all 146 ordered source rows and all 11 inherited NEW proposals; five proposals are explicitly retired in place and no new annotations are proposed. Five family reviews preserve PAINT assertions while qualifying substrate-specific activity. Membership checks pass for 101 assertions. The independent capacity audit passes 49 cases, detects 14 module mutations and tests five evidence-gate sensitivities; it does not infer native flux from a generic activity match.\n\nValidation: focused gene, family, module, quotation, history and rendering checks pass. Full-corpus validation passed on science commit `ce303513876cd1cf236a0f2d9a637280025b008a`: 10,767 reviews, zero blocking errors, 54 pathway checks and 310 frozen inputs verified. Existing advisory warnings remain. Round-one follow-up corrects cargo-specific evidence across all 52 CbcV/CbcW rows, uses the supported exact proposed core function for PP_0303, and adds ten guards for that proposal bridge. Subsequent main integration preserves the validated science; its proof and the full-run receipt are under `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_l_carnitine_catabolism/round1/`. The project ledger also preserves independently verified completion of flavonoid PR #6140. Review, CI and actual merge remain pending.\n\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-carnitine-taxon-audit","number":6182,"title":"Audit cross-species bacterial L-carnitine catabolism","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6182"},{"body":"Review the existing human GRIA4 annotations, preserving all incoming contexts and declared products. The follow-up clarifies repository-cache versus external-original access, adds a cached evidence anchor, and keeps the previously reviewed biological dispositions.\n\nAll 55 original records, 53 stored contexts, four extension atoms and both official products are retained. No NEW annotations or source-cache edits are proposed. Gene, reference, status, genuine history and cache-order checks passed; HTML is current. The section helper added two exact locations and left two ambiguous matches unset. Reference validation checked three snippets and eleven titles, skipped five snippets and found no issues. Independent source-rendering checks verified all eight YAML excerpts. Computed status is COMPLETE with zero warnings; the PR remains draft for new-head review and CI.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/clingen-gria4-20261107","number":6181,"title":"Review human GRIA4 annotations with preserved source contexts","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6181"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nAdds a way to check whether an ARBA rule actually fires on a protein, and to check every ARBA row (`GO_REF:0000117`) of a cached GOA file against the rule it cites.\n\n- `ai-gene-review arba-check ACC RULE...` (`just arba-check`) reports, per rule, which condition set matches or, for the closest failing sets, which conditions fail.\n- `ai-gene-review arba-check-goa GOA_TSV` (`just arba-check-gene ORG GENE`) gives each ARBA row a status: `MATCH`, `RULE_EDITED_SINCE_LOAD`, `MISMATCH`, `RULE_NOT_FOUND` or `PROTEIN_NOT_FOUND`.\n- New module `src/ai_gene_review/etl/arba_match.py`. Conditions are evaluated with `curated_rules.condition_holds`, so ARBA and this project's curated rules share one evaluator.\n\n## Why the load-date guard\n\nARBA serves only the current version of a rule, and GOA can be built from rule data a release ahead of the public UniProt API. A corpus audit run against UniProt 2026_03 flagged 8.9% of 2026-07-27 GOA rows as citing a rule the protein does not satisfy. Rerun against 2026_04, the rate was 1.1%, and 329 of the 331 remaining mismatches cite a rule edited after the row was loaded (e.g. ARBA00086692 / Q88KS6, edited 2026-07-18). Any non-matching row whose rule's `modifiedDate` is later than the GOA row date is therefore reported as `RULE_EDITED_SINCE_LOAD`, and rules are always fetched fresh, never from cache.\n\nWrite-up of the audit: https://claude.ai/artifact/GknGNFzHVFyNDY5zWgDCS3\n\n## Tests\n\n- `tests/test_arba_match.py`: unit tests on the two real versions of ARBA00086692 against Q88KS6's features, covering failure kinds, negation, multi-value conditions, PANTHER family/subfamily matching and the load-status guard. Integration tests run the CLI and the UniProt client against VCR cassettes, including the case of a stale cached rule.\n- Each logic branch was mutation-checked: breaking it fails at least one test.\n- `just mypy` and ruff are clean. The full unit suite has 1 failure, `test_radical_sam_synthesis.py::test_committed_synthesis_is_current`: `projects/RADICAL_SAM/data/synthesis.yaml` is stale on `main`, and this PR does not touch it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-arba-pipeline","number":6180,"title":"Add ARBA rule-condition checker for proteins and GOA rows","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6180"},{"body":"Audit the bacterial Kdp potassium-homeostasis module across transport and regulatory implementations. The model now distinguishes KdpABC from KdpFABC and canonical KdpDE control from the supported Caulobacter KdpE case. Optional KdpD activities remain qualified by the member and assay that support them.\n\n- Curate 14 references, including four reviews, and attach findings to the relevant steps and taxonomic limits.\n- Complete 20 gene reviews across the comparative panel, preserving 274 ordered source rows and all eight inherited NEW proposals; no proposals added.\n- Audit five families with official membership evidence: 97 checks pass. Scope exact KdpD activity and accessory requirements without assuming universal transfer.\n- Exercise 32 capacity scenarios, three module mutations and four optional-activity profiles against the frozen evidence closure.\n\nValidation: `just validate-all` passed on `cc2868a722afc77209973de431b1a03281ddace9` over 10,762 reviews with zero blocking errors. Focused module, family, gene, history and render checks pass; the module retains eight documented advisories. Main `7eee074bca` is integrated with all 281 frozen files outside shared project metadata unchanged. This preservation proof does not claim a new full-corpus run after integration.\n\n[Full validation](analysis/module_audits/bacterial_kdp_potassium_homeostasis/full-validation.json) · [Focused checks](analysis/module_audits/bacterial_kdp_potassium_homeostasis/focused-validation.json) · [Integration proof](analysis/module_audits/bacterial_kdp_potassium_homeostasis/main-integration.json)\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-kdp-taxon-audit","number":6179,"title":"Audit bacterial Kdp transport and regulatory variants across species","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6179"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nRound 4 of `projects/INVOLVED_IN_PRECOMPOSITION.md` (rounds 1–3 were #5562, #5565 and #6008).\n\n- **GO:0098641 *cadherin binding involved in cell-cell adhesion* → its genus GO:0045296 *cadherin binding*** across CDH1, CDH2, CDH3, CDH11, DSG2, DSC2 and FAT4. Every one of these reviews already lists a cell-cell adhesion process in its core functions, so the context is carried there.\n  - **CDH2, CDH3, CDH11:** the IBA rows were already on the genus, and an earlier pass had MODIFYed them to GO:0098641. They are reversed to ACCEPT. Their `propagation_review` changes from TERM_SCOPING_PROBLEM/GRANULARITY_MISMATCH to NO_FAILURE_CORE, and the earlier reasoning is labelled superseded.\n  - **CDH1, DSG2 ×3, DSC2 ×2:** MODIFY replacements now target the genus. The row-specific summaries are kept.\n  - **FAT4:** the NEW row (FAT4–DCHS1, IDA) is now on the genus.\n  - **Core functions:** the MF in all seven reviews now uses the genus.\n  - **Other GOA rows on GO:0098641** (ANXA1, PPP1CA, BAIAP2, BAIAP2L1, EPCAM) were judged over-annotated, non-core or undecided. They aren't MODIFYs, so they are unchanged.\n- **Not a lateral target:** GO:0098632 *cell-cell adhesion mediator activity*, the other parent of GO:0098641, is itself defined as `protein binding and part_of cell-cell adhesion`. It is precomposed by definition even though its label doesn't say \"involved in\". This is recorded as a round-4 lesson.\n- **Follow-ups from the #6008 review:**\n  - the inventory leaves label-only rows out of `module_candidates.yaml`;\n  - the project page notes that GO:0061081 is the weakest class-E term;\n  - yeast ATG7's two GO:0061664 rows now quote the PMID:12965207 sentence that states the interaction.\n- **Project page:** a round-4 section and lessons, figures refreshed against `main` (1,451 rows, 733 genes; 314 authored uses, 56 label-only), and an updated plan.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `just validate-changed` on the eight changed reviews: 8 valid, 0 errors.\n- `just validate` warnings for CDH1, CDH2, CDH3, CDH11, DSG2, DSC2 and FAT4 are identical to `main`'s.\n- All new history records pass `just validate-history`.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `uv run pytest`: 5855 passed, 1 skipped\n- [x] Inventory doctests (21); `ruff check` on the inventory script\n- [x] Re-rendered the edited gene pages that `main` tracks and the project page\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01JvuYRbq5GHbM3BRmYUzAXB)_","headRefName":"claude/go-involved-in-precomp-eode9w","number":6178,"title":"Precomposed terms round 4: decompose cadherin binding","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6178"},{"body":"Reviews all 192 GRIA1 schema contexts while preserving 198 incoming records, nine extension atoms and six official alternative products. Current actions are 63 ACCEPT, 62 KEEP_AS_NON_CORE, 29 TOO_GENERAL, 14 UNDECIDED, 14 MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED, seven REMOVE and three MODIFY, with zero NEW assertions and two core functions.\n\nThe follow-up reconciles repeated-term decisions, declares the retained narrower-context exception in the review schema, adds cached evidence anchors and portable original-access records, and separates the independent PAINT proof from the incoming archive. All 114 source caches are preserved by this correction.\n\nThe five-path correction contains three authored files, regenerated HTML and a genuine EDIT history record. Affected gene/reference validation, strict GOA matching, computed status, history and rendering passed. The default validator reports two advisories: its rule-table checker treats the declaration as unused, while its legacy same-term checker retains the action warning. The review remains DRAFT. Reference validation checked five snippets, skipped none and found zero issues. Current-head review is approved and ordinary CI passed. The full suite was skipped by ordinary routing; merge-group tests remain required before integration.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/clingen-gria1-20261107","number":6177,"title":"Review human GRIA1 annotations with explicit source and product context","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6177"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nThe MMS19 gene review recorded the NAS row for `GO:0030674` protein-macromolecule adaptor activity (PMID:11279242) as MODIFY. Its `proposed_replacement_terms` was `GO:0097361` cytosolic [4Fe-4S] assembly targeting complex, a cellular-component term offered as the replacement for a molecular function. The reviewer bot flagged this on #6009.\n\nThe row's own reason argues that MMS19 *is* a protein adaptor. It bridges early-acting CIA components and Fe-S client apoproteins (PMID:22678362: \"MMS19 thus serves as an adapter between early-acting CIA components and a subset of cellular iron-sulfur proteins\"). What is outdated is the NAS paper's framing of MMS19 as an estrogen-receptor coactivator and direct TFIIH regulator, and the term names no partners. So:\n\n- **`genes/human/MMS19/MMS19-ai-review.yaml`:**\n  - The `GO:0030674` row is now ACCEPT with a rewritten reason, and the wrong-aspect replacement is removed. The title-only PMID:11279242 quote is replaced with the paper's ER-interaction sentence.\n  - Complex membership is unaffected. It stays where it already was: the five `GO:0097361` part_of rows and `core_functions.in_complex`.\n  - The Fe-S core function is refined from `GO:0060090` molecular adaptor activity to its child `GO:0030674`.\n  - The NEW `GO:0060090` row is removed, because the accepted `GO:0030674` row now entails it.\n  - The MMXD/chromosome-segregation core function no longer asserts a molecular function. Its only support is a knockdown phenotype, which shows no activity of MMS19 within MMXD. Its process, complex and locations are kept, and the description points to the existing suggested question on MMXD.\n- **`modules/cytosolic_iron_sulfur_assembly.yaml`:** MMS19's function moves from `GO:0060090` to `GO:0030674`, together with the role description and the gene-review evidence statement, so the module and the gene review stay in step.\n- History records for both, linked to this PR, and re-rendered pages.\n\n## Checks\n\n- `just validate human MMS19` passes with the same 10 warnings as before the edit. Eight concern existing protein-binding rows, and two are environment notices.\n- For the module:\n  - `linkml-validate -C ModuleReview` passes.\n  - `module_validator` passes with 0 warnings; MMS19's module function now matches its reviewed core function.\n- `just validate-history` passes for both records.\n- `git diff --check` is clean.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01HaxDvreJUYVzGhMtsFqLh6)_","headRefName":"claude/relaxed-rubin-d6qbkz","number":6176,"title":"MMS19: accept the GO:0030674 adaptor row instead of a cellular-component replacement","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6176"},{"body":"The existing Isc module treated component requirements too uniformly across bacteria. This audit defines the ferredoxin-supported, HscBA-assisted mechanism, distinguishes reduced anaerobic implementations and scaffold-suppressor bypasses, and keeps IscA as an optional carrier branch.\n\nThe module now mines comparative reviews and primary studies onto individual assembly/transfer steps, verifies family membership, and reviews 18 gene products across the E. coli, P. putida and Azotobacter evidence panels. It preserves ordered annotation source payloads and records the justified retirement of one inherited PSEPK hscA proposal. Capacity checks distinguish a complete compatible assembly from isolated family hits or mixed-species components.\n\nValidation: `just validate-all` passed on `daf2de4dc7d5d5c1cf790f159425007757be841a` (10,753 gene reviews, zero blocking errors; advisory counts are recorded in the proof). Focused module, family/member, gene, history and rendering checks passed, alongside 38 capacity cases. All 268 frozen inputs were verified. Subsequent main integration preserves the scientific files and has a separate scope/hash proof; it is not a new full-corpus run.\n\nFalcon authentication failed; literature research and source limitations are documented without an invented provider report. Independent review, CI and actual merge remain pending.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-isc-taxon-audit","number":6175,"title":"Audit bacterial Isc assembly scope, family realizers and gene evidence","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6175"},{"body":"Part of the organelle lifecycle project (`projects/ORGANELLE_LIFECYCLES.md`). This is a companion to #6171 (Golgi), #6172 (autophagosome) and #6173 (nucleus). All four edit the same plan line, so later merges will take a merge from main.\n\n## New modules\n- **`lipid_droplet_neutral_lipid_storage`** (`RULE_CANDIDATE`): diacylglycerol acylation is a `ONE_OR_MORE` `REALIZER` variant set.\n  - **DGAT1 variant:** PTHR10408:SF7, which excludes the SOAT/Are sterol acyltransferases.\n  - **DGAT2 variant:** PTHR12317 subfamilies SF14 (vertebrate), SF0 (yeast and Dictyostelium Dga1) and SF63 (Arabidopsis). This follows the PTHR12317 family review, which allows GO:0004144 at subfamily level only. MOGAT and AWAT are therefore excluded.\n  - **FIT proteins:** optional, because the family is opisthokont-only in the local tables.\n  - **Grounding:** both DGAT nodes are at Eukaryota, and each is shown to be an ancestor of the human protein. MFs come from the human DGAT1, DGAT2 and FITM2 reviews.\n  - **Not modelled:** seipin (BSCL2 has no local PANTHER entry), PDAT (no human member), sterol acyltransferases and perilipins.\n- **`lipid_droplet_lifecycle`** (umbrella): requires the DGAT module. It references `triacylglycerol_biosynthesis`, `seed_triacylglycerol_biosynthesis` and `triacylglycerol_lipolysis` as optional.\n  - I did not use `triacylglycerol_biosynthesis` as the required step. Read as a rule, it puts DGAT1 and DGAT2 in one variant and fails the oracle even for human. That module is outside this PR's scope, so I left it unchanged.\n\n## Supporting changes\nThe coverage row and plan are updated, and history records are added.\n\n## Checks\n- `linkml-validate` (ModuleReview, HistoryRecord): no issues.\n- `module_validator`: passes with 0 warnings, after refreshing the member index.\n- Family-presence oracle (reviewed entries only):\n  - satisfied in budding yeast, fission yeast, Arabidopsis, Dictyostelium and human;\n  - Plasmodium and Trypanosoma gaps reflect how few of their proteins are reviewed.\n- Module, render-projects and standalone-demo tests: 556 passed.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01G5VmYMsJjuCX8uJG6AQr9J)_","headRefName":"claude/lipid-droplet-lifecycle","number":6174,"title":"Lipid droplet lifecycle umbrella and eukaryote-wide DGAT rule module","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6174"},{"body":"Part of the organelle lifecycle project (`projects/ORGANELLE_LIFECYCLES.md`). This is a companion to #6171 (Golgi) and #6172 (autophagosome). All three edit the same plan line, so later merges will take a merge from main.\n\n## New modules\n- **`nuclear_pore_transport_core`** (`RULE_CANDIDATE`, explicitly **partial**):\n  - **Pore scaffold:** Nup98-Nup96 (PTHR23198:SF6), Nup88/Nup82 (PTHR13257:SF0), Seh1 (PTHR11024:SF3) and Sec13 (PTHR11024:SF2).\n  - **Ran gradient:** Ran (PTHR24071) and NTF2 (PTHR12612).\n  - **Exportins:** Crm1 (PTHR11223:SF2) and Cse1 (PTHR10997:SF8).\n  - **Grounding:** every PAINT node is shown to be an ancestor of the human protein. The NTF2 and Nup88 nodes are at Eukaryota; the others carry no taxon, and their seeds are listed on each node.\n  - **Functions:** from the NUP98 review and from the exportins' F IBD. Ran, NTF2, Nup88, Seh1 and Sec13 are untyped.\n  - **Why partial:** importin beta and alpha, RCC1, RanGAP, RanBP1 and most nucleoporins have no local PANTHER entry for the human protein. A genome that passes this rule has not been shown to have a complete pore; the notes say so.\n  - **Selector caveats on the families:**\n    - Seh1 and Sec13 also work in SEA/GATOR2 and COPII.\n    - The NTF2 family includes NXT1/NXT2.\n    - Ran is grounded on the family, because the SF0 subfamily lists no Arabidopsis member.\n    - No Dictyostelium Nup88 or Seh1 is listed, and no Arabidopsis Sec13.\n- **`nucleus_lifecycle`** (umbrella): requires the core module. It references `dmel_nuclear_pore_complex` and `mrna_nuclear_export` as optional. Pore insertion, envelope sealing (CHMP7/LEM2), mitotic disassembly and the lamina are listed as not yet modelled.\n\n## Supporting changes\nThe coverage row and plan are updated, and history records are added.\n\n## Checks\n- `linkml-validate` (ModuleReview, HistoryRecord): no issues.\n- `module_validator`: passes, after refreshing the member index. Two warnings remain: neither exportin MF has been adjudicated by a family review.\n- Family-presence oracle (reviewed entries only):\n  - satisfied in budding yeast, fission yeast and human;\n  - Arabidopsis has a gap only at Sec13 and Dictyostelium at Nup88 and Seh1, all as stated on the families;\n  - Plasmodium and Trypanosoma gaps reflect how few of their proteins are reviewed.\n- Module, render-projects and standalone-demo tests: 556 passed.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01G5VmYMsJjuCX8uJG6AQr9J)_","headRefName":"claude/nucleus-lifecycle","number":6173,"title":"Nucleus lifecycle umbrella and partial eukaryote-wide pore transport module","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6173"},{"body":"Part of the organelle lifecycle project (`projects/ORGANELLE_LIFECYCLES.md`). This is the companion to the Golgi PR #6171. Both edit the same plan line, so whichever merges second will take a merge from main.\n\n## New modules\n- **`autophagosome_core_biogenesis`** (`RULE_CANDIDATE`): eukaryote-wide rule for the core Atg machinery.\n  - **Initiation:** Atg1/ULK kinase (PTHR24348) with Atg13 (PTHR13430:SF4).\n  - **PI3-kinase complex I:** Vps34 (PTHR10048:SF7), Vps15, Atg6/Beclin. Atg14 is optional: its family is opisthokont-only in the local tables, and its node sits at taxon:33154.\n  - **Membrane supply:** Atg9, Atg2, Atg18/WIPI.\n  - **Conjugation:** Atg7 (PTHR10953:SF3), Atg12, Atg5, Atg3 (PTHR12866:SF2), Atg4 and Atg8. Atg16 is optional because PTHR19878 lists no yeast Atg16.\n  - **Grounding:** every PAINT node is shown to be an ancestor of the human protein. Ten steps have a node at Eukaryota. The Atg18/WIPI node has only animal and yeast seeds, which the notes say.\n  - **Functions:** molecular functions come from the human gene reviews; Vps15 is recorded as contributing to the Vps34 kinase activity. Atg4 has no review and no PAINT table, so it is untyped.\n  - **Caveats on the families:**\n    - The Atg1/ULK selector over-matches: it includes Ksp1, Sks1 and ULK3.\n    - The PROPPIN selector over-matches: it includes Atg21, Hsv2 and WDR45.\n    - No Dictyostelium Atg13 or Atg2 is listed.\n  - **Not modelled:** FIP200/Atg17, ATG101 and Atg10. Their human proteins have no local PANTHER entry.\n- **`autophagosome_lifecycle`** (umbrella): requires the core module. It references `metazoan_autophagosome_biogenesis` and `phagophore_assembly_site` as optional, lineage-specific descriptions. Closure and fusion are listed as not yet modelled.\n\n## Supporting changes\n- The coverage row and plan are updated, and history records are added.\n- Generator detail: GO labels for ids missing from `rules/_labels.json` were read from the local GO database. All five checked against the labels used in the existing human reviews.\n\n## Checks\n- `linkml-validate` (ModuleReview, HistoryRecord): no issues.\n- `module_validator`: passes, after refreshing the member index. Five warnings remain, all intended:\n  - subfamily-grounding suggestions for the ULK, PROPPIN and Atg8 families, explained on each family;\n  - a cellular-component mismatch on the Vps15 node, whose IBD terms are endosomal and PI3K-complex terms;\n  - Vps15 is CONTRIBUTES_ONLY.\n- Family-presence oracle (reviewed entries only):\n  - satisfied in budding yeast, fission yeast, Arabidopsis and human;\n  - Dictyostelium gaps only at Atg13 and Atg2, both as stated on the families;\n  - Plasmodium and Trypanosoma gaps reflect how few of their proteins are reviewed.\n- Module, render-projects and standalone-demo tests: 556 passed.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01G5VmYMsJjuCX8uJG6AQr9J)_","headRefName":"claude/autophagosome-lifecycle","number":6172,"title":"Autophagosome lifecycle umbrella and eukaryote-wide core autophagy module","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6172"},{"body":"Part of the organelle lifecycle project (`projects/ORGANELLE_LIFECYCLES.md`), following the mitochondrion, peroxisome, lysosome and ER PRs.\n\n## New modules\n- **`golgi_copi_vesicle_coat`** (`RULE_CANDIDATE`): eukaryote-wide rule for Arf1-dependent COPI coat assembly.\n  - Arf1 activation: GBF1/Gea (subfamily PTHR10663:SF388) and Arf1 (PTHR11711).\n  - Coatomer: alpha (PTHR19876:SF1), beta' (PTHR19876:SF2), epsilon, beta, gamma, delta and zeta, one PANTHER family or subfamily each.\n  - Every selector has a human representative. Each PAINT node is shown to be an ancestor of the human protein, through its IBA rows or its IBD seeds.\n  - Molecular functions come from the human reviews where they exist (coatomer gamma, delta). GBF1's comes from the node's F IBD. Arf1 is typed as GTP binding from the F IBD, because the Arf family review does not grant GTPase activity family-wide. The other subunits are untyped.\n  - Selector caveats are stated on each family:\n    - the Arf selector over-matches the whole Arf/Arl family;\n    - the GBF1 subfamily has no Dictyostelium member;\n    - the beta' subfamily has no Arabidopsis member;\n    - the zeta family includes Dictyostelium TstD (the TSET complex).\n  - The notes say that the alpha/beta' and zeta nodes have only animal and budding-yeast seeds, so their eukaryote-wide reach rests on the family entries tables.\n- **`golgi_apparatus_lifecycle`**: umbrella module.\n  - `golgi_copi_vesicle_coat` is required.\n  - `dmel_copii_coat` is referenced as optional. A eukaryote-wide COPII rule cannot be grounded locally yet: SEC24A, SEC31A and PREB have no local PANTHER entry, and the SAR1 and SEC23 families have no PAINT table.\n\n## Project updates\n- `coverage.yaml`: the Golgi row is filled in.\n- Plan: the Golgi item is ticked and a COPII/ArfGAP/tether follow-up is added.\n- History records for both modules and the project.\n\n## Checks\n- `linkml-validate` (ModuleReview, HistoryRecord): no issues.\n- `module_validator` (member index refreshed): passes. Two warnings remain, both on Arf1:\n  - Arf1 could be grounded on subfamily SF357, but its lineage spread is not recorded locally, so it stays on the family.\n  - Arf1 GTP binding is not in the review's core_functions.\n- Family-presence oracle (reviewed entries only):\n  - satisfied in budding yeast, fission yeast and human;\n  - Arabidopsis has a gap only at beta' and Dictyostelium only at GBF1, both as stated on the families;\n  - Plasmodium and Trypanosoma gaps reflect how few of their proteins are reviewed.\n- Module, render-projects and standalone-demo tests: 556 passed.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01G5VmYMsJjuCX8uJG6AQr9J)_","headRefName":"claude/golgi-lifecycle","number":6171,"title":"Golgi apparatus lifecycle umbrella and eukaryote-wide COPI coat module","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6171"},{"body":"The ImuABC module now distinguishes shared damage-induced mutagenesis roles from organism-specific biochemistry. It covers three matched cassettes (Pseudomonas, Caulobacter and mycobacteria), with a separately qualified Myxococcus ImuA exemplar; measured ATPase activity is not transferred to every ImuA family member or treated as a demonstrated requirement for every bypass event.\n\n- Curates two narrative reviews and primary evidence across 15 assessed references, with evidence on individual roles and connections.\n- Reviews 12 gene exemplars, preserving all 80 original annotation payloads and three newly seeded Myxococcus rows; adds one experimentally supported ATP hydrolysis annotation. Separates protein origin, assay host and functional organism.\n- Checks family membership and revises unsupported universal family claims. Records the Myxococcus sequence-identity evidence and the remaining inferred link to the 2024 construct.\n- Exercises 63 capacity cases with explicit same-panel compatibility and evidence qualifications. Satisfaction is not presented as proof of native flux or cross-species interchangeability.\n\nValidation: `just validate-all` passed on science commit `9d7cbd20fe52fa4c3be3a3d1563baff493539a67` (10,748 reviews, zero blocking errors; advisories retained), after focused gene/module/family/member/history/render checks and Ruff. All 212 frozen inputs were verified. Subsequent integration of main `e9d50a8a42ab5e6b5560d09ff8c44e64f4c77360` preserves the scientific inputs; the committed integration proof records its scope separately from the full run.\n\nEvidence and reproducible checks: `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_imuabc_damage_induced_mutagenesis/`. Falcon retrieval returned HTTP 403; no provider report was fabricated. Independent review, CI and merge remain pending.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-imuabc-taxon-audit","number":6169,"title":"Audit ImuABC damage-induced mutagenesis across bacterial lineages","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6169"},{"body":"Review all 45 GOA rows for MVP (Q14764). Generic protein-binding rows are\nremoved; the protein activation cascade IEA is removed with a source\ndefect on the mouse IMP it is projected from; Reactome neutrophil granule\nlocations, protein transport (NAS) and cell population proliferation are\nmarked over-annotated; signalling and minor locations are kept as\nnon-core. Adds structural molecule activity as the core function,\nproposes a 'vault complex' CC term, and caches 20 supporting papers.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01VjkEiscPu6LvMyrdRnhBEr","headRefName":"claude/youthful-pascal-2h9oe5","number":6168,"title":"Add review of human MVP (major vault protein)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6168"},{"body":"The module now distinguishes the Bacillus Ac-HepG lipoprotein-acetyl carrier pathway from comparative PcrB enzyme capacity. The native C35 pathway is kept separate from the C20 analogue used in reconstitution and from archaeal membrane-lipid synthesis.\n\n- Curates three reviews and the linked correction, adding source-scoped findings and step evidence. Narrows the demonstrated pathway scope while retaining verified comparative proteins.\n- Reviews seven genes across Bacillus, Geobacillus, Staphylococcus and a Flavobacterium C20 comparator. Preserves 44 original annotation payloads, including two historical NEW rows, and adds one supported LhaT molecular-function annotation.\n- Verifies family/member assignments without inventing an identifier for the unknown native phosphatase. EgsA-to-PcrB flux, membrane crossing, donor regioisomer preference and complete pathways outside Bacillus remain unresolved.\n\nValidation: focused gene/module/family-member/history/render checks and repository Ruff pass. `just validate-all` passes on `5ca4d26e74a5455b6122ecb0c700a920c635ca4e`: 10,752 reviews, zero blocking errors; the full-run proof records all advisory counts. Main integration preserves all 101 frozen nonshared files byte-for-byte (105 total frozen inputs). This preservation check is separate from the full-corpus run. Independent review, CI and actual merge remain pending.\n\nEvidence and validation receipts: `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_heptaprenylglyceryl_ether_lipid/`.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-hepg-taxon-audit","number":6167,"title":"Audit Ac-HepG carrier pathway scope and comparative enzyme evidence","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6167"},{"body":"The module previously lacked a reviewed comparison of folding cages and CnoX support across bacterial lineages. This audit defines a required GroEL/GroES cage with independently qualified active units and keeps the E. coli and Caulobacter CnoX branches optional and mechanistically distinct.\n\n- Complete twelve gene reviews across E. coli, P. putida, B. subtilis, M. tuberculosis and Caulobacter; preserve 201 original annotation payloads, two inherited NEW rows and 81 source files without adding annotations.\n- Assess twelve module references, including three reviews, and mine sixteen findings into the module. Verify family membership and distinguish cognate pairs, an experimentally supported heterologous pair and untested combinations.\n- Preserve the holdase ontology gap, M. tuberculosis noncanonical ATP-independent activity and limits on native client transfer. No universal CnoX requirement or arbitrary pair compatibility is inferred.\n\nValidation: full `just validate-all` passes on science commit6332a1d51e0e1488c73d594a62e1cfb2aeb9f458 (10,742 reviews; zero blocking errors; 220 frozen inputs). Focused gene/module/history/render checks pass; 24 source-scoped coverage cases pass. Seven module advisories remain documented. Main integration, equivalent checker-format fixes and the full run are separately anchored in the audit proofs; repository-wide Ruff and affected checker reruns pass.\n\nThe project ledger also preserves the independently verified merge of cytochrome-c PR #6121. This GroEL audit remains open until independent review, CI and actual merge.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-groel-audit-recovery","number":6164,"title":"Audit GroEL/GroES folding cages and CnoX support across bacteria","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6164"},{"body":"The glycerol module now distinguishes GlpF-mediated uptake, GlpK phosphorylation and the single-protein GlpD route across E. coli, P. putida and B. subtilis. Family membership is qualified by activity and compatible cellular context; oxygen-dependent oxidases and multisubunit GlpABC are not mistaken for GlpD. Eleven assessed references, including three reviews and a correction, provide 24 findings and step-specific evidence. Bacillus membrane assays are not presented as proof of quinone specificity, and E. coli GlpD is not restricted categorically to aerobic conditions.\n\nNine COMPLETE member reviews preserve all 116 original annotation payloads, including one inherited NEW row; no annotation rows were added. Histories, source provenance, family assessments and 29 coverage cases are included. This PR also records the verified ethanolamine #5970 merge and preserves that completion during main integration.\n\nValidation: all nine focused gene checks, module/family checks, histories and renders pass. `just validate-all` passes on science head `744ba398e08d8e9a417335dcfa9c07b395934d70`: 10,718 reviews, zero blocking errors, 54 pathway checks and all 155 frozen inputs verified. Corpus advisories remain explicitly recorded (115 term-label, 53 reference, 27,614 best-practice warnings). The heterogeneous GlpD/GlpA/GlpO family boundary remains an explicit module advisory. Main integration preserves the validated scientific inputs; independent review and CI gate the resulting PR head.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-glycerol-taxon-audit","number":6162,"title":"Audit bacterial glycerol uptake and GlpD catabolism across species","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6162"},{"body":"The glutamate-to-P5C module now represents a bacterial two-enzyme route with evidence for each step and explicit limits on strain identity, feedback regulation and physical channeling. Nine completed member reviews cover E. coli, Pseudomonas, Bacillus and Corynebacterium across three phyla. ProC-mediated proline formation remains outside this module.\n\n- Curate seven references, including two comparative reviews, with 14 mined findings. Distinguish Bacillus ProB/ProJ functions and the tested strains from reference-proteome exemplars. Do not require an obligatory ProB–ProA channeling complex.\n- Ground both steps in verified family descriptors and representatives. Correct the PTHR43654 subfamily scope and qualify ProB RNA binding from the actual assays; retain two bounded yeast comparator corrections without claiming a complete new fission-yeast review.\n- Preserve all 123 ordered annotation payloads and 39 protected source files. Add no annotation rows. Include append-only gene, family, module and project histories.\n\nValidation: all focused member, module, family, history and rendering checks pass, along with 33 source-based coverage cases. `just validate-all` passes on science head `e072e9ee30ea2c16016a38d99f59d4b064decc7a`: 10,719 reviews, zero blocking errors, 54 pathway checks and 137 frozen inputs verified. Existing advisories remain (115 ontology/label, 53 reference and 27,775 best-practice warnings); these are not a warning-free result. See `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_glutamate_to_p5c_biosynthesis/full-validation.json` and the separate main-integration proof for the exact scope.\n\nThe coverage cases test curated reaction/architecture requirements, including same-system and bacterial scope gates; they do not establish native flux or unrestricted interchangeability. Independent review, CI and actual merge remain pending.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-glutamate-p5c-audit-recovery","number":6160,"title":"Audit bacterial glutamate-to-P5C biosynthesis across three phyla","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6160"},{"body":"The gallate module distinguishes two chemically different routes from ring-opened 4-oxalomesaconate to CHA: GalD/GalB uses the enol intermediate, while LigU/LigJ uses KCH. Mixing either isomerase with the other route's hydratase does not count as a complete mechanism. Gallate entry, terminal aldol cleavage and optional GalP uptake are grounded separately.\n\nEleven curated references, including three comparative reviews, provide per-step evidence qualified against primary enzymology. The module is scoped to Pseudomonadota; downstream component evidence from Arthrobacter does not establish complete gallate entry. Verified families and twelve completed gene reviews span Pseudomonas, sphingomonads and Burkholderia. Burkholderia's supported entry/aldolase activities remain distinct from its unresolved intermediate-specific hydratase assignment.\n\nGalD, LigU and LigJ retain supported molecular-function genera while substrate-specific child requests remain separate. GalD's replacement and source-defect report now agree on GO:0016862. The redundant CHA proposal is preserved in an exact structured archive. GalC ancestry is checked against official definitions and ancestor snapshots. Burkholderia activity support no longer cites whole-strain growth as direct enzymology. LigJ gallate participation combines shared chemistry, gallate entry and lower-pathway genetics; PMID:11092855 mutant growth was tested with vanillate and syringate, not directly with gallate.\n\nAll 60 ordered original/seed annotation payloads and actions are preserved, including nine inherited NEW rows; no rows were added. Raw records, publication caches and provider reports are preserved. Eight changed gene reviews pass focused validation, histories and renders. Module validation passes with zero warnings. Twenty-four production-circuit cases cover alternative chemistry, mismatched pairs, partial panels and family-only false positives. The mixed-strain sphingomonad positive is synthetic; Pseudomonas catalytic coverage does not claim parental KT2440 growth without repaired GalT. These checks do not establish native flux or genomic absence.\n\nFull validation passes on `40aa36042295b4d714e07d9f115a23e2c1f6c134`: 10,737 reviews, zero blocking errors, 115 ontology-label advisories, 53 reference advisories, 27,620 best-practice warnings and 54 pathway-reference checks. All 177 frozen inputs were verified. Main integration preserves the scientific inputs; `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_gallate_catabolism/round1-full-validation.json` and `round1-main-integration.json` record the separate validation and integration scopes. Fresh review, CI and actual merge remain pending.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-gallate-taxon-audit","number":6158,"title":"Audit gallate catabolism across alternative bacterial mechanisms","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6158"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- Audit and harmonize **8 IBA rows (plus 2 ISS/IEA refinements) across 5 genes** (`ANOGA/DNAAF1`, `human/ASAH2`, `human/PARD3B`, `worm/pdr-1`, `human/ABHD2`) identified by the donor-support topology regime analysis (`R2` multi-paralog single-taxon, `R3` multi-species single-clade jump, and `R4 + CROSS_SF` cross-subfamily transfers; companion PR #6155):\n  1. **`ANOGA/DNAAF1`** (`GO:0007368 determination of left/right symmetry`, `IBA` on `PTN002915666` from `Danio rerio|Homo sapiens`): `KEEP_AS_NON_CORE` $\\to$ `REMOVE` (`PROPAGATION_BAD`, `LINEAGE_OR_TAXON_MISMATCH`) because Diptera lack nodal motile cilia and establish left–right asymmetry via non-ciliary actin/MyoID mechanisms. Also refined `GO:0060271` (`cilium assembly`, `ISS`) and `GO:0005929` (`cilium`, `IEA`) to `MODIFY` toward the more specific annotated terms `GO:0035082` (`axoneme assembly`) and `GO:0005930` (`axoneme`), synced `supporting_entities` on all 4 IBA rows from `DNAAF1-goa.tsv`, and added `findings`/`reference_review` on `file:ANOGA/DNAAF1/DNAAF1-deep-research-bioreason-sft.md` plus a `DR:InterPro` quote from `file:ANOGA/DNAAF1/DNAAF1-uniprot.txt`.\n  2. **`human/ASAH2`** (`GO:0042759 long-chain fatty acid biosynthetic process`, `IBA` on `PTN004377481` from bacterial ceramidases `UniProtKB:O06769|UniProtKB:Q9I596`): `ACCEPT` $\\to$ `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` (`TERM_SCOPING_PROBLEM`, `ROLE_CONFLATION`), harmonizing with vertebrate ortholog `DANRE/asah2` on the same node: whereas ASAH2 directly performs the sphingosine-forming step of the non-lysosomal salvage pathway (`GO:0046512`, `ACCEPT`), the released fatty acyl chain is a hydrolytic leaving group of ceramide catabolism rather than the output of long-chain fatty acid biosynthesis. Synced `supporting_entities` on all 5 IBA rows from `ASAH2-goa.tsv`.\n  3. **`human/PARD3B`** (`GO:0000226 microtubule cytoskeleton organization` and `GO:0007155 cell adhesion`, `IBA` on `PTN001046835`): `KEEP_AS_NON_CORE` $\\to$ `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` (`PROPAGATION_BAD`, `FUNCTIONAL_DIVERGENCE`), harmonizing with mammalian ortholog `HORSE/PARD3B` because vertebrate `PARD3B` (`PTHR16484:SF4`) diverged from canonical `PARD3` (`PTHR16484:SF10`) by losing the FXR/KR motifs and is incapable of binding to any isotypes of PAR6 or aPKC (`PMID:12459187`, `PMID:33565714`).\n  4. **`worm/pdr-1`** (`GO:0005783 endoplasmic reticulum` and `GO:0005794 Golgi apparatus`, `IBA` on `PTN000188399` from mammalian `PRKN` `RGD:61797|UniProtKB:O60260`): `UNDECIDED` $\\to$ `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` (`PROPAGATION_BAD`, `COMPARTMENT_OR_COMPLEX_MISMATCH`), harmonizing with invertebrate ortholog `DROME/park` and complying with the root organelle policy. Synced `supporting_entities` on all 10 IBA rows from `pdr-1-goa.tsv`.\n  5. **`human/ABHD2`** (`GO:0051792 medium-chain fatty acid biosynthetic process` from *S. cerevisiae* `EHT1|EEB1|YMR210W` `SGD:S000000381|SGD:S000004823|SGD:S000006016` and `GO:0051793 medium-chain fatty acid catabolic process` from `SGD:S000000381|SGD:S000006016` on `PTN000082647`): `KEEP_AS_NON_CORE` $\\to$ `REMOVE` on `GO:0051792` and `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` on `GO:0051793` (`PROPAGATION_BAD`, `FUNCTIONAL_DIVERGENCE`) because human `ABHD2` (`PTHR10794:SF45`) is a progesterone-activated plasma-membrane 2-arachidonoylglycerol and triacylglycerol hydrolase with no fatty-acid synthetic activity (`REMOVE` on `GO:0051792`) and only weak *in vitro* $p$-nitrophenyl butyrate/acetate esterase activity (`MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` on `GO:0051793`). Synced `supporting_entities` on all 9 IBA rows from `ABHD2-goa.tsv`.\n- Add 5 validated curation session history records under `history/genes/`.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `uv run ai-gene-review validate --verbose genes/ANOGA/DNAAF1/DNAAF1-ai-review.yaml genes/human/ASAH2/ASAH2-ai-review.yaml genes/human/PARD3B/PARD3B-ai-review.yaml genes/worm/pdr-1/pdr-1-ai-review.yaml genes/human/ABHD2/ABHD2-ai-review.yaml` (5 valid, 0 errors, 0 warnings)\n- [x] `just validate-history` on all 5 history records\n","headRefName":"iba-single-clade-curation","number":6157,"title":"Audit and harmonize 8 high-distance IBA rows (plus 2 ISS/IEA refinements) across 5 genes","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6157"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- Extend `projects/IBA_REVIEW/single_organism_distance.py` with `compute_mrca_lineage()`, `classify_donor_regime()`, `canonical_seed_gene()`, `--regimes`, and `--all-regimes` to analyze **donor-set MRCA depth ($d_{\\text{MRCA}}$)** vs **closest donor–target LCA depth ($d_{\\text{LCA}}$)** across all 9,410 non-self resolved GOA `IBA` rows (9,090 rated with known donor lineages, plus 156 in Tier 0):\n  1. **R1: Single donor gene (`1 taxon, 1 distinct seed`, $n = 3{,}139$)**: **12.7% Fail**, **4.7% `REMOVE`** (7.6% Refine).\n  2. **R2: Multi-paralog single taxon (`1 taxon, >= 2 distinct seeds`, $n = 744$)**: **12.5% Fail**, **4.7% `REMOVE`** (7.0% Refine) — confirming that multiple co-annotated paralogs in a single donor organism do not reduce over-propagation risk overall, and reverse above `R1` in Tiers 3–4 (**19.2% Fail, 6.9% `REMOVE`**, $n = 261$) and small-$n$ Tier 6 plant-to-fungi/animal jumps (**48.0% Fail, 24.0% `REMOVE`**, $n = 25$).\n  3. **R3: Multi-species single-clade jump (`>= 2 taxa`, $d_{\\text{MRCA}} > d_{\\text{LCA}}$, $n = 1{,}262$)**: **11.2% Fail**, **4.0% `REMOVE`** (7.0% Refine) — demonstrating that multi-species IBAs whose donors all coalesce inside a single donor clade before jumping to the target (e.g., `mouse` + `rat` $\\to$ `worm`/`yeast`, `worm` + `fly` $\\to$ `human`, or two bacteria $\\to$ `human`) fail at nearly twice the rate of phylogenetically bracketed transfers (**12.0% vs 5.4% Fail** in Tiers 3–4; **15.2% vs 7.9% Fail** in Tiers 5–7).\n  4. **R4: Phylogenetically bracketed multi-species (`>= 2 taxa`, $d_{\\text{MRCA}} \\le d_{\\text{LCA}}$, $n = 3{,}945$)**: **5.8% Fail**, **2.1% `REMOVE`** (7.6% Refine) overall, splitting into **3.0% Fail (`1.4% REMOVE`, $n = 1{,}353$; strict all-donor match: 3.1% Fail, `1.4% REMOVE`, $n = 795$)** for `R4 + SAME_SF` vs **9.9% Fail (`3.7% REMOVE`, $n = 1{,}182$)** for `R4 + CROSS_SF` when the target and resolved donors sit in different PANTHER subfamilies.\n- Update `projects/IBA_REVIEW/single-organism-distance.md` and `projects/IBA_REVIEW/HISTORY.md` with current `origin/main` counts (post-#5857 and #5861), Metric 5 tables (Tables 5A–5D), methodological diagnostics (Section 5E), and 5 candidate genes for re-review in companion PR #6157 (`ANOGA/DNAAF1`, `human/ASAH2`, `human/PARD3B`, `worm/pdr-1`, `human/ABHD2`).\n- Add curation session history record `history/projects/IBA_REVIEW/2026-11-07T004305Z-claude-code-9cccf8.yaml`.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `uv run pytest --doctest-modules projects/IBA_REVIEW/single_organism_distance.py` (5 passed)\n- [x] `uv run python projects/IBA_REVIEW/single_organism_distance.py --summary`\n- [x] `just validate-history history/projects/IBA_REVIEW/2026-11-07T004305Z-claude-code-9cccf8.yaml`\n- [x] `uv run pytest tests/test_project_frontmatter.py`\n","headRefName":"iba-single-clade-distance","number":6155,"title":"Extend IBA phylogenetic distance analysis to 4 donor-support topology regimes (d_MRCA vs d_LCA)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6155"},{"body":"## Summary\n- re-review SCHPO/mon1 against the current PTHR13027 PAINT rows\n- record that PTN008545077 now blocks the broad GO:0035658 Mon1-Ccz1 complex IBA on the Schizosaccharomyces branch while the PomBase ISO row remains accepted\n- add a 2026-11-06 no-direct-SCHPO-mon1 literature search note and mark SCHPO/mon1 complete in FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just paint-losses SCHPO mon1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO mon1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/mon1/2026-11-06T234546Z-codex-590725.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T234546Z-codex-e6df54.yaml","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-mon1-pthr13027-20261106","number":6153,"title":"Record SCHPO mon1 PTHR13027 IRD review","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6153"},{"body":"## Summary\n- re-review EMENI/mon1 against the PTHR13027 fungal/eukaryotic/Opisthokonta PAINT nodes\n- add a 2026-11-06 PubMed/web search note; no newer direct A. nidulans MonA or Aspergillus Ccz1/Rab7 paper was found\n- mark EMENI/mon1 complete in projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES with history records and regenerated HTML\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate EMENI mon1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/EMENI/mon1/2026-11-06T233535Z-codex-72dd79.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T233535Z-codex-c1767f.yaml","headRefName":"codex/EMENI-mon1-pthr13027-20261106","number":6152,"title":"Mark EMENI mon1 PTHR13027 reviewed","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6152"},{"body":"The fused PutA module now recognizes both catalytic domains across bacterial architectures without treating a broad family match as proof of the pathway. Eleven completed bacterial reviews cover eight PutA proteins and three PutP transporters; the yeast PUT2 comparator was also corrected. Sixteen curated references, including two comparative reviews, provide 31 findings supporting the three steps and their cross-species limits.\n\nBoth catalytic steps use the verified bifunctional PutA family (`InterPro:IPR025703`) with distinct activities and an explicit same-protein requirement. PANTHER assessments distinguish fused PutA from monofunctional RocA/PUT2 relatives and proline uptake from other sodium-solute transporters. Component exemplars span Pseudomonadota, Thermodesulfobacteriota, Campylobacterota and Actinomycetota. Structural constructs, experimental strains and native whole-pathway capacity are distinguished explicitly.\n\nThe review follow-up consistently removes the erroneous electronic transcription mapping from structurally characterized non-RHH PutAs. PSEPK PutA's unsupported membrane-face assignment is UNDECIDED. Yeast PUT2 retains the supported glutamate-formation process, and target versus comparative evidence is qualified. The exact ledger records 16 action reversals relative to the original bacterial reviews. All 221 ordered annotation payloads across the bacterial panel and yeast comparator, 84 protected sources and the broader 67,771-file source snapshot are preserved. No rows were added or deleted; the one inherited NEW remains historical.\n\nSix changed-gene validations, renders and histories pass. Family attribution preserves the earlier reviewer. The checker passes 44 curation-coverage cases, six mechanism mutations and ten extraction checks. It now includes retained non-core activity, refinements and core synthesis, with explicit provenance. These checks establish consistency of reviewed claims and wiring; they do not independently re-evaluate each assay or prove native flux. Same-protein and same-strain constraints remain explicit external gates.\n\nFresh full validation passed on `6cd2f11830bdc7fc12b608f757934c1ba75d028f`: 10,720 reviews, zero blocking errors, 120 term-label advisories, 53 reference advisories and 27,775 best-practice warnings. All 54 pathway-reference checks passed and all 226 frozen inputs were verified unchanged. See `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_fused_puta_proline_catabolism/round1-full-validation.json` and `round1-review-response.md`.\n\nPublished main was integrated afterward with frozen scientific inputs unchanged. The tracker retains the independently reverified beta-oxidation merge and seven additional module identities, keeping the inventory at 784. The integration proof distinguishes this preserved-input merge from the full-run head. Independent review, fresh CI and actual merge remain pending.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-puta-taxon-audit","number":6150,"title":"Audit fused PutA proline catabolism across bacterial architectures","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6150"},{"body":"## Summary\n- rechecks ASPFU/mon1 against the PTHR13027 PAINT rows after the yeast/Candida/Neurospora refreshes\n- keeps the fungal, eukaryotic, and Opisthokonta IBA decisions unchanged\n- normalizes one PMID:12364329 support quote to the exact cached text\n- records the 2026 search for direct Q4WHL1/AFUA_2G05010 literature and marks ASPFU/mon1 complete in the fungal PAINT family ledger\n- adds append-only gene/project history and renders the previously missing ASPFU mon1 HTML review\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate ASPFU mon1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/mon1/2026-11-06T231033Z-codex-b03e96.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T231033Z-codex-740aaf.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render ASPFU mon1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 uv run ai-gene-review render-projects projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES.md --verbose`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `git diff --check`","headRefName":"codex/ASPFU-mon1-pthr13027-20261106","number":6149,"title":"Refresh ASPFU mon1 PTHR13027 review","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6149"},{"body":"## Summary\n- rechecks NEUCR/apg-13 against PTHR13027 after the yeast and Candida MON1 refreshes\n- keeps the existing accepted fungal, eukaryotic, and Opisthokonta IBA decisions unchanged\n- records the 2026 search for direct Q870Q4/NCU09220 literature in apg-13 notes\n- marks NEUCR/apg-13 complete in the fungal PAINT family ledger and adds append-only gene/project history\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate NEUCR apg-13`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/NEUCR/apg-13/2026-11-06T230735Z-codex-57bbb7.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T230735Z-codex-e2d721.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render NEUCR apg-13`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 uv run ai-gene-review render-projects projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES.md --verbose`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `git diff --check`","headRefName":"codex/NEUCR-apg-13-pthr13027-20261106","number":6148,"title":"Refresh NEUCR apg-13 PTHR13027 review","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6148"},{"body":"## Summary\n- rechecks CANAL/MON1 against the PTHR13027 PAINT node placements after the yeast/MON1 refresh\n- keeps the four MON1 IBA rows accepted and documents that the 2026 literature search found no direct Candida MON1 functional paper requiring a new GO call\n- cites the search note from the accepted CGD ND rows so the NO_DATA decisions are explicit\n- marks CANAL/MON1 complete in the fungal PAINT family ledger and adds append-only gene/project history\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate CANAL MON1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/CANAL/MON1/2026-11-06T230511Z-codex-fc067e.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T230511Z-codex-f964a4.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render CANAL MON1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 uv run ai-gene-review render-projects projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES.md --verbose`\n- `git diff --check`","headRefName":"codex/CANAL-MON1-pthr13027-20261106","number":6147,"title":"Refresh CANAL MON1 PTHR13027 review","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6147"},{"body":"## Summary\n- adds the 2026 Ypt10/VINE and stationary-phase lipid-droplet MON1 papers to the yeast/MON1 review with manual reference reviews\n- confirms the existing PTHR13027 IBA calls remain aligned: fungal PTN000989606 vacuole rows, eukaryotic PTN000315021 GEF activity, and Opisthokonta PTN000315023 Mon1-Ccz1 complex membership\n- starts PTHR13027 in the fungal PAINT family ledger and marks yeast/MON1 checked\n- adds append-only gene and project history records\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate yeast MON1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/yeast/MON1/2026-11-06T225958Z-codex-de37b0.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T225958Z-codex-4faedd.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render yeast MON1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `git diff --check`","headRefName":"codex/yeast-MON1-pthr13027-20261106","number":6146,"title":"Review yeast MON1 PTHR13027 assignments","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6146"},{"body":"The fructose module now distinguishes a fused EI–HPr–EIIA relay from systems using a separate enzyme I, and requires both EIIB and EIIC on the transport protein. Sixteen completed gene reviews cover Pseudomonas, Xanthomonas, Rhodobacter, Azospirillum, Escherichia and Deinococcus. Eleven curated references, including two comparative reviews, support individual steps and their cross-species limits.\n\nThe Bacteria context describes this implementation, not universal bacterial capacity. E. coli FruA/FruK are component comparators because its FPr lacks enzyme I. In Deinococcus, the historical ATP-dependent assay is distinguished from the restored FruB reading frame in the resequenced strain; that sequence does not prove native PEP-dependent uptake. Experimental and reference strains are also kept distinct for Xanthomonas, and the main Azospirillum locus is distinguished from its paralog.\n\nThree family reviews document why family membership alone cannot prove domain fusion, substrate specificity or matched-system coverage. The production-circuit checker applies explicit evidence for those conditions and passes 49 positive/negative cases, rejecting seven mechanism mutations. All 148 original/seed annotation payloads and 34 raw/provider files are preserved, with no new annotation rows.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Focused gene, family, module, source, history and rendering checks passed. Thirteen module advisories document free-text relay chemistry and unresolved family-wide scope.\n- The original full run covered 10,695 reviews but exited 1 after an ontology-service timeout interrupted term checks. A complete 10,695-file term retry finished with no blocking errors and 116 advisory GO label mismatches; its raw exit code is also 1 because the term CLI counts warnings, which the repository policy treats as advisory. This is combined-stage validation, not an unconditional zero-exit claim for either command.\n- Schema, reference, best-practice and all 54 pathway-reference checks completed without blocking errors. The corpus has 52 reference advisories outside this panel and 27,758 best-practice warnings. One term-label advisory is in the panel: DR_B0074 uses the “D-fructose catabolic process” label while the validator expects “fructose catabolic process.” All 196 frozen inputs were verified unchanged before closeout.\n- Published main `577501df51` is integrated with scoped content preserved. See `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_fructose_pts_catabolism/full-validation.json` and `validated-head-integration.json` for the recorded limits and preservation proof.\n\nIndependent review, fresh CI and actual merge remain pending.\n\nThe latest review follow-up corrects module representative names against frozen UniProt records, moves the ontology-only gap into notes, improves AZOBR evidence specificity and reference assessments, and aligns FruB proposed-term definitions without changing their biological scope. All 148 annotation payloads/order/actions and 65 sources are preserved. Current official QuickGO confirms GO:0006001 as D-fructose catabolic process, so that authored label is retained. Focused gene/module/schema/reference/render/history checks pass, as do 49 coverage cases and seven mutations; only five reviewed-file hash pins changed in fixtures. The earlier full/combined-stage result above remains historical; this focused follow-up does not claim a new full-corpus pass. See `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_fructose_pts_catabolism/round1-review-response.md` and preservation proofs.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-fructose-pts-taxon-audit","number":6144,"title":"Audit fused fructose PTS architecture across bacterial lineages","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6144"},{"body":"The current ledger snapshot is derived from its rows: 34 COMPLETE, 5 PR_OPEN, 4 IN_PROGRESS and 734 NOT_STARTED (777 total). Dated journals remain intact.\n\nCurrent integration: head `f6bc2a981ed49a6e108d5b8f8a8066426dce5770` incorporates upstream main and 29 source qualifiers in six reviews. All other parsed review fields and row order are unchanged; 422 frozen files remain byte-identical outside those reviews/renders and shared metadata. Six focused validations, renders and histories pass, as do 107 module cases and four mechanism mutations. Replication completion remains backed by 569 verified merge blobs. Historical full validation remains scoped to its recorded head; fresh review and CI are pending.\n\nThe flagellar module now distinguishes the represented assembly/export machinery from native motor performance and complete flagellum sufficiency. It uses a Gammaproteobacteria scope, 31 roles and 38 completed gene reviews spanning Pseudomonas, Escherichia, Salmonella and Vibrio, with matched MotAB/MotCD/PomAB alternatives and explicit strain, construct and assay limits.\n\nSeventeen curated references, including three reviews, supply 26 mined findings and evidence for individual roles. The audit preserves all 240 original annotation payloads, 29 inherited NEW actions and 131 raw/provider sources; 14 supported additions yield 254 reviewed rows. FliE, compatible envelope/ring assembly and other background requirements remain explicit prerequisites outside the represented panel.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Renewed full `just validate-all` passed on science `813f6cdfc9`: 10,709 reviews, zero blocking errors, and all 54 pathway-reference checks passed. All 428 frozen files were verified unchanged. The 115 term advisories and 52 reference advisories are outside the 38-member panel; corpus warnings remain documented in `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_flagellar_assembly_export/round1-full-validation.json`.\n- Focused gene, source, history and module checks passed; module semantic validation reports zero warnings. The production compiler passed 107 evidence-scoped scenarios and rejected four mechanism mutations; five exact construct/sequence bridges were verified.\n- Review corrections distinguish motor participation in chemotaxis from export/assembly, reconcile FliI activity and complex membership, separate FliJ gate activation from ATPase activation, and retain refinements supported by the current GO graph. All original annotation payloads, order and inherited NEW actions remain unchanged.\n- Published main `577501df51` is integrated. All 428 scoped scientific/source/audit files remain byte-identical to the reviewed content; the verified chromosomal-replication completion record is preserved. Independent review, fresh CI and actual merge remain pending.\n\n\nCI ledger follow-up: three historical TreeGrafter decisions now carry explicit supersession records for the approved FlgE/FlgG reassessments. The original historical fields and all 428 scoped files at the approved head are preserved. The failing outcome-consistency regression now passes; no test or validator was changed. Proof: `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_flagellar_assembly_export/ci-outcome-ledger-reconciliation.json`.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-flagellar-taxon-audit","number":6138,"title":"Audit flagellar assembly and export across gammaproteobacterial lineages","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6138"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nCurates **12 new *Variovorax paradoxus* gene reviews** across `VARPS` (*V. paradoxus* S110, `NCBITaxon:543728`) and `VARPD` (*V. paradoxus* TBEA6, `NCBITaxon:34073`) for **Tranche 3** of the [VARIOVORAX](projects/VARIOVORAX.md) project, bringing total curated *Variovorax* genes to **34** across Tranches 1–3, and expands the three corresponding modules:\n\n### Batch 3A: Nickel-Dependent Urease Structural (`ureA`, `ureB`) & Maturation Accessory Complex (`ureD`, `ureE`, `ureF`, `ureG`)\n- **`VARPS/ureA` (`C5CXZ7`, `Vapar_4240`, 100 aa, Swiss-Prot reviewed):** Cytoplasmic $\\gamma$ structural subunit of the three-chain `(UreABC)3` urease complex (HAMAP `MF_00739`, InterPro `IPR012010`, Pfam `PF00547`). Marks `GO:0009039` (`urease activity`) as `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` and removes `GO:0016151` (`nickel cation binding`) because the binickel active center resides exclusively in the $\\alpha$ subunit `UreC` (`PMID:8718850`), while adding `GO:0005198` (`structural molecule activity`) and `GO:0035550` (`urease complex`).\n- **`VARPS/ureB` (`C5CXZ9`, `Vapar_4242`, 101 aa, Swiss-Prot reviewed):** Cytoplasmic $\\beta$ structural subunit of the `(UreABC)3` complex (HAMAP `MF_01954`, InterPro `IPR002019`, Pfam `PF00699`). Marks `GO:0009039` as `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED`, accepts `GO:0035550` (`urease complex`), `GO:0043419` (`urea catabolic process`), and `GO:0005737` (`cytoplasm`), and adds `GO:0005198` (`structural molecule activity`, `PMID:8718850`).\n- **`VARPS/ureD` (`C5CVT2`, `Vapar_3971`, 276 aa, Swiss-Prot reviewed):** Cytoplasmic urease accessory apoprotein scaffold (HAMAP `MF_01384`, InterPro `IPR002669`, PANTHER `PTHR33643:SF1`). Retains `GO:0016151` (`nickel cation binding`) as `KEEP_AS_NON_CORE` (`PMID:22369361`) and adds `GO:0018237` (`urease activator activity`), `GO:0044183` (`protein folding chaperone`), and `GO:0043419` (`urea catabolic process`) (`PMID:7909161`).\n- **`VARPD/ureE` (`A0A0H2LU38`, `VPARA_50940`, 200 aa):** Cytoplasmic nickel-binding metallochaperone (HAMAP `MF_00822`, InterPro `IPR012406`, Pfam `PF02814`/`PF05194`) with a 15-histidine C-terminal tail (residues 148–200). Accepts `GO:0016151` (`nickel cation binding`) and `GO:0005737` (`cytoplasm`), refines `GO:0019627` to `GO:0043419` (`urea catabolic process`), and modifies `GO:0006457` and `GO:0065003` to `GO:0051604` (`protein maturation`) (`PMID:8318889`).\n- **`VARPD/ureF` (`A0A0H2LZ72`, `VPARA_50930`, 223 aa):** Cytoplasmic GTPase-gating urease accessory protein (HAMAP `MF_01385`, InterPro `IPR002639`, PANTHER `PTHR33620:SF1`). Removes over-propagated `GO:0016151` (`nickel cation binding`) and adds `GO:0018237` (`urease activator activity`), `GO:0030695` (`GTPase regulator activity`), and `GO:0043419` (`urea catabolic process`) (`PMID:22369361`).\n- **`VARPS/ureG` (`C5CY05`, `Vapar_4248`, 216 aa, Swiss-Prot reviewed):** Cytoplasmic SIMIBI-class G3E GTPase and nickel metallochaperone (HAMAP `MF_01389`, InterPro `IPR004400`, PANTHER `PTHR31715:SF0`) preserving the P-loop (`24-GPVGSGKT-31`) and `Cys72-Pro73-His74` nickel-binding motif. Accepts `GO:0003924` (`GTPase activity`), `GO:0016151` (`nickel cation binding`), `GO:0043419` (`urea catabolic process`), and `GO:0005737` (`cytoplasm`), and adds `GO:0005525` (`GTP binding`) (`PMID:10500143`; `PMID:22369361`).\n\n### Batch 3B: ExbB-ExbD Siderophore-Import Motor (`exbB4`, `exbD5`), Tripartite CzcCBA & CzcD Heavy-Metal Efflux (`czcB`, `czcC`, `czcD`), and Exopolyphosphatase (`ppx`)\n- **`VARPD/exbB4` (`A0A0H2MHH3`, `VPARA_25810`, 238 aa):** Three-TM inner-membrane MotA/TolQ/ExbB pentameric proton-channel stator (`Pfam:PF01618`, residues 100–201) in the `exbD5-exbB4-tonB-bfrD1` (`VPARA_25800-VPARA_25830`) operon. Marks TreeGrafter `GO:0017038` (`protein import`) as `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` (matching `PSEPK/exbB`), accepts `GO:0005886` (`plasma membrane`), and adds `GO:0015078` (`proton transmembrane transporter activity`), `GO:1902600` (`proton transmembrane transport`), `GO:0015891` (`siderophore transport`), and `GO:0098797` (`plasma membrane protein complex`) (`PMID:37219427`; `PMID:20420522`).\n- **`VARPD/exbD5` (`A0A0H2M1F3`, `VPARA_25800`, 137 aa):** Single-pass type II inner-membrane proton-coupling dimer (`Pfam:PF02472`, `PANTHER:PTHR30558`) with conserved transmembrane `Asp23` (within `20-PLVDVMLVLLIIFIITVPVM-39`) in the `exbD5-exbB4-tonB-bfrD1` operon. Refines `GO:0022857` to `GO:0015078` and `GO:0055085` to `GO:1902600` and `GO:0015891`, and adds `GO:0030288` (`outer membrane-bounded periplasmic space`) and `GO:0098797` (`PMID:37219427`; `PMID:20420522`).\n- **`VARPD/czcB` (`A0A0H2LX82`, `VPARA_44900`, 455 aa):** Periplasmic HME-RND membrane-fusion adaptor (`NCBIfam:TIGR01730`, `InterPro:IPR006143`) encoded between `czcA3` (`VPARA_44890`) and `czcC` (`VPARA_44910`), with an N-terminal 10-His metal-binding region (residues 41–121) and four CzcB/CusB periplasmic domains (`PF25973`, `PF25893`, `PF25954`, `PF25975`). Marks `GO:0022857` (`transmembrane transporter activity`) as `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED` because CzcB is a non-transporting periplasmic adaptor, adds `GO:0005198` (`structural molecule activity`) with `contributes_to` `GO:0015562` (`efflux transmembrane transporter activity`) in `core_functions`, generalizes CusB copper terms (`GO:0015679`, `GO:0060003`) to `GO:0098655` (`monoatomic cation transmembrane transport`), keeps `GO:0046914` (`transition metal ion binding`) as `KEEP_AS_NON_CORE`, and accepts `GO:0030288` (`PMID:10559151`; `PMID:12829273`).\n- **`VARPD/czcC` (`A0A0H2M0U4`, `VPARA_44910`, 445 aa):** Outer-membrane factor (`Pfam:PF02321`, `PANTHER:PTHR30203`) efflux duct subunit of the tripartite CzcCBA complex. Accepts `GO:0015562` (`efflux transmembrane transporter activity`), refines `GO:0016020` to `GO:0009279` (`cell outer membrane`), `GO:0022857` to `GO:0015562` and `GO:0008324`, and `GO:0055085` to `GO:0098655` (`PMID:10559151`).\n- **`VARPD/czcD` (`A0A0H2M558`, `VPARA_15480`, 320 aa):** 6-TM inner-membrane Cation Diffusion Facilitator (CDF) $\\text{Zn}^{2+}/\\text{Co}^{2+}/\\text{Cd}^{2+}$ antiporter (`NCBIfam:TIGR01297`, `Pfam:PF01545`) with conserved TM2/TM5 Site A ligands (`His59`, `His62`, `His188`, `Asp192`) and a 15-His cytoplasmic loop (`152-HHHGHGHDHGHGHAHPHHEGHGHAHGHD-179`). Accepts `GO:0005385` (`zinc ion transmembrane transporter activity`), `GO:0071577` (`zinc ion transmembrane transport`), and `GO:0006882` (`intracellular zinc ion homeostasis`), and refines `GO:0016020` to `GO:0005886` (`plasma membrane`) (`PMID:10559151`; `PMID:27797207`).\n- **`VARPD/ppx` (`A0A0H2MBK5`, `VPARA_10490`, 506 aa):** Cytosolic exopolyphosphatase (EC 3.6.1.11, `PANTHER:PTHR30005:SF14`, `InterPro:IPR030673`) with an N-terminal ASKHA catalytic domain (`Pfam:PF02541`, P1 `11-DLGSNSFR-18`, P2 `142-DIGGRSTE-149`) and C-terminal polyP-binding domain (`Pfam:PF21447`). Accepts `GO:0004309` (`exopolyphosphatase activity`) and `GO:0006798` (`polyphosphate catabolic process`), marks `GO:0016462` as `TOO_GENERAL`, and adds `GO:0005829` (`cytosol`) (`PMID:23687963`).\n\n### Module & Project Updates\n- Updated [`modules/urease_biogenesis_urea_hydrolysis.yaml`](modules/urease_biogenesis_urea_hydrolysis.yaml), [`modules/bacterial_tonb_siderophore_import.yaml`](modules/bacterial_tonb_siderophore_import.yaml), and [`modules/variovorax_metallophore_metal_homeostasis.yaml`](modules/variovorax_metallophore_metal_homeostasis.yaml).\n- Updated [`projects/VARIOVORAX.md`](projects/VARIOVORAX.md) and [`projects/VARIOVORAX/VARIOVORAX_MODULE_PLAN.md`](projects/VARIOVORAX/VARIOVORAX_MODULE_PLAN.md) and rendered all HTML pages.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-variovorax-tranche3","number":6137,"title":"Curate Variovorax Tranche 3 genes (ureABDEFG, exbB4/D5, czcBCD, ppx) and update modules","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6137"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nThis PR completes **Tranche 2** of the ***Variovorax* Rhizosphere Plant–Microbe Interactions & Critical Metal Recovery (`VARIOVORAX`)** curation project (following Tranche 1 in #6000), adding **10 new *Variovorax paradoxus* gene reviews** (`VARPS` and `VARPD`), **1 new pathway module** (`bacterial_tonb_siderophore_import`), module member/evidence updates across **3 existing pathway modules** (`pqq_biosynthesis`, `urease_biogenesis_urea_hydrolysis`, `taurine_uptake_and_desulfonation`), and the **PR #6000 non-blocking follow-up polish**:\n\n### 1. Batch 2A — Coenzyme PQQ Biosynthesis & Periplasmic Quinoprotein Dehydrogenase (6 Genes)\n- **`VARPS/pqqA` (`C5CPT7`, `Vapar_3057`, reviewed Swiss-Prot, 24 aa):** Ribosomally synthesized precursor peptide supplying conserved Glu16 and Tyr20 for PQQ biosynthesis (`ACCEPT` `GO:0018189`, `NEW` `GO:0005829` `cytosol`, linked in `modules/pqq_biosynthesis.yaml`).\n- **`VARPD/pqqB` (`A0A0H2M8Q2`, `VPARA_00950`, 307 aa):** Metallo-$\\beta$-lactamase-fold $\\text{Fe(II)}$-dependent non-heme hydroxylase catalyzing $\\text{O}_2$ insertion into the PqqA-derived Glu-Tyr cross-linked intermediate (`ACCEPT` `GO:0018189`, `NEW` `GO:0016705`, `GO:0005506`, `GO:0005829`, supported by `PMID:30811189` and `PMID:32731194`, linked in `modules/pqq_biosynthesis.yaml`).\n- **`VARPD/pqqC` (`A0A0H2MDI9`, `VPARA_00940`, 254 aa, EC 1.3.3.11):** Cofactor-less pyrroloquinoline-quinone synthase catalyzing ring cyclization and 8-electron oxidation of AHQQ to mature PQQ (`ACCEPT` `GO:0033732`, `GO:0018189`, `NEW` `GO:0005829`, linked in `modules/pqq_biosynthesis.yaml`).\n- **`VARPD/pqqD` (`A0A0H2M992`, `VPARA_00930`, 98 aa):** Cofactor-less RiPP recognition element (RRE) peptide chaperone that binds PqqA ($K_d \\approx 200\\text{ nM}$) and forms a ternary complex with PqqE (`ACCEPT` `GO:0018189`, `REMOVE` erroneous InterPro2GO `GO:0048038` `quinone binding` with structured `source_defects` on `InterPro:IPR022479`, `NEW` `GO:0042277` `peptide binding` and `GO:0005829`, supported by `PMID:25817994` and `PMID:26961875`, linked in `modules/pqq_biosynthesis.yaml`).\n- **`VARPD/pqqE` (`A0A0H2MPV4`, `VPARA_00920`, 383 aa, EC 1.21.98.4):** Radical SAM SPASM-domain PqqA peptide cyclase curated per `projects/RADICAL_SAM.md` (`MODIFY` `GO:0003824` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0009975` `cyclase activity`; `MODIFY` `GO:0005506`, `GO:0046872`, `GO:0051536` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0051539` `4 iron, 4 sulfur cluster binding`; `ACCEPT` `GO:0009975`, `GO:0051539`, `GO:1904047` `S-adenosyl-L-methionine binding`, `GO:0018189`; `NEW` `GO:0005829`; `proposed_new_terms: PqqA peptide cyclase activity`, linked in `modules/pqq_biosynthesis.yaml`).\n- **`VARPD/VPARA_00790` (`A0A0H2MDH4`, `VPARA_00790`, 608 aa, EC 1.1.2.7):** Periplasmic PQQ-dependent alcohol/methanol dehydrogenase (`ACCEPT` `GO:0015050` `alcohol dehydrogenase (quinone) activity`, `GO:0046872` `metal ion binding`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0016614` `oxidoreductase activity, acting on CH-OH group of donors`; `MODIFY` `GO:0070967` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0070968` `pyrroloquinoline quinone binding`; `NEW` `GO:0070968`, `GO:0006066` `alcohol metabolic process`, `GO:0042597` `periplasmic space`).\n\n### 2. Batch 2B — TonB-Energized Ferric-Siderophore Import, Nickel Urease & Taurine Dioxygenase (4 Genes + 1 Module)\n- **`modules/bacterial_tonb_siderophore_import.yaml` (`MODULE:bacterial_tonb_siderophore_import`):** New `TRANSPORT_PATHWAY` module modeling the conserved Gram-negative two-part outer-membrane ferric-siderophore uptake mechanism coupling inner-membrane TonB proton-motive-force energy transduction (`tonB`) to outer-membrane TonB-dependent receptor plug unfolding (`bfrD1`).\n- **`VARPD/bfrD1` (`A0A0H2M677`, `VPARA_25830`, 739 aa):** Outer-membrane TonB-dependent ferric-siderophore receptor (`ACCEPT` `GO:0015344`, `GO:0033214`, `GO:0009279`; `MODIFY` `GO:0015343` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0015344` and `GO:0015891` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0033214`; `REMOVE` `GO:0038023` `signaling receptor activity` with structured `source_defects` on `InterPro:IPR010105`, as this 739-aa canonical TBDT lacks an N-terminal FecR-coupled anti-sigma signaling extension).\n- **`VARPD/tonB` (`A0A0H2M2E3`, `VPARA_25820`, 232 aa):** Inner-membrane proton-motive-force energy transducer (`ACCEPT` `GO:0031992`, `GO:0005886`, `GO:0030288`, `GO:0098797`; `MODIFY` `GO:0055085` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0015891` `siderophore transport`; `REMOVE` `GO:0015031` `protein transport`).\n- **`VARPS/ureC` (`C5CY00`, `Vapar_4243`, reviewed Swiss-Prot, 572 aa, EC 3.5.1.5):** Nickel-dependent urease catalytic $\\alpha$ subunit (`ACCEPT` `GO:0009039`, `GO:0016151`, `GO:0043419`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0016787`, `GO:0016810`; `MODIFY` `GO:0005737` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0005829` `cytosol`; `NEW` `GO:0035550` `urease complex`, linked in `modules/urease_biogenesis_urea_hydrolysis.yaml`).\n- **`VARPD/tauD1` (`A0A0H2MHV8`, `VPARA_23370`, 302 aa, EC 1.14.11.17):** Cytosolic $\\text{Fe(II)}$/2-oxoglutarate-dependent taurine dioxygenase (`ACCEPT` `GO:0000908`; `TOO_GENERAL` `GO:0016706`, `GO:0016491`; `MODIFY` `GO:0005737` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0005829` `cytosol`; `NEW` `GO:0005506` `iron ion binding` and `GO:0019529` `taurine catabolic process`, linked in `modules/taurine_uptake_and_desulfonation.yaml`).\n\n### 3. PR #6000 Follow-Up Polish\n- Refined `NEW` BP annotation on `VARPD/acd` (`B9U6P5`) from `GO:0006790` (`sulfur compound metabolic process`) to `GO:0044273` (`sulfur compound catabolic process`).\n- Removed redundant `NEW` `GO:0005886` (`plasma membrane`) row in `VARPD/gcd` (already proposed via `MODIFY` on `GO:0016020`) and fixed `GO:0019595` $\\rightarrow$ `GO:0019521` (`D-gluconate metabolic process`) in `gcd-notes.md`.\n- Added inline `[PMID:..., \"...\"]` citations and explicit prospective-hypothesis framing for lanthanide/REE roles in `gcd-notes.md`, `ppk-notes.md`, and `czcA3-notes.md`.\n\n## Validation\n- `ai-gene-review validate --verbose --terms` passed on all 11 new/updated gene reviews (`0 errors, 0 warnings`).\n- `python -m ai_gene_review.validation.module_validator` passed on `modules/bacterial_tonb_siderophore_import.yaml`, `modules/pqq_biosynthesis.yaml`, `modules/urease_biogenesis_urea_hydrolysis.yaml`, and `modules/taurine_uptake_and_desulfonation.yaml` (`0 errors`).\n- `linkml-validate` passed on all history records.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-variovorax-tranche2","number":6135,"title":"Curate Variovorax Tranche 2 (PQQ biosynthesis, quinoprotein ADH, TonB/BfrD1 siderophore import, UreC, TauD1)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6135"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- 13 mouse keratin-associated protein reviews had 37 ND rows marked MODIFY on the strength of family-level KAP literature. Each review's notes file records what has been published on the mouse gene itself: sequencing, cluster descriptions, RNA expression, GOA rows attributed to the wrong gene, and one binding study whose species is not stated. None of it identifies a function, so under the ND rule from #5416 all 37 rows are now ACCEPT with `nd_case: INFERABLE`. Each one cites the notes file as the record of the search, which `nd_policy` requires.\n- 33 of the proposed replacement terms are not on any other row in their review. These are kept as `NEW` ISS rows with the earlier quotes, so the inference stays in the review and the ND keeps its root term.\n- `projects/NO_DATA/scripts/apply_acceptance_rule.py` changes:\n  - it accepts gene prefixes as arguments;\n  - ACCEPT decisions can carry search quotes;\n  - when an inherited reason opens on the ND row, the argument after \"but\" is kept.\n\n  The decision table, inventory and project page are updated. The project page also lists the five rows from main that are still unswept: human CDKAL1 BP, ARATH PGR3 CC, and rat Actl7a, Aph1a and Pik3c2a BP. They are left for a separate decision.\n- There are history records for the 13 genes and for the project.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `just validate mouse <Krtap…>` on all 13 reviews: 0 errors. Warnings fell from 50 to 13, and the 13 that remain come from the environment (`UV_NATIVE_TLS`), not from the reviews.\n- `just validate-history` passes on all 14 new records.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] 1,580 related tests pass (nd_policy, compliance, action consistency, history).\n- [x] Re-running the apply script on the Krtap prefixes changes nothing (it is idempotent).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_011NfK5BnZSJayf7CqUrtyLq)_","headRefName":"claude/happy-cray-yngr26","number":6132,"title":"NO_DATA: apply the ND acceptance rule to the mouse Krtap reviews","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6132"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nFollow-up raised in the #5879 review. `_cites_search`, shared by the `nd_policy` warning and the `nd_search_support` score, credited any reference whose name contained `-notes.md` or `-deep-research`, with no quote. Adding a bare notes filename to an accepted ND therefore earned the compliance credit whether or not the notes recorded a search, and the weekly-compliance agent optimizes that score.\n\nThe predicate, now `cites_search(review, data, gene_dir=None)`:\n- **Publications** (`PMID:`, `DOI:`, `PMC:`, `PMCID:`) count as before, in `supported_by` or `additional_reference_ids`.\n- **A deep-research or notes file** counts only when both hold:\n  - it is cited in `supported_by` with a nonblank `supporting_text` (or `supporting_text_fulltext`), so the existing verbatim local-quote check confirms the file says it;\n  - it is in the review's own gene folder. Both `file:ORG/GENE/...` and `file:genes/ORG/GENE/...` forms are accepted. The validator and the compliance analyzer pass the review's real `ORG/FOLDER`, taken from its path, so the test is exact, including synonym folders such as `SCHPO/rhp51`. When no path is known, a symbol/accession/self-UniProt fallback is used.\n- A bare or unquoted file, a file in `additional_reference_ids`, another gene's file, and the UniProt record do not count.\n- Any verbatim line of the gene's own file passes. That narrows the loophole rather than closing it, and the docs say so.\n\nUpdated to match: the warning's suggestion text, CLAUDE.md's ND section, `projects/NO_DATA.md`, `docs/compliance.md` and the `qc_config.yaml` comment.\n\nAlso in this PR:\n- The private name is promoted to `cites_search`.\n- Plugins whose `evaluate` accepts `source_path` now receive the review path. Two-argument plugins still work.\n- A history record for `NO_DATA` is added.\n\n**Effect:** the warning now fires on 210 of 321 accepted ND rows in 150 reviews. These are the rows the follow-up workflow is working through, quoting each gene's recorded search where one exists.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `pytest tests/test_nd_policy.py tests/test_compliance.py`: 108 passed. New tests cover:\n  - publications in either slot;\n  - a search file passing only when it is quoted and is the gene's own;\n  - both `file:` path forms, for own and other genes;\n  - the ALG14 case (organism code must not widen the own-folder set);\n  - the exact folder given by path;\n  - `supporting_text_fulltext`;\n  - files in `additional_reference_ids`;\n  - the per-row compliance score;\n  - a plugin with the existing two-argument signature.\n- [x] Doctests in `nd_policy.py`.\n- [x] `just validate CANAL CEK1`: its own-notes quote in the `genes/` form passes.\n- [x] `ruff check` on the changed files.\n- [x] `just validate-history` on the record.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01RSuFVGcWp4qv76US48Weh4","headRefName":"claude/nd-search-quote","number":6126,"title":"ND search citation: require a quote from the gene's own search record","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6126"},{"body":"The DnaK/DnaJ/GrpE module now distinguishes experimentally supported bacterial implementations of the refolding cycle. Canonical J-protein ATPase activation and Thermus client handoff have separate evidence; only DnaK hydrolyzes ATP, and occupied DafA is an optional inhibitory context. Five curated compatible cycles prevent family membership alone from implying a functioning relay.\n\nThe audit includes 13 curated references (three reviews, 20 findings), evidence for all three roles and six connections, two family reviews, and 14 complete gene reviews across E. coli, P. putida, M. tuberculosis and T. thermophilus. It preserves 227 historical annotation payloads and 43 raw/provider/prediction files, adds six supported molecular-function annotations, and leaves 14 unverifiable claims UNDECIDED. Donor strain, assay host, construct sequence and experimental temperature limits are explicit.\n\nValidation: focused gene/family/module checks, histories and renders pass; 178 scoped controls and 82 quotation checks pass. Full `just validate-all` completed on 10,589 reviews with zero best-practice errors, no blocking ontology errors and all 54 referenced pathways passing. Its exit 1 comes from 17 known citation failures in 16 unrelated yeast reviews; all 22 relevant inputs match the pinned starting base. Four module advisories retain two proposed-function limitations and two unresolved family-wide grants. Published-main integration preserves every scoped nonshared file and changes no validation implementation. CI and independent review remain required.\n\nEvidence and limitations: `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_dnak_dnaj_refolding_cycle/README.md`, `fullrun-terminal.json`, and `prepublication-integration.json`.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-dnak-taxon-audit","number":6125,"title":"Audit comparative DnaK–DnaJ–GrpE refolding mechanisms and gene functions","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6125"},{"body":"The module distinguishes dye-linked DgcA/DdhC oxidation from oxygen-dependent, THF-coupled DMGO, and separates heterotetrameric SoxABDG chemistry from monomeric sarcosine oxidation. Nineteen curated references supply 23 findings; individual activities carry comparative evidence and strain/construct boundaries. Twenty-two completed gene reviews and four refined family reviews preserve all 96 original/seeded annotation rows, including 28 inherited NEW rows, 44 raw files, six provider artifacts and the existing PAINT assertions.\n\nThe historical P. putida var. naraensis C-83 sarcosine dehydrogenase has biochemical evidence but no established gene or family identity. It remains an explicitly ungrounded alternative; the module stays DRAFT and this coverage gap remains open. Generic oxidoreductase annotations cannot satisfy that alternative in the panel checker. No gene or family identifier was invented to make the audit look complete.\n\nAll 22 focused gene validations pass, as do 125 capacity/chemistry/preservation cases, 59 module and 74 family quotations, family grounding, history checks and rendering. Full validation of science commit `8bde501e2ff0646446a2e4deda82fad8b4b1192c` ended exit 1 across 10,553 reviews, solely from 17 reproduced reference errors in 16 unrelated yeast reviews. All 22 relevant inputs match pinned base `f0306d4de38a86ed077b066841167cc071bd56fe`. Schema, ontology and best practices have no blocking errors; 52 reference advisories, 115 ontology advisories and 27,708 best-practice warnings remain. All 54 PMID-bearing pathways pass. Exact error tuples and hashes are committed in `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_dimethylglycine_sarcosine_catabolism/fullrun-terminal.json`.\n\nThe comparative catalog was subsequently relocated byte-for-byte outside recursive module schema discovery; all 779 module YAML files discovered at that validation passed. Historical validation proofs retain their original paths and commits. The earlier module PR #5979 is merged; this PR carries the comparative audit. Reviewed-panel capacity does not establish native expression, genomic absence or organism-wide sufficiency.\nThe latest access/prose correction removes four stale full-text-unavailable flags, accurately distinguishes the DgcA and DdhC FMN verdicts, removes schema commentary from twelve proposed-term justifications, and preserves the authors' missing-cysteine explanation as a hypothesis. All 18 changed reviews pass focused validation, rendering and history checks; their 82 rows/actions, 24 inherited NEW rows, core functions and quotes are unchanged, as are 73 source artifacts. The historical full run above was not rerun for this prose/access-only correction. Details and exact preservation proof: `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_dimethylglycine_sarcosine_catabolism/round5-consultation.json` and `round5-root-verification.json`.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-dimethylglycine-taxon-audit","number":6122,"title":"Audit bacterial dimethylglycine and sarcosine catabolism across species","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6122"},{"body":"## Summary\n- mark stale PTHR32379 Rmt2 checklist entries as covered by PRs #6035, #6037, #6038, #6039, and #6040\n- mark the stale PTHR24055 later-candidate row as covered by the existing MAPK Family 4 section\n- expand the PTHR32379 initial-family row to name ASPFU/rmt2 and EMENI/rmt2 explicitly\n- re-render the fungal PAINT project page and add append-only project history\n\n## Verification\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T110443Z-claude-code-4a1d69.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/fungal-paint-ledger-cleanup-20261106","number":6119,"title":"Reconcile fungal PAINT family ledger","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6119"},{"body":"## Summary\n- re-review S. cerevisiae CSH1 in the fungal PTHR32385 IPC mannosyltransferase context\n- add direct PTN001285482 PAINT support to the generic MF and MIPC-biosynthesis IBA rows\n- align the broad sphingolipid parent rows and downstream M(IP)2C rows with the exact IPC-to-MIPC reaction\n- record newer 2022 Csh1/Sur1 N-glycan and 2026 ethanol-stress literature without adding downstream stress-response annotations\n- mark yeast/CSH1 complete in the FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES ledger\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate yeast CSH1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-families\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/yeast/CSH1/2026-11-06T104721Z-claude-code-090171.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T104721Z-claude-code-18727b.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/yeast-CSH1-pthr32385-20261106","number":6118,"title":"Review yeast CSH1 in PTHR32385 family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6118"},{"body":"## Summary\n- review the Aspergillus fumigatus Af293 MitA/Q4WDG6 IPC mannosyltransferase annotations in the fungal PTHR32385/GT32 PAINT context\n- accept the PTN001285482 PAINT biological-process IBA and modify the generic mannosyltransferase rows to exact GO:0103064 IPC mannosyltransferase activity\n- update the FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES ledger and refresh the PTHR32385 family review with the ASPFU member\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate ASPFU Q4WDG6\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/Q4WDG6/2026-11-06T102649Z-claude-code-070446.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/other/PTHR32385-family-review/2026-11-06T102653Z-claude-code-60c93a.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T102649Z-claude-code-a0d42b.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-families\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/ASPFU-Q4WDG6-pthr32385-20261106","number":6116,"title":"Review ASPFU Q4WDG6 in PTHR32385 family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6116"},{"body":"## Summary\n- Re-reviews yeast SUR1 in the PTHR32385 fungal IPC mannosyltransferase family context\n- Aligns the PTN001285482 IBA rows with PAINT evidence: GO:0051999 is accepted as conserved MIPC biosynthesis, while generic GO:0000030 is modified to GO:0103064\n- Marks downstream M(IP)2C process rows as over-annotated because Sur1 makes MIPC and Ipt1 performs the subsequent M(IP)2C-forming step\n- Adds the newer 2022 Glycobiology Sur1/Csh1 N-glycan paper cache and reference review\n- Updates SUR1 notes, the fungal PAINT project ledger, generated HTML, and append-only history\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate yeast SUR1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/yeast/SUR1/2026-11-06T100801Z-codex-1dbbd4.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T100802Z-codex-860274.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render yeast SUR1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES","headRefName":"codex/yeast-SUR1-pthr32385-20261106","number":6115,"title":"Review yeast SUR1 in PTHR32385 family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6115"},{"body":"## Summary\n- fetch and complete CANAL/MIT1 as the CGD-seeded Candida albicans IPC mannosyltransferase in PTHR32385\n- review both PTN001285482 IBA rows as sound and add propagation reviews\n- refine broad GO:0000030 and sphingolipid-process rows to exact GO:0103064 or GO:0051999 decisions\n- retain the 2026 CGD root cellular-component ND row because no direct Candida Mit1 localization paper was found\n- mark the downstream M(IP)2C and mannosyltransferase-complex ARBA rows as overextensions\n- tick the family ledger, render the gene and project pages, cache PMID:15347680, and add append-only history records\n\n## Verification\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate CANAL MIT1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render CANAL MIT1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/CANAL/MIT1/2026-11-06T095733Z-codex-8513b1.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T095733Z-codex-96fbbe.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/CANAL-MIT1-pthr32385-20261106","number":6114,"title":"Review CANAL MIT1 in fungal IPC mannosyltransferase family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6114"},{"body":"## Summary\n- complete SCHPO/SPAC17G8.11c as imt3 in the PTHR32385 IPC mannosyltransferase family\n- strengthen both PTN001285482 IBA rows with PAINT support and propagation reviews\n- add a NEW exact GO:0103064 IPC mannosyltransferase row because GOA only has the generic GO:0000030 PAINT parent\n- record the 2023 Willet et al. imt3/css1 result as newer paralog-specific context without adding downstream cell-wall process annotations\n- tick the family ledger, render the gene and project pages, and add append-only history records\n\n## Verification\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO SPAC17G8.11c\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render SCHPO SPAC17G8.11c\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/SPAC17G8.11c/2026-11-06T095255Z-codex-4f429e.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T095255Z-codex-f2d250.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-imt3-pthr32385-20261106","number":6113,"title":"Review SCHPO imt3 in fungal IPC mannosyltransferase family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6113"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- re-review SCHPO/SPCC4F11.04c against the PTHR32385 fungal IPC mannosyltransferase PAINT node\n- align both PTN001285482 IBA rows with the cached PANTHER family review and replace the weak UniProt protein-name quote on GO:0000030 with PAINT plus PMID:20388730 support\n- add a conservative NEW row for exact GO:0103064 IPC mannosyltransferase activity so the core molecular function is reflected in the review\n- add manual reference reviews/findings, imt2 notes, rendered HTML, append-only history, and the PTHR32385 ledger update\n\n## Verification\n\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO SPCC4F11.04c\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render SCHPO SPCC4F11.04c\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/SPCC4F11.04c/2026-11-06T094608Z-codex-22ee8b.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T094608Z-codex-610815.yaml\n- git diff --check\n\n## Notes\n\nI searched newer S. pombe sphingolipid literature before making this pass. PMID:37792890 gives useful imt2/imt3-specific MIPC context, but it does not justify adding downstream cell-wall annotations to SPCC4F11.04c.","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-imt2-pthr32385-20261106","number":6112,"title":"Review SCHPO SPCC4F11.04c in fungal IPC mannosyltransferase family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6112"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- re-review `SCHPO/imt1` against the `PTHR32385` fungal IPC mannosyltransferase PAINT node\n- align both `PTN001285482` IBA rows with the cached PANTHER family review and replace the weak UniProt protein-name quote on `GO:0000030` with PAINT plus PMID:20388730 support\n- add manual reference reviews/findings, `imt1` notes, the exact `GO:0103064` core molecular function, rendered HTML, and append-only history\n- start Family 21 for `PTHR32385` in `projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES.md` and regenerate the project page\n\n## Verification\n\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO imt1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render SCHPO imt1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/imt1/2026-11-06T094247Z-codex-da9062.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T094247Z-codex-fa684e.yaml`\n- `git diff --check`\n\n## Notes\n\nI searched newer S. pombe sphingolipid literature before making this pass. PMID:37792890 is useful family context for MIPC/cell-wall biology and imt3, but it did not justify adding downstream process rows to `imt1`.","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-imt1-pthr32385-20261106","number":6111,"title":"Review SCHPO imt1 in fungal IPC mannosyltransferase family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6111"},{"body":"## Summary\n- review ASPFU/sec2 in the PTHR14430 Sec2/Rabin3 family\n- accept the Opisthokonta Rab-GEF IBA and fungal cell-tip IBA as sound transfers to the long Aspergillus Sec2 ortholog\n- cache and review PMID:39852455 as direct sec2 phenotype context without adding unsupported autophagy, cell-wall, or virulence process rows\n- update the FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES ledger and add matching history records\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate ASPFU sec2\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render ASPFU sec2\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/sec2/2026-11-06T092443Z-codex-d6ba9e.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T092443Z-codex-83caf4.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/ASPFU-sec2-pthr14430-20261106","number":6110,"title":"Review ASPFU sec2 in fungal Sec2 family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6110"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- Create the `CANAL/SEC2` review for the PTHR14430 Sec2/Rabin3 fungal stack\n- Accept the Opisthokonta Rab-GEF and fungal cell-tip PAINT IBA rows as sound for Candida Sec2\n- Remove the stale ND MF row, refine generic secretory-vesicle placement to `GO:0070319`, and mark broad filamentous growth as too general relative to the direct hyphal-growth row\n- Add cached full-text publications, rendered pages, GO term-cache entries, and append-only history for the gene/project edits\n\n## Validation\n\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate CANAL SEC2`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render CANAL SEC2`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/CANAL/SEC2/2026-11-06T091921Z-codex-37d5aa.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T091921Z-codex-4dbc64.yaml`\n- `git diff --check`","headRefName":"codex/CANAL-SEC2-pthr14430-20261106","number":6109,"title":"Review CANAL SEC2 in fungal Sec2 family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6109"},{"body":"The module models choline import followed by two independently supported oxidation steps across thirteen completed bacterial gene reviews. Round2 adds the missing OpuBC and OpuBD choline-transport process proposals, supported by substrate capture/translocation roles and primary OpuB uptake evidence. It also removes a duplicated GbsB reference and shortens repeated quotes while preserving complete source excerpts.\n\nAll 105 prior annotation payloads are unchanged; two appended IC proposals give 107 current rows. The 94 historical rows, 26 raw files and four provider artifacts remain intact. Three focused gene checks, five histories, checker lint, 43 capacity cases, project rendering and preservation checks pass.\n\nThe round2 full run on `96797150bae52515f832f291d90438d540c96bbe` is terminal, exit 1: 10,543 reviews, 19 exact reproduced reference errors in 17 unrelated yeast reviews and 23 base-identical input blobs. Schema and term checks have no blocking errors; best practices has zero errors and 27,994 warnings in 5,036 files. There are 52 reference advisories; all 54 PMID-bearing pathway files pass. `round2-fullrun-terminal.json` records the exact tuples and log SHA256 `accb42525d8fca86a5d6f468f8abba33dcf29348d40797fd7184ba5335776a8f`. Earlier full runs remain recorded separately. Two additional closure histories validate; science, payloads and protected sources remain unchanged. Renewed review/CI and actual merge remain pending.\n\n\nCI packaging follow-up: ontology evidence now lives under `analysis/module_audits`, with byte-identical content. CodA changes only two source-reference paths; parsed equivalence and original annotation payloads are verified. All 779 recursive ModuleReview schemas, focused CodA validation, ten helper histories and 43 capacity cases pass. The historical full-corpus result is unchanged; the path-only change was checked separately.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-choline-betaine-taxon-audit","number":6108,"title":"Audit choline-to-glycine-betaine import and oxidation across bacteria","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6108"},{"body":"## Summary\n- review S. pombe spo13/SPCC1183.12 as the short meiosis-specific PTHR14430:SF4 Sec2-family Rab GEF\n- accept the direct and InterPro guanyl-nucleotide exchange rows, narrow generic spindle pole body to meiotic spindle pole body, and remove generic Spo2 protein binding\n- record that no PTHR14430 PAINT IBA rows propagate to Spo13, consistent with the fungal cell-tip node staying on the long Sec2 branch\n- update the FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES ledger and add append-only history\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO spo13\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render SCHPO spo13\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/spo13/2026-11-06T091150Z-codex-3787db.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T091147Z-codex-ce6cac.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-spo13-pthr14430-20261106","number":6107,"title":"Review SCHPO spo13 in fungal Sec2 family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6107"},{"body":"## Summary\n- create the SCHPO/sec2 review for PTHR14430\n- accept the Opisthokonta GEF and fungal cell-tip PAINT rows as sound for fission-yeast Sec2\n- record the CACAO PMID:27630562 wrong-reference defect and modify that row to GO:0035974\n- update the fungal PAINT family ledger and add append-only history\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO sec2\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render SCHPO sec2\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/sec2/2026-11-06T085950Z-codex-a6b597.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T085950Z-codex-fa8347.yaml","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-sec2-pthr14430-20261106","number":6106,"title":"Review SCHPO sec2 in fungal Sec2 family","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6106"},{"body":"## Summary\n- review all yeast SEC2 GOA annotations, including the PTHR14430 IBA rows for the Opisthokonta Rab GEF node and fungal cell-tip node\n- cache/read the SEC2 primary literature, plus newer 2022/2023 Sec2-relevant papers found during manual search\n- remove generic Sec4 protein-binding rows in favor of exact Sec4 GEF activity, keep PI4P binding/autophagy/prospore-membrane roles as supported non-core activities, and synthesize the post-Golgi Sec2-Sec4 exocytosis core function\n- start the PTHR14430 tranche in the fungal PAINT project ledger and add append-only history records\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate yeast SEC2\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render yeast SEC2\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/yeast/SEC2/2026-11-06T084345Z-codex-89fc8d.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T084345Z-codex-93f35d.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/yeast-SEC2-pthr14430-20261106","number":6104,"title":"Review yeast SEC2 PTHR14430 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6104"},{"body":"## Summary\n- review C. albicans PHM5/PPN1 against the fungal PTHR10340/PTN000818947 PAINT node\n- accept the conserved endopolyphosphatase, exopolyphosphatase, fungal-type vacuole, and polyphosphate-catabolism IBAs\n- add the CGD/PMID:35575514 genetics evidence, fetched GOA/UniProt/publication cache, project ledger update, and append-only history records\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate CANAL PPN1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/CANAL/PPN1/2026-11-06T082105Z-codex-d8972e.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T082105Z-codex-1e40b8.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/CANAL-PPN1-pthr10340-20261106","number":6102,"title":"Review CANAL PPN1 PTHR10340 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6102"},{"body":"## Summary\n- add a de-novo NEUCR/epp-1 review from the Q9P3S1 GOA/UniProt seed\n- accept the four PTN000818947 fungal Ppn1 PAINT transfers and exact UniProt endopolyphosphatase inferences\n- refine generic vacuole and membrane rows to fungal-type vacuole or fungal-type vacuole membrane\n- record manual literature-search notes, project ledger updates, and append-only history records\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate NEUCR epp-1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/NEUCR/epp-1/2026-11-06T081554Z-codex-ae5c97.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T081554Z-codex-8c1dcf.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/NEUCR-epp-1-pthr10340-20261106","number":6101,"title":"Review NEUCR epp-1 PTHR10340 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6101"},{"body":"Current head `9797c5d2f381212eb10f6487190ea8ec050577fe` integrates main and 37 source qualifiers in five reviews. Every other parsed review field and row order is preserved. The original-payload checkers now verify those exact recorded additions without changing their baselines; both preservation checks and 65 capacity cases pass. Five focused validations/renders/histories pass. The project snapshot matches its ledger, and DsbAB completion remains backed by 201 verified merge blobs. Historical full-validation limits remain scoped to the recorded heads. Fresh review and CI are required.\n\nBacterial chemotaxis was represented chiefly by a Pseudomonas relay, with broadly named protein families obscuring distinct receptor, adaptation, reset and motor implementations. This audit retains Bacteria scope and models those mechanisms explicitly, using 37 curated references and 46 completed gene reviews across E. coli, P. putida, B. subtilis, H. pylori, S. meliloti and two Vibrio species.\n\nThe module now distinguishes cytoplasmic from transmembrane receptors, CheW from CheV coupling, motor-output CheY from the CheY1 phosphate sink, noncatalytic CheC/CheD feedback from receptor deamidation, and FliN/FliY switches and proton/sodium stators. CheZ, CheC, FliY, CheX and phosphate drainage can coexist. Optional adaptation and acceleration describe basal machinery coverage; they do not predict normal native behavior after deletion. Strain transfers and source defects are documented in the individual reviews.\n\nPreservation checks retain all 431 machine-sourced annotation payloads, 92 raw UniProt/GOA files and 22 inherited provider artifacts. One inherited broad signaling proposal was refined and one uninformative protein-binding proposal retired, with exact archives. The audit adds 29 NEW rows. All module exemplars have completed reviews and matching session provenance.\n\nValidation: focused gene, family, module and history checks pass. The production compiler plus an explicit complex-aware oracle passes 65 positive/negative capacity cases, requiring every complex unit and distinguishing individual activity from contribution to an assembled activity. Full-root cases use KT2440; other taxa primarily supply component/subcircuit checks, and one synthetic FliY C-ring case does not claim cross-species assembly. Four module advisories remain documented: an unconfigured Pfam label namespace and three family-scope warnings that cannot express the CheA IRD exclusion or mixed FliN/FliY architecture in the current scope vocabulary.\n\nFull-corpus validation finished across 10,559 reviews: schema, ontology and best-practices have zero blocking errors, and all 54 pathway files with PMIDs pass. The command exits 1 solely for 19 reference errors in 17 unrelated yeast reviews. The committed failure inventory proves all 23 review/source/configuration inputs are unchanged from the audit base; no chemotaxis member causes a blocking error. There are 115 ontology label advisories, 52 reference advisories and 27,993 best-practice warnings across the repository.\n\nThe fresh full-corpus run on follow-up science commit 2471af88ef is now terminal and reproduces exactly the documented unrelated reference error set. External review approved that commit. The completion ledger records the log hash and unchanged-input proof; subsequent closure edits affect only validation and project metadata.\n\nFliY prediction follow-up: all nine predictions were assessed against primary evidence. Flagellum assembly and swarming are CNN, with strain 3610 evidence and inference to reference 168 distinguished. The other seven assessments and all raw prediction fields remain unchanged. Prediction validation passed with 19 verified quotes; all 49 SFT regression tests passed. The gene review and module science remain unchanged, so the existing full-corpus result still applies to them.\n\nAdditional CI fix: moved the CheA ontology audit YAML out of recursive module discovery and updated three notes links. All 779 ModuleReview schema inputs pass; relocation bytes and scientific inputs are unchanged. Exact proof is committed in ontology-artifact-relocation-proof.yaml.\n\n\nLatest main integration preserves all 349 scoped scientific/source/audit files. Published main `577501df51` is integrated and the independently verified DsbAB completion row is retained; the ledger counts are regenerated from its 777 rows. Validation implementation is unchanged. The U1 ledger correction and U2 proof link remain in place. Renewed review and CI are required; the historical unrelated-reference-error qualification above still applies. Proof: `analysis/module_audits/bacterial_chemotaxis_signal_transduction/validated-head-main6-integration.json`.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-chemotaxis-taxon-audit","number":6100,"title":"Audit bacterial chemotaxis across receptor, reset and motor architectures","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6100"},{"body":"## Summary\n- create the SCHPO/ppn1 PTHR10340 review from PomBase GOA, UniProt Q9C1W8, cached PMID:16823372, and the completed fungal Ppn1 PAINT reviews\n- accept the four fungal PTN000818947 PAINT IBAs for endopolyphosphatase activity, exopolyphosphatase activity, fungal-type vacuole, and polyphosphate catabolism\n- refine generic vacuole, vacuolar membrane, membrane, hydrolase, and energy-reserve rows; keep cytoplasm and fungal vacuole membrane as non-core\n- mark SCHPO/ppn1 drafted in the fungal PAINT project ledger and add append-only history records\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO ppn1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render SCHPO ppn1`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/ppn1/2026-11-06T075821Z-codex-649d0d.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T075821Z-codex-159daf.yaml`\n","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-ppn1-pthr10340-20261106","number":6099,"title":"Review SCHPO ppn1 PTHR10340 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6099"},{"body":"## Summary\n- create the C. glabrata PPN1 gene review from GOA, UniProt, the completed yeast PPN1 review, and the PTHR10340 PAINT/family review\n- accept conserved fungal Ppn1 endopolyphosphatase, exopolyphosphatase, fungal-type vacuole, and polyphosphate-catabolism transfers from PTN000818947\n- keep vacuole membrane and cytosol as non-core, leave the inferred nuclear localization undecided, and remove stale TreeGrafter GO:0008081 in agreement with the 2026 PAINT IRD\n- update/render the fungal PAINT family ledger and add append-only history\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate CANGA PPN1\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/CANGA/PPN1/2026-11-06T074855Z-codex-d8fd99.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-06T074855Z-codex-fe5928.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES","headRefName":"codex/CANGA-PPN1-pthr10340-20261106","number":6098,"title":"Review CANGA PPN1 IBA assignments","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6098"},{"body":"## Summary\n- create the ASPFU erg6 review from freshly fetched UniProt/GOA data\n- align the three Erg6 PTHR44068 IBA assignments with the curated family review\n- use the full-text 2024 A. fumigatus Erg6 paper plus the 2025 RttA sterol-regulation paper\n- remove over-propagated mitochondrial localization, accept direct lipid-droplet localization, and refine broad InterPro/UniPathway parents\n- add an append-only history record and rendered HTML\n\n## Validation\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate ASPFU erg6\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/erg6/2026-11-06T071035Z-claude-code-7e8f92.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-families\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render ASPFU erg6\n","headRefName":"codex/ASPFU-erg6-pthr44068-20261106","number":6096,"title":"Review ASPFU erg6 IBA assignments","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6096"},{"body":"## Summary\n- align SCHPO erg6 PAINT IBA acceptances with the curated PTHR44068 family review from #6092\n- add explicit NO_FAILURE_CORE propagation reviews for the sterol 24-C-methyltransferase, ER, and ergosterol-biosynthesis IBAs\n- refresh the SCHPO erg6 UniProt cache and record the 2026 newer-literature search\n- render the updated SCHPO erg6 HTML and add append-only history\n\n## Stacking\n- Stacked on #6092 so this PR can use the shared PTHR44068 family review while keeping this commit gene-specific.\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate SCHPO erg6`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/erg6/2026-11-06T070426Z-codex-d4279c.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render SCHPO erg6`","headRefName":"codex/SCHPO-erg6-pthr44068-20261106","number":6095,"title":"Review SCHPO erg6 IBA assignments","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6095"},{"body":"## Summary\n- align yeast ERG6 PAINT IBA acceptances with the curated PTHR44068 family review from #6092\n- add explicit NO_FAILURE_CORE propagation reviews for the sterol 24-C-methyltransferase, ER, and ergosterol-biosynthesis IBAs\n- refresh the yeast ERG6 UniProt cache and record the 2026 newer-literature search\n- add PMID:40267201 as low-relevance 2025 context for erg6Delta sterol-intermediate behavior\n- render the updated yeast ERG6 HTML and add append-only history\n\n## Stacking\n- Stacked on #6092 so this PR can use the shared PTHR44068 family review while keeping this commit gene-specific.\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate yeast ERG6`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/yeast/ERG6/2026-11-06T070235Z-codex-3229aa.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render yeast ERG6`","headRefName":"codex/yeast-erg6-pthr44068-20261106","number":6094,"title":"Review yeast ERG6 IBA assignments","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6094"},{"body":"The carboxyspermidine module previously represented the two-step route using a narrow set of bacterial examples. This audit grounds the reaction steps and their connection in 14 curated references and reviews 18 gene products across Pseudomonadota, Campylobacterota, Bacteroidota and Bacillota. The two-step putrescine-to-spermidine route remains distinct from related diaminopropane-to-norspermidine and CAPA chemistry; family membership alone does not establish the reaction.\n\nThe expanded panel includes 13 reaction representatives and five comparators. Three uncertain upstream candidates deliberately have no asserted core function. Assayed strains and constructs are distinguished from reference accessions: the active Campylobacter 81116 decarboxylase construct differs from its canonical sequence, and a reproducible structure analysis identifies an unresolved N-terminal difference in the Helicobacter upstream candidate. PANTHER family reviews are reconciled with these substrate and evidence boundaries.\n\nAll 18 focused gene validations pass; all ten reviews changed after external review have been revalidated. The audit preserves 64 seeded annotation rows (including five inherited NEW rows), all 36 raw source files, and adds nine proposals. Module validation, family checks, evidence quotes, history records and 22 production compiler scenarios pass; the specific carboxyspermidine decarboxylase activity remains a proposed term because no appropriate GO molecular-function term is available. Full-corpus validation completed with 19 pre-existing reference errors in 17 unchanged yeast reviews; the affected review, source and validation-input bytes match the pre-audit base. Schema, ontology, best-practices and pathway checks passed. A committed provenance report records the failures; no full-corpus pass is claimed.\n\nExternal-review corrections distinguish measured substrate scope (dual-substrate Campylobacter, carboxyspermidine-only Bacteroides evidence), retain homodimerization as non-core, and record the Helicobacter suspected start-site issue and Campylobacter source defect in structured fields. Eight isomer-specific tetraamine BP proposals were withdrawn: native tetraamine observations support a plausible route, while native isomers and locus-specific flux remain unresolved. The demonstrated molecular activities are retained.\n\nLatest main integration: `7eee074bca991ff31c7ec235110dd6c93ebb2ece` is integrated at `d1ed61b0625ff80b214e0aff50fcf45209c3561b`, preserving all 192 approved nonshared files. The tracker now synchronizes every current integration field and records the three intervening approvals plus the latest metadata-only change request. Four previously verified completion rows remain preserved. Project history and rendering pass; no new full-corpus pass is claimed. Fresh review and CI gate actual merge. [Integration proof](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/blob/f99bd51e261ca33835a26db1c8e5e673afd71fb0/analysis/module_audits/bacterial_carboxyspermidine_biosynthesis/approved-main10-integration.json).\n","headRefName":"cmungall/aigr-carboxyspermidine-taxon-audit","number":6089,"title":"Audit carboxyspermidine biosynthesis across bacterial lineages","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6089"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- create a Candida albicans `ERG13` review for PTHR43323 / HMG-CoA synthase\n- accept the inherited `GO:0004421` HMG-CoA synthase and fungal `GO:0006696` ergosterol-biosynthesis PAINT assignments\n- keep broad acetyl-CoA/FPP context as non-core and mark CGD screen-derived filamentation/starvation/xenobiotic IMP rows as over-annotations\n- add the rendered review and append-only history record\n\n## Validation\n\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate CANAL ERG13`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-references genes/CANAL/ERG13/ERG13-ai-review.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-gene-pmids CANAL ERG13`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/CANAL/ERG13/2026-11-06T042502Z-claude-code-8df6c4.yaml`\n- `UV_FROZEN=1 just render CANAL ERG13`\n- `git diff --check`\n","headRefName":"codex/CANAL-erg13-pthr43323-20261106","number":6059,"title":"Review CANAL ERG13 PTHR43323 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6059"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- review all 22 seeded CANGA EAF3 GOA rows for the PTHR10880 fungal Eaf3/MRG family pass\n- align NuA4 and Rpd3S calls with the cached PAINT IBD nodes while treating the C. glabrata rows as EnsemblFungi/UniProt transfers from S. cerevisiae evidence\n- add review notes documenting the absence of newer direct C. glabrata/Nakaseomyces EAF3 literature and keep the rDNA/RNA-stability transfer unresolved\n\n## Validation\n\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate CANGA EAF3\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-references genes/CANGA/EAF3/EAF3-ai-review.yaml\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just render CANGA EAF3\n- UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/CANGA/EAF3/2026-11-06T030033Z-codex-c7e6bc.yaml\n- git diff --check","headRefName":"codex/CANGA-EAF3-pthr10880-20261106","number":6049,"title":"Review CANGA EAF3 PTHR10880 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6049"},{"body":"## Summary\n- create the initial ASPFU pptA review for PTHR12215/SF10\n- accept the PTN000259224 holo-carrier-protein activity and PTN000954835 fungal lysine-biosynthesis IBA calls\n- add a conservative NEW protein phosphopantetheinylation row and refine the broad pigment row to DHN-melanin biosynthesis\n- retain direct carrier-domain natural-product rows for TAFC, DHN-melanin, fumiquinazoline C, fumigaclavine C, fumagillin, and gliotoxin\n- cache PMID:15719355, PMID:21195204, PMID:22344643, and PMID:41498629\n\n## Validation\n- just validate ASPFU pptA\n- just validate-references genes/ASPFU/pptA/pptA-ai-review.yaml\n- just paint-losses ASPFU pptA\n- just render ASPFU pptA\n- just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/pptA/2026-11-06T015641Z-claude-code-75cd5b.yaml","headRefName":"codex/ASPFU-pptA-pthr12215-20261106","number":6043,"title":"Review ASPFU pptA PTHR12215 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6043"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nModule, family-review, GO-CAM-review, rule and project quotes were mostly never checked against their sources. This PR adds one checker for all of them and runs it on every PR. It fixes the 31 failing quotes in modules and family reviews, and lists the 222 ARBA rule-review failures in a baseline that can only shrink.\n\nWhat was unchecked before:\n\n| Kind | Gap |\n|---|---|\n| Modules | Only `EvidenceItem.source_id` quotes citing a PMID or DOI were read. `file:`/`Reactome:` quotes (the gap in #6021), plus every `reference_id` quote (`knowledge_gaps[].provenance`, `supported_by`) and every `references[].findings` quote, passed whatever they cited. |\n| Family reviews | ~6,000 `file:` quotes. The external reference validator lists `file` under `skip_prefixes`. |\n| GO-CAM reviews | Schema validation only. |\n| Rules, projects | No quote check. |\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`src/ai_gene_review/validation/quote_check.py`**: finds every `supporting_text`, whether its source is named by `source_id`, `reference_id`, `reference` or the enclosing `references[].id`.\n  - PMID/DOI quotes are checked against `publications/` with the shared `SupportingTextValidator`; `file:`/`Reactome:` quotes against the file on disk with `local_source_text`. The normalisation is the same as for gene reviews.\n  - A quote whose source has no readable text (`url`, `GO:`, `PMCID:`, no reference id) is reported as unchecked, never as verified.\n- **`module_validator.validate_supporting_text`** now uses it for every slot. `validate-families` and `validate-gocam-review` also run it.\n- **CI: `just validate-quotes` runs on every PR, not only when matching paths change.** A quote breaks when its *source* changes, and the source is usually a different file. Merging `main` into this branch two days after it opened broke 9 more family quotes, which shows the problem.\n- **`conf/quote_check_baseline.yaml`**: 222 known ARBA rule-review failures, which warn instead of block. Each is filed under its outcome:\n  - `not_found` (138): the source is readable and the quote is wrong, so it can be fixed now.\n  - `unresolved` (84): the source is missing. These are mostly gitignored `*-analysis.yaml` / `*.enriched.json` outputs (`rules/.gitignore`).\n  - A baselined entry only covers its own outcome. The file only shrinks: `--prune-baseline` drops passing entries, and `--init-baseline` refuses a partial scope or an existing file.\n- **Fixed quotes:**\n  - 2 module knowledge-gap quotes paraphrased their source.\n  - 29 family-review quotes quoted older versions of gene reviews.\n  - Each now quotes a verbatim sentence from the current source.\n- **Family assessments brought in line with current gene-review verdicts:**\n  - Reason text corrected: PTHR10336, PTHR11358, PTHR19443, PTHR22760, PTHR23359 and PTHR31829.\n  - PTHR23429 PTN000604427: `UNRESOLVED` → `SOUND`, because every reviewed recipient retains the term.\n  - PTHR31559 PTN000774355: `SOUND` → `UNRESOLVED`, because no reviewed recipient keeps the pyridoxine term.\n- Removes a stray duplicate review tree, `rules/arba/ARBA00022722/rules/`, which was committed at a doubled path in #5962 with a conflicting verdict.\n- Commits `publications/PMID_25248657.md`, which an ARBA review cites but which was never cached.\n- History records for every edited module and family review, and for the ARBA00022722 cleanup. CLAUDE.md now describes the check.\n\nItem 5 of #6021, other YAML kinds:\n- **Prediction reviews** were already covered by `prediction_evidence`, and are included in the census.\n- **Gene reviews** are left to `validator.py`, which already checks them.\n- **dismech** fails on every unreadable prefix except the 6 MetaboLights quotes in `Parkinsons_Disease`. Those match their cache files; un-skipping the prefix properly needs a MetaboLights fetcher.\n\nCloses #6021\n\n## Validation\n\n- `just validate-quotes`: 19,064 verified; 222 baselined (138 not found, 84 missing source); 0 blocking; 223 unchecked; 2,909 files; 101 s locally.\n- Module validator over all modules: 0 errors.\n- Family reviews: 9,586 quotes verified, 0 failed, including the Pfam reviews. Schema validation passes for every edited family file.\n- All new history records pass `just validate-history`.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `uv run pytest tests/test_quote_check.py tests/test_module_validator.py tests/test_local_source_text.py` (integration tests included)\n- [x] ruff, mypy and doctests clean on the changed modules\n- [ ] CI: `validation`, including the new `Validate supporting_text quotes (whole repo)` step\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBY8HCbniXEUEVK1frgT","headRefName":"strict-quote-validation-6021","number":6041,"title":"Check every supporting_text quote outside gene reviews; fix 31 stale quotes","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6041"},{"body":"## Summary\n- create a de novo `EMENI/rmt2` review for the PTHR32379 RMT2/GAMT fungal branch\n- accept the fungal PTN001285370 `GO:0019702` protein arginine N5-methyltransferase IBA and the broad nucleus/cytoplasm localization transfers\n- mark the InterPro `GO:0016274` mapping as `TOO_GENERAL` and the Ensembl translational-fidelity process transfer as `MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED`\n- cache the S. cerevisiae RMT2 biochemical/translation papers used to ground the fungal seed and downstream phenotype\n- add append-only history for the new review\n\n## Validation\n- `just validate EMENI rmt2`\n- `just validate-references genes/EMENI/rmt2/rmt2-ai-review.yaml`\n- `just validate-history history/genes/EMENI/rmt2/2026-11-06T013647Z-codex-5b01bc.yaml`\n- `just paint-losses EMENI rmt2`\n- `just render EMENI rmt2`\n\nNotes: automated deep research could not run because Falcon and the Perplexity fallback were unavailable in this runner; `rmt2-notes.md` records that and the manual Q5B058/AN6072 literature search.\n","headRefName":"codex/EMENI-rmt2-pthr32379-20261106","number":6040,"title":"Review EMENI rmt2 PTHR32379 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6040"},{"body":"## Summary\n- reviews the A. fumigatus rmt2 / AFUA_2G09080 GOA seed in PTHR32379\n- accepts fungal-node protein arginine N5-methyltransferase activity and the broad inherited nucleus/cytoplasm localizations\n- marks the InterPro GO:0016274 row as a true but redundant parent and the Ensembl translational-fidelity transfer as over-annotated for Aspergillus\n- records the lack of direct A. fumigatus rmt2 literature in notes and adds cached S. cerevisiae seed PMIDs\n\n## Validation\n- `just validate ASPFU rmt2`\n- `just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/rmt2/2026-11-06T012709Z-claude-code-61346b.yaml`\n- `just paint-losses ASPFU rmt2`\n- `just render ASPFU rmt2`\n- annotation-reviewer sidecar pass","headRefName":"codex/ASPFU-rmt2-pthr32379-20261106","number":6039,"title":"Review ASPFU rmt2 PTHR32379 annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6039"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- **Batch 1 of the migration deferred from #5984**: every source defect whose fix is an InterPro2GO mapping change. **85 `resource` values across 75 merged gene reviews**, plus one history record per target.\n- **The migration is not mechanical, and #5984's body was wrong to call it that.** Each defect needs a test applied; keyword matching on \"InterPro\" gets it wrong, and did for three of the 81 candidates.\n- **It also closes a split that was already in the corpus**: InterPro2GO defects were being filed *both* `INTERPRO` and `GOA_EBI`, so one class of defect was reaching two different curators.\n\nNo tracking issue exists for this work, so there is no `Closes` line.\n\n### The test, and where it comes from\n\nI did not invent the rule. `SourceResourceEnum`'s own `INTERPRO` description, added in #5984, sets it:\n\n> InterPro, including the InterPro2GO mappings behind `GO_REF:0000002` and the signature descriptions those mappings are drawn from. **The fix is to the entry's GO mapping or its description, not to any gene's annotation.**\n\nApplied per defect, that reassigns 83 to `INTERPRO` and sends three elsewhere:\n\n| review | → | why keyword triage was wrong |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GLUOX/pstC` | `GOC` | The defect is `GO:0005315` asserted `is_a GO:0015291`. Its own explanation already says \"Gene Ontology (ontology editors, not an annotation source)\". |\n| `human/C5` | `PINC` | Explanation opens \"Assigned by PINC (TAS, PMID:10820279)\". InterPro appears only in the supporting argument about anaphylatoxin vs chemokine folds. |\n| `human/ELAC2` | `INTERPRO` | Not a mapping defect at all — the wrong PMID is in InterPro's own entry description. The \"**or its description**\" half of the enum description is what covers it. |\n\n### The split that was already there\n\nSeven InterPro2GO defects were filed `INTERPRO`, but **`ARATH/NFYA3`, `NFYA5`, `NFYA7` and `MVP1` were filed `GOA_EBI`**. Migrating `OTHER → INTERPRO` without looking would have deepened that split rather than closing it — the same contradictory-routing problem that #5984 hit on CACAO, at corpus scale. All four ask for the mapping itself to change (\"Please consider `contributes_to` for this mapping\"), so they move to `INTERPRO`.\n\n**`worm/dpm-1` stays `GOA_EBI`, and the distinction is the point.** It asks for \"a minimum-coverage requirement on the catalytic domain before InterPro2GO transfer\" — the transfer pipeline, not the mapping's content. `GOA_EBI` is GOA's own curation; a mapping GOA merely materialises is not.\n\n### The qualifier class was not my call\n\nTwenty-six rows ask for `contributes_to` on a mapping InterPro2GO cannot qualify at all. Two explanations already work the problem through and land on InterPro:\n\n- `GLUOX/gdh` — \"Because the mapping file cannot carry a non-default qualifier, the alternatives are to accept the limitation, to restrict the mapping to the soluble members, or to drop the component mapping … **The resource is InterPro**\"\n- `human/LALBA` — \"if InterPro2GO cannot carry a `contributes_to` relation, **the mapping should be withdrawn**\"\n\nSo the corpus settled this already and the `NFYA` trio is the outlier, not the precedent. **The alternative reading — that the relation is GOA's to emit, since the pipeline supplies it — is defensible, and is yours to overrule.** If you prefer `GOA_EBI` for this class, it is a one-line change to the decision table and a re-run.\n\n## Validation\n\n```\n✓ All changed files passed validation.      # just validate-changed, all 75, strict, exit 0\n```\n\nExporter re-run, before → after:\n\n```\nOTHER      193 -> 112   (-81)\nINTERPRO     7 ->  90   (+83)\nGOA_EBI      8 ->   4    (-4)\nGOC          0 ->   1    (+1)\nPINC         1 ->   2    (+1)\ntotal     1548 -> 1548          # nothing dropped\n```\n\nInterPro is now the **second-largest external defect source** after UniProt (440).\n\n`linkml-validate` on all 75 new history records — No issues found. `test_history_schema.py` + `test_export_source_defects.py` — 52 passed.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] **The edit cross-checks itself per file**: the k-th textual `resource: <FROM>` line must correspond to the k-th parsed defect with that resource, and any file where the two enumerations disagree is skipped rather than edited on a guess. **75 files, 0 skipped, 85 flips**, matching the decision table exactly.\n- [x] **Only `resource` lines changed**: the diff is 85 insertions and 85 deletions, and `git show -U0` has **zero** changed lines in `genes/` that are not a `resource:` line. `defect_type`, `requested_change`, `slot`, `asserted_value`, `corrected_value`, `cause`, `confidence`, `explanation` and `supported_by` are untouched.\n- [x] Per-resource counts measured before and after, not predicted; total conserved\n- [x] All 81 candidate explanations read individually — this is what caught the three above\n- [x] One history record per target, scaffolded with `new_history.py`, per `docs/history.md` (\"for each curated target a PR touches, it writes one record\")\n- [x] 14 lines of `cache/` ontology-lookup churn from running validation locally reverted, not carried in\n- [x] Generated reports confirmed git-ignored and absent from the diff\n- [ ] **Reviewer decision:** the qualifier class — `INTERPRO` (as filed here, per the corpus's own explanations) or `GOA_EBI` (the pipeline reading)\n- [ ] **Reviewer check:** `human/ELAC2` is a description defect rather than a mapping defect; it rides the same enum value, which may or may not be what you want\n\n## What is left, and one correction to #5984's figures\n\n**112 `OTHER` defects remain.** By keyword: CACAO 13, Complex Portal 9, Ensembl 6, CollecTF 5, ENZYME 4, PseudoCAP 3, DisProt 2, CAFA 2, UniProt 2, HPA 2, RGD 2, NCBI 1, GOC 1; 15 multi-candidate; **45 that name no resource at all.**\n\nThose 45 are the concrete form of the floor caveat I put on #5984: the original sweep keyed on defects that *stated* a reason (\"the schema has no enum value, so `OTHER` is used\"), so defects that stated none were invisible to it. They cannot be triaged by keyword and need their explanations read, which is the real cost of the remaining migration.\n\nAlso worth noting for whoever does batch 2: the `slot` values in this class are inconsistent — `interpro2go`, `InterPro2GO`, `InterPro2GO mapping`, `GO_ID`, `GO ID`, `Qualifier`, `qualifier`, `term`. Normalising them is a separate change from re-filing `resource` and is deliberately not in this PR.\n\n---\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBY8VbV7JqUW9kcoaMRB)_","headRefName":"source-defect-other-migration","number":6027,"title":"Route InterPro2GO source defects to InterPro, and close a pre-existing filing split","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6027"},{"body":"Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBY9EZdngS6ipaoYBuCd","headRefName":"pseudocap-tier2","number":6019,"title":"PseudoCAP: Sha full-text wording, wzm general-term rows refined","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6019"},{"body":"Stacked on ai4curation/ai-gene-review#6004 (the base branch); merge that first. These reviews use its `UNRESOLVED_COMPARTMENT` and `NONADJACENT_COMPARTMENTS` QC flags and refer to its modules.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Eleven complete `GoCamReview`s.** Each activity has a verdict, QC flags, consistency with the gene review, and verbatim evidence; errors in the source models are recorded as structured `source_defects`. The eleven are the two motivating cases (`59cc772c00000000` IL-1β, `600ced8500002343` BMP9), two models whose receptor → nucleus edges the new modules explain (`66e382fb00001232` BMP4, `5f46c3b700002685` IL-6 trans-signaling), and the seven top review leads from `projects/GOCAM_COMPARTMENTS.md`: S. pombe ER-to-Golgi transport and mitochondrial import, the two mouse gluconeogenesis models, SARS-CoV-2 entry, RNF115/TLR transport, and ZDHHC4/MAVS.\n- **Compartment findings were treated as leads.** Each flagged edge was checked against the cited evidence. About a third pointed to a real problem:\n  - missing steps: IL-1β release; the Emp43 hand-over of luminal Ssp120;\n  - merged entry routes in the SARS-CoV-2 model;\n  - wrong locations: Tim50 placed in the matrix; GDI1 placed in the ER.\n  The rest were dismissed as coarse locations, contact-site biology, or reasonable simplifications. The project page gains a table of these outcomes.\n- **Notable source defects found along the way:**\n  - Sec23 (a GAP) drawn as inhibiting Sar1;\n  - TOLLIP drawn as activating IRAK1;\n  - an \"ACAT1\" node that is really SOAT1 (a symbol collision);\n  - ISO with/from IDs pointing to the wrong yeast genes;\n  - several references that look copied from other models.\n  Where the cached paper is abstract-only, defects are marked PROBABLE or SUSPECTED with a CHECK request rather than asserted.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `linkml-validate -C GoCamReview`: no issues on all 11 reviews.\n- `just validate-history`: no issues on all 12 new records (11 GO-CAM, 1 project).\n- Every PMID, Reactome and `file:` UniProt `supporting_text` was checked as a whitespace-normalized verbatim substring of the cached source: 199 quotes, 0 mismatches. (`validate-gocam-review` checks only the schema, so this check was run separately.)\n- 83 newly cached `publications/PMID_*.md` files that the reviews cite are included.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] Schema validation of each review\n- [x] History record validation\n- [x] Verbatim quote check over all reviews\n- [x] Project page renders\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBYAJcjefzBqdctMjRhp)_","headRefName":"claude/nice-archimedes-4k6xo8-gocam-reviews","number":6007,"title":"Review eleven GO-CAMs flagged by the compartment check","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6007"},{"body":"family_residue_validator crashed with KeyError: 'sequence' when a cited\naccession had been deleted from UniProtKB (C0H4F3 in PTHR21340), stopping\nvalidation of every family. SequenceCache.get now raises InactiveEntryError\nfor an inactive record; the anchor, control and gene/finding residue checks\nreport it as a FAIL naming the reason, and the remaining checks still run.\n\nPTHR21340: replace the deleted P. falciparum C0H4F3 (representative member\nand nudix_catalytic_glutamates positive control) with its P. reichenowi\northolog A0A060RPH8 (SF0, 148/152 identical, E64/E68 kept). The PAINT seed\nstays as PANTHER lists it.\n\nFixes #6003\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBY8NSckVMTahZfKJpAF","headRefName":"fix-inactive-uniprot-6003","number":6005,"title":"Report inactive UniProt entries as FAILs in residue validation (#6003)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6005"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- **Compartment check for GO-CAMs** (`just gocam-compartments`): a disjoint spatial compartment model with adjacency rules (`src/ai_gene_review/data/gocam_compartments.yaml`). Each activity's `occurs_in` is resolved through is_a/part_of. Locations that reach no compartment are flagged (e.g. chromatin, which GO does not place in the nucleus), as are causal edges between compartments that do not touch (e.g. CASP1 in the cytosol directly regulating extracellular IL1B). Two new `GoCamQcFlagEnum` values record confirmed findings: `UNRESOLVED_COMPARTMENT` and `NONADJACENT_COMPARTMENTS`. Findings are leads for reviews, never pre-filled flags.\n- **Modules as explanations.** A plasma-membrane receptor kinase or JAK \"directly regulating\" a nuclear SMAD or STAT is a GO-CAM convention: the release, SMAD4/STAT pairing and nuclear import steps are left out. A new reusable `karyopherin_nuclear_protein_import` module states the import cycle. `jak_stat_signaling`, `bmp_signaling` and `tgfb_smad_signaling` now include it by `module_ref`; BMP also gains the R-SMAD/SMAD4 complex step. GO-CAM activities at both ends of each edge are pinned with `gocam_associations`. The checker reports an edge as `EXPLAINED` only when a pinned module path passes through a `TRANSPORT_STEP` whose declared locations (including those of any `module_ref`'d module) touch both ends of the edge. It reports the path and the compartments spanned; a refused explanation is kept with `spans_edge: false`. 62 of 76 direct receptor → nucleus edges are now explained.\n- **Regulation vs material flow.** The check separates the two. `RELOCATING` covers direct regulation of a protein whose curated locations (GOA CC rows in its gene folder, minus ones its review REMOVEs) sit next to the regulator. The remaining leads (54 direct edges in 33 models, mostly metabolite transfer with no transporter) are tabulated in `projects/GOCAM_COMPARTMENTS.md`, with the full report beside it.\n\nAlso included: gocam-curation and module-curation skill guidance, a modules README section, history records, and seven newly cached publications cited by the modules.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `just validate-history` passes on the new records. The four modules pass `linkml-validate -C ModuleReview`, and the module validator reports no errors (only pre-existing PANTHER-cache and coverage warnings).\n- One behaviour change: in `jak_stat_signaling` the Boolean rule for `stat_transcription` now depends on `stat_nuclear_import`, because the old dimerization/import node is now a composite of two steps. One scenario expectation and one assertion in `tests/test_module_dynamics.py` were updated to match. This is an intended model change, not a loosened test. The frozen Boolean conformance fixture and the module's `.bnet` were regenerated to match (`build_boolean_conformance.py --check` passes).\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `pytest tests/test_gocam_compartments.py tests/test_gocam_module_bridges.py` plus doctests\n- [x] `pytest tests/test_module*.py tests/test_gocam*.py tests/test_boolean*.py tests/test_project_frontmatter.py tests/test_project_counterparts.py` (1934 passed)\n- [x] `ruff check`, `ruff format`, `mypy` on the new modules\n- [x] Full-corpus run over 2,036 cached models (report committed)\n- [x] Project page and the four modules render\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBYAJcjefzBqdctMjRhp)_","headRefName":"claude/nice-archimedes-4k6xo8","number":6004,"title":"GO-CAM compartment QC, and modules that explain compartment jumps","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/6004"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- The CYP17A1 review already proposes a new `steroid 17,20-lyase activity` molecular function, on the grounds that GO:0004508 is defined as 17α-hydroxylation only (RHEA:65760) and GO has no term for the C17–C20 cleavage reaction. That proposal rested on reaction identity (EC 1.14.14.32, RHEA:50244/14753 via PMID:40340376) and substrate-preference structures (PMID:25301938).\n- This PR adds a third, different kind of support: a paper that assays both reactions separately on the same mutant protein, normalized to enzyme abundance, and finds residual lyase activity exceeds residual hydroxylase activity in **all seven** variants tested. Single substitutions perturb the two reactions to different degrees, which argues for separability more directly than reaction identity does.\n- **The source is a preprint and is not peer reviewed** (`DOI:10.20944/preprints202610.2735.v1`, preprints.org, 28 Oct 2026). It is cited alongside the two peer-reviewed references in both places, never in place of them, and the `reference_review` says so explicitly.\n\nNo annotation action changed and nothing is marked `NEW`. The paper characterizes variant alleles; it does not identify a wild-type function GOA lacks, and variant residual-activity measurements are not grounds for a new process or function term on the gene.\n\nTouched: `references` (new entry, three findings), the 17,20-lyase `core_functions` entry, the `proposed_new_terms` entry, and `CYP17A1-notes.md`. Plus the newly cached `publications/DOI_10.20944_preprints202610.2735.v1.md`.\n\nOne caveat is recorded in both the notes and the `core_functions` explanation, because it is easy to over-read the result: the lyase assay supplies 17α-hydroxypregnenolone directly and so bypasses the upstream hydroxylation step. For severe alleles the authors argue the hydroxylase step is the in vivo bottleneck, so measurable lyase capacity does not imply sex-steroid output in patients. The dissociation is a claim about the enzyme's two catalytic steps, not about pathway flux.\n\nAlso flagged in the notes for a later pass, not acted on: the paper measures R449H as retaining more lyase than hydroxylase activity, which is the opposite of what selective loss of cytochrome b5 stimulation would predict. If that survives peer review it bears on how this review describes b5-dependence of the lyase reaction.\n\n## Validation\n\n```\njust validate human CYP17A1\nValidating genes/human/CYP17A1/CYP17A1-ai-review.yaml...\n✓ Valid (with 2 warnings)\n...\n✓ All validations passed for human/CYP17A1\n```\n\nBoth warnings are the `UV_NATIVE_TLS` deprecation notice being captured as warning text, not content findings.\n\nThe cached record is abstract-only and a forced re-fetch returned identical bytes (`full_text_declined: landing_page_only`), so every `supporting_text` is an abstract sentence. The PDF's tables — per-variant residual activities, the predictor score matrix — are not quotable and were kept out of the YAML; where the notes draw on them, they say so instead of quoting.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `just validate human CYP17A1` passes\n- [x] Cached publication generated by the fetcher (`linkml-reference-validator cache reference`), not hand-written\n- [x] All four `supporting_text` values confirmed verbatim substrings of the cached record\n- [x] `references[].title` matches the cached frontmatter title exactly\n- [ ] Reviewer check: is a non-peer-reviewed preprint acceptable as corroborating support here? If not, the reference entry and the two `supported_by` citations can come out and the notes entry stands on its own.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBY8uyvrczDXYCvk1RAc","headRefName":"claude/exciting-fermat-7qap9d","number":5987,"title":"Cite CYP17A1 variant enzymology preprint for lyase/hydroxylase separability","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5987"},{"body":"Stacked on [ai4curation/ai-gene-review#5983](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5983), the ThiI gene reviews this family review links to. The diff shown is only the family review. Retarget to `main` once #5983 merges.\n\n## Summary\n\n- New family review, `interpro/panther/PTHR43209/PTHR43209-review.yaml` (NEEDS_REVIEW), with a history record.\n- **Tree structure.** The PANTHER tree (25 reference-proteome leaves, from treeinfo) splits into a bacterial clade (PTN001804955) and an archaeal/eukaryotic clade (PTN001804942). The C-terminal rhodanese module, together with the thiazole signature IPR026340, occurs in exactly one subclade: **PTN000769419**, the six γ-proteobacteria (E. coli, Salmonella, Yersinia, Vibrio, Shewanella, Pseudomonas).\n- **PAINT node assessments:**\n  - **TOO_DEEP:** thiazole biosynthetic process at the root PTN001804941. Rhodanese-less ThiI is not required for thiamine in B. subtilis (PMID:22773787) or in archaeal M. maripaludis (PMID:22904325). The rhodanese domain is sufficient for thiazole in Salmonella (PMID:21724998). Recommendation: move the IBD to PTN000769419; the seed Q9HU66 is a leaf of that node.\n  - **SOUND:** s4U biosynthesis and sulfurtransferase activity at the root.\n  - **UNRESOLVED:** sulfurtransferase complex at the root, because many archaea lack a cysteine desulfurase.\n  - **UNRESOLVED:** PTN000382878, a PTHR14911 methyltransferase node. It enters this family's PAINT slice through MJ0421 (Q57864), explained below.\n- **Residue sites.** All anchored on E. coli P77718 v1 and checked against the real sequences:\n\n  | Site | Residues | Strength | Controls |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | `pp_loop_adenylation` | D189, K321 | REQUIRED for GO:0140741 | positive: E. coli, B. subtilis D189, Shewanella D191; negative: MJ0421 |\n  | `rhodanese_persulfide` | C456, C344 | CONTRIBUTES (essential only in the rhodanese clade) | positive: E. coli C456, Shewanella C458 |\n  | `rhodanese_domain` | domain presence | REQUIRED for thiazole | negative: B. subtilis, M. jannaschii |\n\n- **Term scope.** Thiazole is scoped **RESIDUE_DETERMINED** by `rhodanese_domain`. This is a workaround: PTHR43209 has a single subfamily, SF1, and the schema has no slot for scope conditioned on a domain or clade. GO:0004810 (CCA-adding) is NOT_APPLICABLE; it is the InterPro2GO IPR020536 error ([geneontology/go-annotation#6646](https://github.com/geneontology/go-annotation/issues/6646)).\n- **MJ0421 (Q57864) is a member exception.** It is a THUMP–SPOUT methylase with no PP-loop domain. UniProt's PANTHER cross-reference puts it in PTHR43209, but PANTHER's own tree places it in PTHR14911. It also receives sulfurtransferase activity and tRNA thio-modification from InterPro2GO IPR003720. This is not filed upstream yet.\n\n## Validation\n\nRun on this branch:\n\n- `linkml-validate` (family schema): passes.\n- Term validator: All validations passed.\n- Supporting-text reference check: ✅ PTHR43209-review.yaml (911 files).\n- `family_residue_validator interpro/panther/PTHR43209/PTHR43209-review.yaml`: 37 pass, 0 fail, 3 unresolved. The unresolved ones are negative controls that lack the domain entirely, so there is no residue position to check.\n- `family_gene_crosscheck`: no conflicts.\n- `just validate-history`: No issues found.\n\n**Pre-existing, not from this PR:** the full `just validate-families` currently crashes in the residue step with `KeyError: 'sequence'`. A control accession in `interpro/panther/PTHR21340/PTHR21340-review.yaml`, `UniProtKB:C0H4F3`, has been deleted from UniProtKB, so its JSON has no sequence. That needs either a fix in that review or the validator to tolerate inactive entries; it is left for a separate PR.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [ ] `uv run python -m ai_gene_review.validation.family_residue_validator interpro/panther/PTHR43209/PTHR43209-review.yaml`\n- [ ] Check the PTN000769419 clade membership with `ai_gene_review.etl.panther_trees`\n- [ ] Review the RESIDUE_DETERMINED workaround for the domain-conditioned thiazole scope\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBY9ZTgjhvQ6vd63JoXM)_","headRefName":"claude/pthr43209-thii-family","number":5985,"title":"Add PTHR43209 (ThiI) family review: rhodanese module decides the thiazole role","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5985"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- New reviews of ThiI, tRNA-uracil-4 sulfurtransferase (EC 2.8.1.4, RHEA:24176), in three organisms:\n  - `genes/ECOLI/thiI` (P77718): 27 GOA rows, experimentally characterized.\n  - `genes/BACSU/thiI` (O34595): the rhodanese-less Firmicute form.\n  - `genes/SHESA/thiI` (A0KZA4): homology only.\n- **InterPro2GO error.** IPR020536 \"ThiI, AANH domain\" is mapped to GO:0004810 CCA tRNA nucleotidyltransferase activity. All three reviews remove that row and record a `source_defects` entry (`record: InterPro:IPR020536`, `slot: interpro2go`, REPOINT to GO:0140741, `report_status: REPORTED`) pointing at [geneontology/go-annotation#6646](https://github.com/geneontology/go-annotation/issues/6646). All 287 Swiss-Prot entries carrying RHEA:24176 get the CCA term from this mapping alone. The existing `genes/PSEPK/thiI` review already removes the same row.\n- **The thiamine role depends on the rhodanese domain.**\n  - B. subtilis ThiI lacks the rhodanese domain, and its knockout abolishes s4U but not thiamine synthesis (PMID:22773787). Its thiazole row (IBA) and thiamine diphosphate row (UniRule) are therefore removed.\n  - These removals carry source defects against PAINT node PTN001804941, UniRule UR000075655, and two sentences of the UniProt FUNCTION text (the ThiS role, and IscS as donor; B. subtilis uses NifZ).\n  - E. coli and Shewanella ThiI have the rhodanese domain and keep the role.\n- **Evidence from the papers.** PMID:15037613 (Lauhon et al. 2004) and the other ThiI papers are recorded as structured `findings`, with verbatim quotes checked against the cached abstracts.\n- **Other E. coli changes:**\n  - Detection of UV, L-cysteine catabolism and sulfur carrier activity are marked over-annotated: ThiI is not the actor in any of them.\n  - Bare protein binding is removed under the project policy.\n  - Sulfurtransferase rows are narrowed to GO:0140741, plus a proposed term for the ThiS sulfurtransfer reaction (RHEA:43340), which has no GO term.\n- No deep-research provider was available. The PubMed search is recorded in each `thiI-notes.md`.\n\nFound through the RHEA→GO completeness work (Swiss-Prot RHEA annotations vs native GO annotations).\n\n## Validation\n\nRun on this branch:\n\n```\njust validate ECOLI thiI   -> ✓ Valid (with 2 warnings); ✓ All validations passed\njust validate BACSU thiI   -> ✓ Valid (with 2 warnings); ✓ All validations passed\njust validate SHESA thiI   -> ✓ Valid (with 2 warnings); ✓ All validations passed\njust validate-history history/genes/{ECOLI,BACSU,SHESA}/thiI/*.yaml -> No issues found\n```\n\nThe two remaining warnings per gene come from the `UV_NATIVE_TLS` environment-variable deprecation in this environment, not from content.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [ ] `just validate ECOLI thiI`, `just validate BACSU thiI`, `just validate SHESA thiI`\n- [ ] Spot-check the BACSU thiazole/thiamine removals against PMID:22773787\n- [ ] Check that `just export-source-defects` lists the IPR020536 and UniRule UR000075655 defects\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHoBY9ZTgjhvQ6vd63JoXM)_","headRefName":"claude/thii-interpro-ipr020536","number":5983,"title":"Review ThiI (E. coli, B. subtilis, Shewanella): InterPro2GO IPR020536 defect and rhodanese-dependent thiamine role","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5983"},{"body":"## Summary\n- **Module promoted to COMPLETE and generalized** beyond *P. putida* KT2440. It now uses the experimentally characterized *E. coli* K-12 proteins as exemplars: QueF Q46920, Tgt P0A847, QueA P0A7F9 and QueG P39288. The *Thermotoga maritima* and *Acinetobacter baylyi* QueH proteins are kept for the separate, non-homologous final step. Scope stays CONCRETE, and the notes name KT2440 as one exemplar and *A. baylyi* as the organism with both routes.\n- **6 new COMPLETE gene reviews:** ECOLI queF, tgt, queA and queG, which are also the reference proteins for their families, plus THEMA queH and ACIAD queH. Their verdicts match the existing KT2440 reviews.\n- **5 new COMPLETE PANTHER family reviews:**\n  - PTHR34354 (QueF), PTHR30307 (QueA), PTHR30002 (QueG).\n  - PTHR46499 (Tgt). It records that ancestral node PTN002460924 was seeded by mammalian QTRT1, which current models place in PTHR43530, so bacterial Tgt gets no IBA for its own activity. It also records that SF2 is archaeosine synthase.\n  - PTHR36701 (QueH). This family has no annotated PAINT nodes.\n- **Knowledge gap moved to notes:** the ontology-only gap now sits in the notes, together with the GO:0008479 definition mismatch.\n- **History records** for all 12 targets.\n\n## Validation\n- `linkml-validate -C ModuleReview`: no issues. Recommended-field compliance is 100%, and gene reviews are 10/10 complete.\n- `module_validator` passes with 1 warning, FAMILY_SCOPE_RESTRICTED on `tgt_activity`. It is deliberate: PTHR46499:SF1 also holds archaeal tRNA-guanine(15) transglycosylases, so the family review scopes GO:0008479 SUBFAMILY_ONLY. Grounding the step at SF1 would overclaim, and the module notes explain this.\n- Full `just validate-modules`: exit 0, with no FAMILY_CONFLICT anywhere.\n- `validate-families`: the five families pass the residue-site checks, 55 pass and 0 fail, and the cross-check finds 0 conflicts. The full run stops only on the known crash on PTHR21340, which is already on `main` and cites a UniProt entry that has since been deleted.\n- `pytest tests/test_treegrafter_rereview_outcomes.py tests/test_panther_families.py tests/test_merge_group_ci.py`: pass.\n- Cache: only rows are added, for the two new taxa. The files are sorted (`cache_lint`).\n\n## Test plan\n- [ ] CI is green.\n- [ ] Check that GO:0008616 is kept rather than its de novo child GO:0160253, because these steps also serve salvage.\n- [ ] Check the QueH verdict. It rests on in vivo complementation and the *A. baylyi* double-mutant phenotype; that the product has not been detected in vitro is recorded as a biology knowledge gap.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_016p6NCPLmxKu9HCtbWVsUDT)_","headRefName":"claude/pensive-darwin-e7gi2m-preq1-queuosine-maturation","number":5981,"title":"Generalize bacterial preQ1 incorporation and queuosine maturation module and promote to COMPLETE","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5981"},{"body":"## Summary\n- **Module generalized.** `modules/bacterial_nitrogen_regulatory_pts.yaml` now covers Pseudomonadota, because PTS(Ntr) is the proteobacterial paralogue of the sugar PTS (PMID:23517463). Each step has two exemplars: *E. coli* K-12 for the enzymology and the KdpD/TrkA outputs, and *P. putida* KT2440 for the in vivo relay order and the KdpD/AceE outputs. Status is COMPLETE.\n- **Groundings.**\n  - The #5076 PAINT nodes are adopted for PtsP (PTN000532509) and PtsN (PTN002021052).\n  - The NPr step is grounded at `PTHR33705:SF2` rather than the bare family, because SF2 holds both NPr exemplars and the family review gives GO:0016775 only to SF1 and SF2. The notes explain that SF2 also holds Firmicute HPr.\n  - The NPr function is now GO:0016775 phosphotransferase activity, nitrogenous group as acceptor, which replaces the more general GO:0016772.\n- **Knowledge gaps.** The two ontology-only gaps moved to the notes. One biology gap remains: whether the effect of PtsN on KdpD has the same sign across lineages.\n- **New PANTHER family reviews:** PTHR46244 (PtsP/EI-Ntr), PTHR33705 (HPr/NPr) and PTHR47738 (EIIA-Ntr). Each has subfamilies, PAINT node assessments and term scopes. The node verdicts are:\n\n  | Node | Term | Verdict |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | PTN000532509 | GO:0008965 | SOUND |\n  | PTN000532509 | GO:0015764 | TOO_DEEP |\n  | PTN002135312 | GO:0009401 | NEEDS_PRUNING (the NPr clade inherits sugar-PTS terms) |\n  | PTN002021052 | GO:0030295 | TOO_DEEP (the activator sign is contradicted by *P. putida* repression) |\n\n- **Gene reviews.** New COMPLETE reviews for *E. coli* ptsP, ptsO and ptsN; *P. putida* ptsP, ptsH and ptsN completed. Diagnosed errors are recorded as `source_defects`: sugar-PTS InterPro2GO/UniRule mappings, PAINT over-propagations, the TrkA enzyme-inhibitor term and the TreeGrafter activator term on *P. putida* PtsN.\n- **History records** for every target.\n\n## Validation\n- `linkml-validate -C ModuleReview`: no issues.\n- `module_validator`: 0 warnings. Recommended-field compliance is 100%, and gene reviews are 6/6 complete.\n- Full `just validate-modules`: exit 0, with no errors and no FAMILY_CONFLICT.\n- `just validate` is clean for all six genes.\n- `validate-families`: the schema, term and quote steps and the cross-check pass. The three new families pass their residue and node checks. The full run stops only on the known crash on PTHR21340, which is already on `main` and cites a UniProt entry that has since been deleted.\n- `pytest tests/test_treegrafter_rereview_outcomes.py tests/test_panther_families.py tests/test_merge_group_ci.py`: 81 passed.\n- The cache files are sorted (`cache_lint`). The only cache change is one added row, for GO:0016775.\n\n## Test plan\n- [ ] CI validation is green.\n- [ ] Review the GO:0016775 choice for the NPr phosphocarrier.\n- [ ] Review the PAINT node verdicts.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_016p6NCPLmxKu9HCtbWVsUDT)_","headRefName":"claude/pensive-darwin-e7gi2m-nitrogen-regulatory-pts","number":5978,"title":"Promote nitrogen-regulatory PTS module to COMPLETE with PTS(Ntr) family and gene reviews","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5978"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- Full reviews of every GOA row for the human gene products in `modules/calmodulin_camkii_stat1_potentiation.yaml` (#5904): CAMK2B, CAMK2D, CAMK2G and PTK2B (Pyk2). Each includes a description, core functions, a notes file recording the literature search, a rendered page and a history record.\n- **CAMK2G:** NEW IDA row for serine phosphorylation of STAT protein (GO:0042501, PMID:11972023). That paper identifies CaMKII γ/δ as the STAT1 kinases in IFN-γ signalling, which supports the module and the GO-CAM 62b4ffe300004489 review: the model should name CAMK2G/CAMK2D, not CAMK2A. The γ-specific Ca²⁺/CaM nuclear shuttle to CREB is a core function.\n- **CAMK2B:** NEW rows for actin filament binding and actin filament bundle assembly. These cover the β subunit's kinase-independent role in synaptic structural plasticity.\n- **CAMK2D:** cardiac roles covered: RyR2 regulation, SR Ca²⁺ release and relaxation, and HDAC4 nuclear export.\n- **PTK2B:**\n  - The ISS calcium/calmodulin-dependent Ser/Thr kinase row (GO:0004683, from rat) is removed. Pyk2 is a tyrosine kinase (EC 2.7.10.2) that Ca²⁺ activates only indirectly.\n  - PMID:8497321 is marked MISCITED; it describes an SH3-containing Cdc42-binding kinase (ACK).\n  - No interferon/STAT1 term is asserted, because the only evidence is from an inhibitor. This is recorded as a BIOLOGY knowledge gap, consistent with the module's optional Pyk2 relay.\n- **Protein binding:** bare protein-binding rows are removed under the current policy; paralog and SRC-family partners become kinase binding terms. The older CAMK2A review still keeps many such rows as non-core and would need migrating to match.\n- The fetched sources and newly cached publications these reviews cite are included.\n\nThe bacterial OspC1/OspC3 effectors from the same module will come in a separate PR with the Van proteins. No tracking issues exist for these genes.\n\n## Validation\n\n`just validate human <GENE>` passes with no errors for all four. The remaining warnings are non-blocking best-practice notes, such as two PTK2B core-function processes held as KEEP_AS_NON_CORE, plus the environment's `UV_NATIVE_TLS` notice. Each history record passes `just validate-history`. Before committing, I checked that no row was still PENDING and that each review's `id` matches its UniProt record.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [ ] CI validation passes for the four gene reviews\n- [ ] Spot-check the CAMK2G STAT1 NEW row against PMID:11972023\n- [ ] Spot-check the PTK2B GO:0004683 removal\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01VjkEithCA39Mn75X8xMQrd)_","headRefName":"claude/vibrant-gauss-3g6g1j-calmodulin-gene-reviews","number":5945,"title":"Gene reviews for the calmodulin-CaMKII STAT1 module (CAMK2B, CAMK2D, CAMK2G, PTK2B)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5945"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- Full reviews of every GOA row for five IL-1 pathway genes, with descriptions, core functions, notes recording the literature search, rendered pages and history records. These are the gene products that `modules/il1_signaling.yaml` and GO-CAM 59cc772c00000000 use (both in #5904).\n- **IRAK1** keeps two core functions:\n  - signaling adaptor activity in IL-1/MyD88 signalling, because kinase-dead IRAK1 (K239A) fully restores IL-1 responses (PMID:10373513);\n  - Ser/Thr kinase activity, which the TLR7/9 → IRF7 branch needs (PMID:15767370).\n  \n  This answers the IRAK1 knowledge gap in the IL-1 module. **IRAK2** is typed as a signaling adaptor; it has no kinase row, and its one phosphorylation row is marked over-annotated.\n- ComplexPortal propagates process rows (e.g. negative regulation of IL-1 signalling) from IL1R2/IL1RN decoy and antagonist complexes onto the ligand and receptors those complexes capture. These rows are removed on IL1A, IL1R1 and IL1RAP. The schema has no ComplexPortal source-defect resource, so the reason is given in each row and in `suggested_questions`.\n- **IL1B is not included.** Its review stopped partway when a safety filter withheld the agent's output; I did not retry it. It can be reviewed separately.\n- The fetched sources these reviews cite are included: `*-uniprot.txt`, `*-goa.tsv`, 39 newly cached publications and one Reactome entry.\n\nNo tracking issues exist for these genes.\n\n## Validation\n\n`just validate human <GENE>` passes with no errors for IL1A, IL1R1, IL1RAP, IRAK1 and IRAK2; the only warnings are the environment's `UV_NATIVE_TLS` deprecation notice. Each history record passes `just validate-history`. Before committing, I checked that no row was still PENDING and that every review's `id` matches its UniProt record.\n\nNo deep-research provider keys were available, so each `-notes.md` records the sources checked instead of a `-deep-research-*.md` file.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [ ] CI validation passes for the five gene reviews\n- [ ] Spot-check IRAK1's two core functions against PMID:10373513 and PMID:15767370\n- [ ] Spot-check the ComplexPortal decoy-complex removals on IL1A\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01VjkEithCA39Mn75X8xMQrd)_","headRefName":"claude/vibrant-gauss-3g6g1j-il1-gene-reviews","number":5941,"title":"Gene reviews for the IL-1 signaling module: IL1A, IL1R1, IL1RAP, IRAK1, IRAK2","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5941"},{"body":"First review for human **TREX1** (Q9NSU2), from the [cGAS-STING project](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/blob/main/projects/CGAS_STING_PATHWAY.md) queue in #3955. TREX1 was the first unchecked item there and the pathway's principal negative regulator: everything upstream of CGAS in that cohort was already reviewed, so this closes the sensor-restraint side.\n\n**Why this issue.** No `high-priority`/`low-priority` labels or reaction signals separated the candidates, so I kept the pre-scan queue order and took the first item with no open PR already attached. #3951-#3954 at the head of the queue are each covered by open PRs (EIF2S1 alone has three), so working them would have duplicated in-flight work; #3955 had none. Within it, ENPP1 is already reviewed and `modules/cgas_sting_signaling.yaml` exists, leaving TREX1, SAMHD1, IRF7 and IFNB1.\n\n## What's here\n\n- `genes/human/TREX1/TREX1-ai-review.yaml` — all **56** GOA rows reviewed, no `PENDING`. `just validate human TREX1` is clean.\n- `genes/human/TREX1/TREX1-notes.md` — my own literature synthesis with inline provenance. `just deep-research human TREX1 --provider perplexity` failed in this environment (\"Provider 'perplexity' not available. Available: claude_code\"), so per CLAUDE.md this is a `-notes.md`, deliberately **not** named `-deep-research-*`.\n- Cached publications for 14 PMIDs plus two Reactome entries and PANTHER family PTHR13058.\n\nThree core functions: the Mg(2+)-dependent 3'→5' DNA exonuclease; ER-tethered negative regulation of cGAS activation at ruptured micronuclei; and the DNase-independent C-terminal restraint of the oligosaccharyltransferase complex — the genotype split that separates AGS1/chilblain lupus (nuclease-dead) from RVCL (C-terminal frameshift, enzyme still active).\n\n## Findings worth a look\n\n**An ATRIP annotation sitting on TREX1.** GOA carries two MGI IDA rows from PMID:16713580 — `GO:0032405` MutLalpha complex binding and `GO:0032407` MutSalpha complex binding. That paper is about MutSα/MutLα-dependent recruitment of **ATR-ATRIP** and never mentions TREX1. Three checks point to ATRIP rather than to a curator reading I can't see:\n\n1. UniProt's own `CC:CAUTION` on Q9NSU2: \"The gene for this protein is either identical to or adjacent to that of ATRIP. Some of the mRNAs that encode ATRIP also encode TREX1 in another reading frame.\" — exactly the mechanism by which an ATRIP assertion lands here.\n2. QuickGO (2026-11-03) returns the paper twice for `GO:0032407` in human, on **ATR** and on **TREX1**. ATR is correct; human ATRIP (Q8WXE1) has **no** annotation from it, which is implausible for a study whose central claim is about the ATR-ATRIP complex.\n3. No literature places TREX1 in a MutSα or MutLα complex.\n\nBoth rows are `REMOVE`d with a `source_defects` entry (`WRONG_GENE_PRODUCT`, MGI, re-point `Q9NSU2` → `Q8WXE1`, `SEQUENCE_MISMAPPING`, confidence `PROBABLE` since I only had the abstract). This is the one place I'd most like a second opinion, as it is a `REMOVE` on experimental rows — the argument is about *which gene the row is attached to*, which is checkable without the full text, not about the experiment.\n\n**`GO:0008250` oligosaccharyltransferase complex is `part_of` but the evidence is binding.** PMID:26320659 (full text cached) shows co-IP with two subunits, DDOST and RPN1, and explicitly *no* detectable interaction with RPN2 or STT3A, via the TREX1 C-terminal tail. `MODIFY` → `GO:0062062` oligosaccharyltransferase complex binding, which exists and says what was shown. Worth keeping rather than dropping: this is the DNase-independent arm and the proposed mechanism of RVCL.\n\n**Three NAS rows from one sentence of intent.** `GO:0006260` DNA replication, `GO:0006298` mismatch repair and `GO:0006310` DNA recombination all trace to PMID:10391904's closing line — \"provides the genetic tools to investigate the physiological roles of these exonucleases in mammalian DNA replication, repair, and recombination pathways\". Marked over-annotated, not removed. Its `GO:0005635` nuclear envelope NAS row gets the same treatment: nothing in that paper addresses localisation, and the membrane pool is the ER membrane, continuous with the outer nuclear membrane.\n\n**`GO:0045824` is kept beside `GO:0160049`, not subsumed by it.** Tempting to treat the general innate-immunity term as redundant, but `GO:0160049` sits under `GO:0039532`/`GO:0045088` and `GO:0045824` is **not** among its ancestors (checked against QuickGO). Both are accepted as complementary, and the OST arm suppresses ISGs without any DNA sensing at all, so the broader term is not just a weaker restatement.\n\n**A UniProt topology query.** Q9NSU2 records the ER-membrane location as \"Peripheral membrane protein\" with no `TRANSMEM` feature, while PMID:33476576 — the very paper cited for that location — states TREX1 \"possesses a single-pass transmembrane helix at its C-terminus\". Recorded as a `SUSPECTED` source defect with `requested_change: CHECK` rather than asserted, since peripheral vs. integral anchoring isn't settled.\n\n**Ontology gap.** `proposed_new_terms` asks for a CC term for the mammalian micronucleus: `GO:0031040` is defined for ciliate germline nuclei, so neither TREX1's nor CGAS's localisation to ruptured micronuclei can currently be annotated.\n\n## Status and remaining work\n\nLeft at `status: DRAFT` rather than `COMPLETE` — it validates with zero warnings, so promote it when a reviewer is happy with the `REMOVE` calls above. Still open in #3955: SAMHD1, IRF7, IFNB1, and folding TREX1 into `modules/cgas_sting_signaling.yaml`.\n\nNo history record yet; I'll add one with this PR number in a follow-up commit.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","headRefName":"curation/cgas-sting-trex1","number":5937,"title":"Review human TREX1 (Q9NSU2), the cGAS-STING cytosolic DNA eraser (#3955)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5937"},{"body":"Builds `modules/integrated_stress_response.yaml` for #3951.\n\n### Why this item, and why the module rather than a gene\n\n#3951 was top of the scanner queue (least recently commented, no competing\neffort signals, no `high-priority`/`low-priority` label, no reactions on any\ncandidate). Its twelve gene-review checkboxes turn out to be fully accounted\nfor already: EIF2AK1, EIF2AK4, DELE1 and EIF2B1-EIF2B5 have merged reviews, and\nEIF2S1, EIF2AK2, DDIT3, PPP1R15A and PPP1R15B each have an open PR (#5901,\n#5732, #5722, #5821, #5855, #5747, #5779 — three competing branches for EIF2S1\nalone). Starting another gene review would have duplicated work in flight. The\nthirteenth checkbox — \"Build an ISR module including the OMA1-DELE1\nmitochondrial branch\" — had no PR, so that is what this does. The issue stays\nopen on its gene PRs.\n\n### Structure\n\nDRAFT, scope CONCRETE, context Mammalia. Five required parts plus one optional:\n\n1. **eIF2alpha kinase activation** — a `ROUTE` variant set over HRI/PKR/PERK/GCN2\n   with `ONE_OR_MORE` selection, because co-occurring stresses engage more than\n   one kinase. Declared `ROUTE` explicitly: all four carry GO:0004694, so the\n   `REALIZER` inference would otherwise fire, and what distinguishes the\n   branches is the input sensed. GCN1 is included as the ribosome-bound GCN2\n   activator.\n2. **The eIF2 hub** — the heterotrimer as a complex participant with\n   EIF2S1/EIF2S2/EIF2S3 active units, so the Ser51-bearing subunit stays visible\n   to module reasoning.\n3. **eIF2B inhibition** — the decamer as a complex participant with the catalytic\n   epsilon subunit and the alpha/beta/delta regulatory subcomplex.\n4. **ATF4 translational and transcriptional output.**\n5. *(optional)* **CHOP and ATF3.**\n6. **Feedback dephosphorylation** — PPP1CA plus a `REALIZER` variant set over the\n   GADD34 and CReP targeting subunits, `ONE_OR_MORE` because they differ in\n   timing (constitutive vs ISR-induced) rather than being interchangeable.\n\n### Composition, not duplication\n\nThe HRI branch takes `mitochondrial_upr#oma1_dele1_cleavage` and\n`mitochondrial_upr#dele1_hri_activation` by `module_ref` rather than\nre-authoring the OMA1 and DELE1 annotons. Both referencing parts are optional,\nsince heme limitation and cytosolic protein misfolding (PMID:31273097) activate\nHRI without DELE1. The two child nodes were referenced rather than the whole\n`mammalian_oma1_dele1_hri_route`, which also carries its own HRI and eIF2alpha\nannotons.\n\nThe PERK branch deliberately does **not** use a `module_ref`:\n`er_unfolded_protein_response#perk_arm` covers the same chemistry but carries\nits own eIF2alpha and ATF4 annotons, so including it would duplicate parts 2\nand 4. The relationship is recorded in prose on the PERK variant.\n\n### Two things recorded rather than papered over\n\n- **An ontology gap at the centre of the pathway.** GO has no molecular-function\n  term for the activity of phosphorylated eIF2 toward eIF2B — the mechanism that\n  makes the ISR work. The hub annoton therefore carries eIF2's ordinary\n  GO:0003743 and the inhibition is stated on the `eif2_complex -> eif2b_gef`\n  `NEGATIVELY_REGULATES` connection. Per the project rule this is a notes/ontology\n  matter, not a `knowledge_gaps` entry; the right place to propose the term is\n  whichever EIF2S1 review lands.\n- **Six participants without gene reviews** (EIF2S1, EIF2AK2, DDIT3, PPP1R15A,\n  PPP1R15B, EIF2S2), grounded on verified UniProt accessions plus production\n  GO-CAM activities, with each such annoton carrying a `notes:` field saying so.\n  That is curation work, not a knowledge gap, so it is tracked on the project\n  page instead.\n\nTwo genuine `BIOLOGY`/`BP_DARK` knowledge gaps are recorded: what sets the\nadaptive-versus-apoptotic threshold, and whether the four branches are\ninterchangeable routes to one mark or distinct pathways sharing a hub.\n\n### Also in this PR\n\n- `modules/integrated_stress_response-notes.md` — build record, boundary\n  decisions table, accession verification, and one citation trap worth knowing:\n  PMID:12882984 is **not** the Jousse CReP paper despite looking the part; the\n  CReP paper is PMID:14638860.\n- `projects/INTEGRATED_STRESS_RESPONSE.md` — the bottom line still said \"There is\n  no ISR module yet\" and \"Thirteen have no gene folder\"; refreshed with real\n  counts, a Module section, and a status checklist.\n- Four newly cached publications (PMID:11381086, 14638860, 15277680, 16179258).\n  All `supporting_text` values were extracted from the cached files rather than\n  typed, including the Unicode `eIF2α` forms.\n\n### Validation\n\n```\nlinkml-validate -C ModuleReview modules/integrated_stress_response.yaml   # No issues found\nmodule_validator modules/integrated_stress_response.yaml                  # passed, 0 warnings\nmodule_qc data_qc                                                         # 100% recommended-field compliance\n                                                                          # no leaf-grounding / conformance / module_ref violations\n                                                                          # feedback loop reported closed\nmodule_logic.compile_module_file                                          # 15 atom occurrences, 4 core atoms,\n                                                                          # both module_ref targets resolving\nrender-module, render-projects                                            # clean\nvalidate-history                                                          # No issues found\n```\n\n`mitochondrial_upr` and `er_unfolded_protein_response` were re-validated to\nconfirm the reference introduces no new warnings there (their existing warnings\nare unchanged and pre-existing).\n\nDraft because the module should probably be promoted past DRAFT only once the\nhub (EIF2S1) and feedback (PPP1R15A/B) reviews land and the annoton `notes:`\ndisclaimers can be replaced by `file:` evidence.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","headRefName":"curation/isr-module-3951","number":5931,"title":"Build the human integrated stress response module (#3951)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5931"},{"body":"Creates the human G3BP1 (Q13283) review for the [stress granule project](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/blob/main/projects/STRESS_GRANULES.md), #3953. That issue lists G3BP1 as one of two unreviewed core nucleators; it had no in-flight PR, and it is the stalest unassigned `curation` candidate whose next step was both well-defined and unclaimed (#3951 already has seven open PRs, #3952's module work is in #5752).\n\nAll 139 GOA rows adjudicated: 39 ACCEPT, 83 REMOVE, 8 KEEP_AS_NON_CORE, 6 MODIFY, 2 MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED, 1 TOO_GENERAL. Every term has exactly one action.\n\n## Core functions\n\n`molecular condensate scaffold activity` (GO:0140693). NTF2-mediated dimerisation plus the RG-rich region give G3BP1 the valence to crosslink unfolded, polysome-released mRNA into a networked condensate, while the acidic disordered region holds it autoinhibited until mRNA outcompetes the intramolecular contacts. The RG-rich region also recruits 40S subunits, and CAPRIN1/USP10 tune the threshold in opposite directions through one shared NTF2 pocket.\n\nA second core function records the same multivalent-clustering activity applied to cytosolic sensors: G3BP1 promotes large cGAS complexes and cGAS DNA binding, and binds RIG-I plus viral dsRNA via the RGG, raising type I interferon output.\n\n## The helicase annotations are removed\n\nAll 12 DNA/RNA helicase and ATP hydrolysis rows (GO:0003678, GO:0003724, GO:0033677, GO:0016887) trace to a single 1999 report, PMID:9889278, which purified an unwinding activity from HeLa nuclear extract and assigned it to G3BP by microsequencing the gel band. Microsequencing identifies which protein is in the band, not which carries the activity, and the result has not been reproduced in 27 years.\n\nA new reproducible analysis (`genes/human/G3BP1/G3BP1-bioinformatics/`, no API key needed, standard library only) makes the negative case checkable:\n\n* **Part 1 — sequence features.** Zero hits for Walker A/P-loop, Walker B, DExD/H box, SF2 motif VI, SF2 motif III and the Q motif; none of the entry's 19 domain signatures names a helicase or RecA/AAA/P-loop ATPase; no nucleotide-binding feature. The same test flags all four positive controls (DDX3X, EIF4A3, BLM, UPF1) and clears both fold-matched negative controls (NUTF2 for the NTF2 domain, HNRNPA1 for the RRM+RGG half), so G3BP1's architecture does not score positive by accident. Annotated domains tile all 466 residues, leaving no room for the two RecA-like lobes.\n* **Part 2 — family census.** QuickGO over all 11 reviewed members of PTHR10693: helicase rows occur in exactly one subfamily (SF21, the G3BP1 orthologs), and **Q13283 is the only member in any species with experimental evidence for any helicase term** — mouse, cattle and orangutan rows are all ISO/ISS/IBA/IEA/RCA. The paralogous SF10 subfamily (G3BP2) has none, nor do the fungal, nematode and plant members.\n\nAccordingly:\n\n* a `source_defects` entry asks UniProt to retire EC 3.6.4.12 and EC 3.6.4.13, the `ATP-dependent DNA helicase VIII` alternate name, and the helicase `CC FUNCTION` / `CATALYTIC ACTIVITY` / `COFACTOR` statements — these EC numbers are what generate the GO_REF:0000003 IEA rows;\n* a `propagation_review` on the three IBA rows records why node PTN002581351 cannot carry the inheritance claim. Per `projects/IBA_REVIEW.md` this is argued as node placement, and the target's self-reference in `WITH/FROM` is explicitly **not** treated as circular — the comment says so. The problem is that the node's only grounding is an annotation that is itself unsound.\n\n## Other decisions\n\n* `GO:0007265` Ras protein signal transduction → over-annotated. Binding the RASA1 SH3 domain is not relaying a Ras signal; the source paper says \"suggesting\", and UniProt now carries the interaction as By similarity only. Not removed, because HRAS-dependent dephosphorylation still gates granule recruitment.\n* `GO:0005925` focal adhesion → over-annotated. One HDA row from a 905-protein isolated-adhesion proteome whose authors state half their list may be non-specific contaminants.\n* `GO:0005737` cytoplasm → MODIFY to cytosol; `GO:0003723` RNA binding and `GO:0003676` nucleic acid binding → MODIFY to mRNA binding, since both HDA sources are poly(A)/oligo(dT)-selected interactome capture.\n* The four ComplexPortal rows (projected from CPX-21432 CAPRIN1-G3BP1 and CPX-26721 G3BP1-USP10) kept as non-core, except `GO:0140694` membraneless organelle assembly, which is TOO_GENERAL under the accepted `GO:0034063`. `GO:0016579` protein deubiquitination is kept rather than removed: the catalysis is USP10's, but G3BP1's RGG holds the complex on its 40S substrate, so `involved_in` survives the participation test. Noted that `GO:0004843` would be wrong for G3BP1.\n* All 71 bare `GO:0005515` rows removed under the protein-binding policy, each with the partner or screen named. 34 are SARS-CoV-2-era rows recording the viral N protein's capture of G3BP1.\n* **Not** annotated to `GO:0035617` stress granule disassembly: G3BP1 is the ubiquitinated substrate that FAF2/VCP extracts, not a participant. Also declined `GO:0004521` (single unreproduced endoribonuclease report, recorded as a question instead) and `GO:0003727` (redundant with mRNA binding).\n\n## Caveats for the reviewer\n\n* **No deep-research provider was reachable** in this environment: `--provider perplexity` reported \"Provider 'perplexity' not available\", and the claude_code provider reported \"Not logged in\". Per CLAUDE.md the research record is in `G3BP1-notes.md`, which says so explicitly and is not named as a provider file. A real deep-research file should be generated alongside it when a provider is available.\n* `GO:0034063`'s label is written as the current ontology label `stress granule assembly`, not GOA's `cytoplasmic stress granule assembly`, matching existing reviews (TIA1, TARDBP) and the term validator.\n* PMID:34469731 is marked `MISCITED`: its full text is about RNF10/USP10 and 40S and never mentions G3BP. That is recorded as a reference-level note, **not** as a wrong-gene source defect, since complex-level assertions are legitimately projected onto members.\n* Draft because the helicase removal is a deliberately bold call on a 27-year-old UniProt enzyme annotation with EC numbers attached, and because the `source_defects` entry is an upstream request. The bioinformatics evidence is reproducible, but a curator should sign off before it goes to UniProt.\n\nValidated with `just validate human G3BP1` (clean, no warnings), `validate-references` (244 snippets, 0 issues) and `validate-terms`. History record added under `history/genes/human/G3BP1/`.\n\nProgresses #3953.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","headRefName":"curation/sg-g3bp1","number":5916,"title":"Review human G3BP1, the core stress-granule nucleator","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5916"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- **Harvest.** New `projects/REGULATION_SIGN/sign_inversion_harvest.py` (+ YAML output) collects every `existing_annotations` row tagged `REGULATORY_SIGN_INVERSION` in `propagation_review`: 91 propagated rows in 64 reviews after merging main (ISO 40, IBA 23, IEA 17, ISS 10, IGI 1). Two shapes emerge:\n  - 62 signed terms carrying the wrong direction; the largest class is rodent ISO transfers.\n  - 29 unsigned process terms given to an inhibitor of the process; most are IBA over mixed families such as BCL2/CFLAR. This shape is added to the project as origin hypothesis 6.\n- **Source defects.** The 34 `SOURCE_BAD` / `PROPAGATION_BAD` rows still in current GOA that lacked one now carry `review.source_defects` (25 reviews), so `just export-source-defects` routes them upstream. `resource` is the group that **holds the defective record**, as rat Alpl already does for GO:0140928:\n  - A wrong donor annotation is filed against the donor's MOD with `record` set. That gives RGD 8 and MGI 5.\n  - A sound donor transferred to the wrong target, or an orphaned transfer, is filed against the transferring group: GO_CENTRAL 15, OTHER 3 (TreeGrafter, YuBioLab, named in the explanation), and ARUK_UCL, SGD and UNIPROT 1 each.\n  - Requested changes are CHECK 17 (all IBA rows, which PAINT fixes at the node), DELETE_ROW 10 and REPOINT 7.\n  - 50 of the 91 harvested rows now carry a defect. The rest are term-scoping, unresolved or weak-source cases, plus Candida CEK2, which arrived from main.\n  - No review actions, reasons or summaries changed.\n- **Small fix.** GNB1's GTPase TAS defect now names `PINC` (added to `SourceResourceEnum` since #5350) instead of `OTHER`.\n- **Project page** (`projects/REGULATION_SIGN.md`). Updated with the harvest findings, the routing convention, counts and checklist. The checklist gains follow-ups for ADIPOQ's and mouse Alpl's MGI origin rows and for the CEK2 row. A history record accompanies every edited review and each project-page edit.\n\nDeferred to a separate docs/schema PR: an `ENSEMBL` resource value, a sign-specific `defect_type`, and docs on `record` for donor rows and on `CHECK` with `corrected_value`.\n\n## Validation\n\n`just validate` passes for all 26 edited reviews: Cgas, ABCC8, ADIPOQ, AKAP12, ALPL, ARG2, CFLAR, EGLN1, GNAI2, GNB1, GOT1, MCL1, RGS2, SPRY2, Aldh7a1, Bnip3, Btk, Cacnb3, Cd48, Ghsr, Spr, sta-1, CDC55, FUN19, ROF1 and RPD3. All new history records pass `just validate-history`, and `tests/test_project_frontmatter.py` passes. `just export-source-defects` reads all 34 entries under the stated resources; its reports are generated and not committed.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `just validate <org> <gene>` for each edited review\n- [x] `just validate-history` for each new record\n- [x] `uv run pytest tests/test_project_frontmatter.py`\n- [x] `just export-source-defects` includes the new rows under the expected resources\n- [x] `uv run python projects/REGULATION_SIGN/sign_inversion_harvest.py genes` reproduces the committed YAML\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01SaREHoG5AgjiGG7FmHaKSj","headRefName":"regulation-sign-harvest","number":5905,"title":"REGULATION_SIGN: harvest sign-inversion rows and route their source defects upstream","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5905"},{"body":"Fill the GoCamReview for all 9 activities. Flags a sign error on the\nTOLLIP -> IRAK1 edge (cited paper and UniProt describe TOLLIP as\ninhibitory), generic kinase activity on IRAK4/IRAK1/TAK1, and an\nevidence-free IRAK1 -> TAK1 edge that omits TRAF6. Adds history record.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01VjkEithCA39Mn75X8xMQrd","headRefName":"claude/vibrant-gauss-3g6g1j","number":5904,"title":"Review GO-CAM 59cc772c00000000 (IL1beta-mediated signaling pathway)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5904"},{"body":"Progresses #3951 (*Complete integrated stress response reviews and module*) by\nreviewing the project's central missing candidate, **EIF2S1 / eIF2-alpha**\n(UniProtKB:P05198).\n\n### Why this item, and why this gene\n\n#3951 was the oldest unassigned `curation` issue in the high-effort queue with\nno comments at all, and no reactions or priority labels distinguished any of the\ntop candidates. Of its 13 unchecked gene boxes, 8 turned out to be already done\nthrough other work; EIF2S1 was the one the project page itself calls \"the hub\",\nit is a dependency of the planned ISR module, and it is also listed on #3953\n(stress granules), so one review unblocks the most downstream work.\n\n### The review\n\n102 GOA rows, all reviewed: **41 ACCEPT, 20 MODIFY, 20 REMOVE, 17\nKEEP_AS_NON_CORE, 4 MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED**. Three core functions:\n\n1. Contributes to cap-dependent initiation as part of the eIF2 heterotrimer\n   (`contributes_to_molecular_function`, since the activity belongs to the trimer).\n2. The Ser-52 phospho-switch that converts eIF2 from an eIF2B substrate into a\n   competitive eIF2B inhibitor — the ISR's convergence point.\n3. HRI-branch mitophagy: phospho-eIF2-alpha on the mitochondrial surface.\n\nPoints worth a reviewer's attention, all argued in\n`genes/human/EIF2S1/EIF2S1-notes.md`:\n\n- **Mitophagy is `ACCEPT`, not a necessity-only annotation.** The deciding\n  evidence is the gain of function: rapalog tethering of phosphomimetic\n  eIF2-alpha(S51D) to the outer membrane induces mitophagy with no stress\n  applied, while WT and S51A do not (PMID:38340717). The step contributed is\n  autophagosome recruitment, downstream of PINK1/PARKIN marking.\n- **Mitochondrion stays at the root organelle term.** Two direct lines plus an\n  ER-marker control support the organelle, but `GO:0005741` was *not* added:\n  no TM segment or presequence, and stress-dependent peripheral recruitment to\n  the cytosolic face is the case `projects/ORGANELLES.md` tells us not to call\n  an outer-membrane location.\n- **20 `MODIFY` rows are granularity refinements**, each to an `is_a`\n  descendant, and in all but one case to a term the gene already carries — e.g.\n  `GO:0003743` → `GO:0160296` (+`GO:0160297` on the IDA row that supports both),\n  `GO:0034976` → `GO:0036499` where GOA records the child from the same\n  reference. The exception is `GO:0006413` → `GO:0002183`, justified because\n  mitochondrial initiation uses MTIF2/MTIF3.\n- **The GCN2 branch asymmetry was deliberately *not* made a `NEW` row.** The\n  comparator check says it looks like a real gap (GO annotates `GO:0140468`\n  across the whole HRI chain including EIF2S1, and EIF2S1 carries `GO:0036499`,\n  yet `GO:0140469` has only EIF2AK4/GCN1/IMPACT/MAP3K20). But the human\n  GCN2→eIF2-alpha evidence is `By similarity` in UniProt and ISS/IEA in GOA, so\n  it is a `suggested_questions` entry with a matching experiment design instead.\n- **25 bare `GO:0005515` rows** follow the protein-binding policy: `REMOVE`,\n  except five `MODIFY`d to `GO:0019901` protein kinase binding (PKR ×3, PERK)\n  and `GO:0019903` protein phosphatase binding (PPP1CA) — the on- and\n  off-switches of the ISR.\n\n### Also in this PR\n\n- `projects/INTEGRATED_STRESS_RESPONSE.md`: the bottom line still said five\n  candidates had reviews; 15 of ~18 now do. Updated, with the four remaining\n  gene folders named (EIF2AK2, DDIT3, PPP1R15A, PPP1R15B) and the carry-over\n  findings for the module recorded.\n- History records for the gene and the project page.\n\n### A caveat and a loose end\n\n`just deep-research human EIF2S1 --provider perplexity` **failed** on the runner\n(`Provider 'perplexity' not available. Available: claude_code`; no `agentapi` on\nPATH), so there is no `-deep-research-*.md` file — per `CLAUDE.md` nothing was\nwritten under a provider name. The literature-search provenance is recorded in\n`EIF2S1-notes.md` instead, and the review is grounded in the UniProt record, the\n54 GOA references (full text read where cached), five references fetched here,\nthe PAINT table for PTHR10602, and QuickGO comparator queries.\n\nSeparately: UniProt's PTM block cites `PubMed:15489334` for \"Substrate for at\nleast 4 kinases\", but that PMID is the MGC full-length cDNA project paper and\nsays nothing about eIF2-alpha kinases. This looks mis-keyed, but it was **not**\nfiled as a `source_defects` entry because the correct replacement identifier\ncould not be established and a defect entry needs a `corrected_value`. Noted in\nthe gene notes for whoever can identify the intended reference.\n\n### Validation\n\n- `just validate human EIF2S1` — ✓ Valid, all validations passed\n- `just validate-references` — 85 snippets checked, **0 issues**, 33 titles checked\n- local-source quote check (UniProt/Reactome quotes) — 0 issues\n- `just backfill-record-sections` — 108 `record_section` values derived\n- `just validate-history` on both records — no issues\n- project page renders cleanly\n\nDraft, because `status: DRAFT` on the review and because the GCN2-branch\nquestion and the UniProt mis-citation are judgement calls a maintainer may want\nto settle differently.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","headRefName":"curation/eif2s1-isr","number":5901,"title":"Review human EIF2S1 (eIF2-alpha), the integrated stress response hub","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5901"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nThis PR adds comprehensive curation for the medium-chain acyl-CoA synthetase (ACSM) family, including:\n\n- **New gene reviews** for ACSM4 orthologs (human, mouse, rat) and related enzymes (B. subtilis ytcI, M. sedula Msed_0406)\n- **EC class consistency validator** (`ec_class_consistency.py`) that warns when a kept molecular-function GO term names a different EC class than the protein's UniProt record, indicating likely misannotation\n- **Project page** for proposed obsoletion of GO:0004321 (fatty-acyl-CoA synthase activity), which conflates fungal type I fatty acid synthase with acyl-CoA ligases\n- **Supporting infrastructure**: UniProt records, GOA annotations, bioinformatics analyses, and publication metadata for all reviewed genes\n\nThe EC class consistency check addresses a systematic annotation problem: GO:0004321 is defined by EC 2.3.1.86 (transferase class), but most annotations are to acyl-CoA ligases (EC 6.2.1.*, ligase class). This validator helps identify such mismatches during curation.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `just validate human ACSM4` ✓\n- `just validate mouse Acsm4` ✓\n- `just validate rat Acsm4` ✓\n- `just validate BACSU ytcI` ✓\n- `just validate METS5 Msed_0406` ✓\n- `just validate-all` passes with new reviews included\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] Unit tests added for EC class consistency validator (`tests/test_ec_class_consistency.py`)\n- [x] All gene reviews pass schema validation\n- [x] Project page renders correctly with usage summary and audit results\n- [x] History records created for all curation sessions\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01VjkEixAWJ3ApHK6GjDRVDN","headRefName":"claude/lucid-brahmagupta-ep1gba","number":5900,"title":"Add EC class consistency validation and ACSM4 gene reviews","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5900"},{"body":"## Summary\n- Add PANTHER FamilyReviews for all seven member families: PTHR44379 (NicA), PTHR47495 (NicB), PTHR13789 (NicC), PTHR34448 (NicX), PTHR43798 (NicD), PTHR43540 (NicF) and PTHR40267 (MaiA).\n  - PAINT nodes are assessed against the cached PANTHER 19.0 tree.\n  - NicD's TreeGrafter membrane annotation traces to PTN001661081. That node has a single mouse Abhd6 seed but spans the bacterial subfamilies, so it is assessed TOO_DEEP.\n- Drop the PANTHER subfamily ids for NicC, NicD and NicF, because those subfamilies mix unrelated activities. These roles are now grounded by representative members plus the required reaction.\n  - Subfamily ids are kept where they match the role: NicA, NicB, NicX, and MaiA (with the Bordetella AMDase recorded as an exception).\n- PSEPK nicD: the membrane IEA is marked MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED.\n- BORBR nicX had no GOA rows, so it could never count as complete. It gets a NEW GO:0047075 row, supported by the FASEB 2011 meeting abstract (metadata cached from OpenAlex, because Crossref does not resolve that DOI) and the IPR058739 signature.\n- The module stays CONCRETE and bounded to Pseudomonadota. The full route is experimentally supported only in Pseudomonas and Bordetella, and the notes say why. History records are added.\n\n## Validation\n- `linkml-validate -C ModuleReview`: no issues.\n- `module_validator`: passes, with warnings of two kinds:\n  - 8 NO_FUNCTION_ID warnings, because GO has no term for N-formylmaleamate deformylase or maleamate amidohydrolase activity. The gene reviews propose new terms for both.\n  - PANTHER member-index lookups, which fire only locally.\n- module_qc: 100% recommended-field compliance, 0 conformance violations, 9/9 gene reviews complete.\n- The family reviews pass the schema, term and reference checks. `family_gene_crosscheck` finds 0 conflicts.\n- `just validate` passes on the edited genes, and the history records are valid. The branch merges cleanly with `main`.\n\n## Test plan\n- [ ] Module and family CI is green.\n- [ ] Reconcile `PTHR13789-review.yaml` with open PR #5077, which adds a machine-drafted version of the same file, before the second of the two merges.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_016p6NCPLmxKu9HCtbWVsUDT)_","headRefName":"claude/pensive-darwin-e7gi2m-aerobic-nicotinate-degradation","number":5897,"title":"Complete aerobic nicotinate degradation module: nic family reviews and gene completeness","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5897"},{"body":"## Summary\n- review EMENI/mpkC GOA rows for the PTHR24055 fungal MAPK tranche, including PTN001172058 HOG/Sty1 IBAs\n- cache direct A. nidulans mpkC/SakA/PbsB literature and document the Falcon/Perplexity deep-research fallback failure in notes\n- add core MAPK function synthesis, append-only gene/project history records, and the FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES ledger entry\n\n## Validation\n- UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just validate EMENI mpkC\n- UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just render EMENI mpkC\n- UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES\n- UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/EMENI/mpkC/2026-11-02T013013Z-codex-1e6faa.yaml\n- UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-02T013013Z-codex-a09564.yaml","headRefName":"codex/emeni-mpkc-pthr24055-20261102","number":5891,"title":"Review EMENI mpkC MAPK annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5891"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- create a complete ASPFU/mpkC gene review for the PTHR24055 MAPK tranche\n- accept the fungal HOG/stress-MAPK PAINT placements, while keeping broad kinase imports as non-core or over-annotated\n- cache and adjudicate the MpkC/SakA literature, including the carbon-utilization, stress-translocation, transcriptome, interaction, and biofilm papers\n- render the mpkC review, add append-only gene/project history, and add ASPFU/mpkC to the Family 4 ledger\n\n## Validation\n\n- `just validate ASPFU mpkC`\n- `just render ASPFU mpkC`\n- `just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`\n- `just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/mpkC/2026-11-02T011812Z-codex-95496c.yaml`\n- `just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-02T011815Z-codex-af3c30.yaml`","headRefName":"codex/aspfu-mpkc-pthr24055-20261102","number":5889,"title":"Review ASPFU mpkC MAPK annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5889"},{"body":"## Summary\n- align SCHPO spk1 generic kinase and Rhea rows with the PTHR24055 MAPK review policy\n- tighten UniProt and InterPro support for MAP kinase / ATP-binding evidence and mark two unverifiable high-throughput localization rows UNDECIDED\n- add cached 2026 Sms1 scaffold context, append notes/history, and record SCHPO/spk1 in the fungal PAINT ledger\n\n## Validation\n- `just validate SCHPO spk1`\n- `just validate-history history/genes/SCHPO/spk1/2026-11-02T004718Z-codex-221f64.yaml`\n- `just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-02T004722Z-codex-d087de.yaml`\n- `just render SCHPO spk1`\n- `just render-project FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES`","headRefName":"codex/schpo-spk1-pthr24055-20261102","number":5886,"title":"Refresh SCHPO spk1 PTHR24055 review","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5886"},{"body":"## Summary\n- create the ASPFU/sakA PTHR24055 fungal HOG MAPK review\n- review all seeded SakA GOA rows, including the PTN000622075 broad MAPK and PTN001172058 fungal stress-MAPK IBA placements\n- fetch and cite direct SakA/MpkC literature, add a focused core MAP kinase function, and record the Falcon deep-research failure in notes\n- add SakA to the Family 4 fungal MAPK ledger and include matching history records\n\n## Validation\n- `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just validate ASPFU sakA`\n- `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/genes/ASPFU/sakA/2026-11-02T000655Z-claude-code-e7c41c.yaml`\n- `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/srv/home/cmungall/orca/workspaces/ai-gene-review/aigr-ros-yeast/.venv UV_FROZEN=1 just validate-history history/projects/FUNGAL_PAINT_FAMILIES/2026-11-02T000655Z-claude-code-8e39e8.yaml`","headRefName":"codex/aspfu-saka-pthr24055-20261101","number":5883,"title":"Review ASPFU sakA MAPK annotations","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5883"},{"body":"## Summary\n\nRecord completion of the fresh GO coverage comparison for all 18 assessed equivalent pairs and link the commit-pinned results in [uniprot-orphan-analysis](https://github.com/ai4curation/uniprot-orphan-analysis/pull/1).\n\nThe orphan accessions carry 48 non-IEA rows, including 41 experimental-code rows, without matching evidence on their counterparts. The linked analysis distinguishes shared assertions with different evidence from absent exact assertions, retains qualifier/context differences, and explains why these counts are not automatically missing functions. AVR9 and nas-8 provide complementary examples for future UniProt feedback.\n\nOnly the project page and its new curation history record change here. All raw responses, comparison code, and generated analysis tables remain in the separate analysis repository. No annotation transfers or external feedback were made.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Project history record passed `just validate-history`.\n- Existing counterpart reports passed `just project-counterparts --check`.\n- The linked analysis passed 64 tests, including offline report reproduction; all 18 live membership/taxonomy checks passed.\n- Pre-existing local GO-cache and report-formatting changes were preserved and excluded from this PR.\n","headRefName":"cmungall/orphan-go-coverage","number":5870,"title":"Record GO evidence differences on orphan equivalent records","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5870"},{"body":"## Summary\n\n- **Problem:** `SequenceCache` in `family_residue_validator.py` treated any UniProt fetch failure as fatal and never retried. A new gene review's own protein is never in the CI sequence cache, because merge-queue runs cannot read caches saved by PR runs. So during a UniProt outage, `just validate-families` fails for every PR that adds residue claims or residue mappings. This removed #5693 from the merge queue twice: H2KYP7 (pnk-1) returned HTTP 500, then 503.\n- **Retry:** transient failures are retried twice, after 2 s and 8 s. These are 5xx and 429 responses, timeouts, connection errors, and bodies cut off mid-transfer (`IncompleteRead`) or mid-JSON. A 4xx such as 404 means the accession is wrong; it is still a hard failure and is not retried. An accession that uses up its retries is not retried again in the same run.\n- **Fallback:** if UniProt is still unavailable, the UniProt record committed beside the gene review (`<GENE>-uniprot.txt`) is used for that review's primary accession. Both `gene_residue_claims` and `finding_residue_mappings` register it. The fallback is used only when:\n  - the record's sequence matches the length and CRC64 on its own `SQ` line, so a truncated or edited file cannot produce a confident wrong FAIL;\n  - `UNIPROT_FALLBACK` is not `0`. `main.yaml` sets it to `0` for the scheduled and manual full passes, which must reach UniProt.\n- **Making a fallback visible:** in Actions, each fallback is a `::warning::` annotation. The validators' summary lines name the accessions served from committed records.\n- **Unchanged:** fallback sequences are never written to the disk cache, so the next run with UniProt back fetches the live record. An accession with no committed record still fails, now as `UniProtUnavailable`, a `RuntimeError` subclass. Failures are still never turned into UNRESOLVED results.\n- **Why retries alone are not enough:** the outage that hit #5693 lasted over an hour. Retries cover a short blip; the committed-record fallback is what would have kept #5693 in the queue.\n\nOne existing assertion changes on purpose. `test_sequence_cache_persistence.py` pinned \"one request per accession, no retry\". It now counts the retry attempts, and an autouse fixture removes the backoff sleeps so the tests stay fast.\n\n## Validation\n\nn/a (no gene review content changed).\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `pytest` on `test_sequence_cache_persistence.py`, `test_gene_residue_claims.py`, `test_family_residue_validator.py` and `test_finding_residue_mappings.py`: 139 passed (124 on `main`).\n- [x] New tests cover:\n  - retry then success;\n  - 404 not retried, and the committed record not used;\n  - 500 or `IncompleteRead` throughout, falling back;\n  - no second retry cycle;\n  - a secondary accession not registered;\n  - truncated and wrong-checksum records rejected;\n  - `UNIPROT_FALLBACK=0`;\n  - CRC64 against UniProt's printed values;\n  - gene-level and finding-level outages.\n- [x] All 600 of a random sample of the 10,168 committed `-uniprot.txt` files on `main` parse as intact.\n- [x] End to end on `genes/worm/pnk-1`, with H2KYP7 uncached:\n  - unchanged `main`, network blocked: `RuntimeError: Could not retrieve UniProt record H2KYP7`;\n  - this branch, network blocked: 6 pass, plus the fallback note;\n  - this branch, network blocked with `UNIPROT_FALLBACK=0`: exit 1;\n  - this branch, live: 6 pass.\n- [x] `ruff check`, `mypy` and doctests pass on the changed files.\n- [ ] The `-m integration` tests in these files fail identically on `main` in my sandbox (VCR cassette replay mismatches). They are deselected in CI by `pytest.ini`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01XqHpFyQcY6sF2FwKqJSoTH","headRefName":"fix/uniprot-fetch-resilience","number":5860,"title":"Make UniProt sequence fetches survive outages in residue validation","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5860"},{"body":"Closes the gene queue in the [integrated stress response project](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/blob/main/projects/INTEGRATED_STRESS_RESPONSE.md), tracked in #3951.\n\n## Why this gene\n\n#3951 lists twelve genes to fetch and review. Eight now have reviews (EIF2AK1, EIF2AK3, EIF2AK4, DELE1, EIF2B1, EIF2B2, EIF2B3, EIF2B5) and four more are in flight: EIF2AK2 (#5821), PPP1R15B (#5779), PPP1R15A (#5747) and EIF2S1 (#5732 and #5722, which appear to be duplicates). **DDIT3 was the only one with neither a folder nor an open PR**, so it is the single piece of that issue a run can finish without colliding with existing work. The ISR module and the project-page status refresh are deliberately left out of this PR to avoid conflicting with the four open gene PRs.\n\n## What is here\n\n`just fetch-gene human DDIT3` produced 251 GOA rows; all are reviewed, none PENDING.\n\n| action | rows |\n|---|---|\n| REMOVE | 91 |\n| ACCEPT | 84 |\n| KEEP_AS_NON_CORE | 31 |\n| MODIFY | 25 |\n| MARK_AS_OVER_ANNOTATED | 12 |\n| TOO_GENERAL | 8 |\n\n**Core functions** (three): pol II transcription activator activity (`GO:0001228`, DR5 and TRIB3 promoters), transcription corepressor activity (`GO:0003714`, ASNS with ATF4), and leucine-zipper heterodimerization (`GO:0046982`) as the switch that selects between them. CHOP has no characterised monomeric activity, so partner choice is the organising fact of the review.\n\n**Judgements worth a look:**\n\n- **90 bare `GO:0005515` rows removed** (36% of the file), from coiled-coil arrays, Y2H and HuRI/BioPlex-scale screens. The informative replacements are all already annotated (`GO:0046982`, `GO:0043522`, `GO:0008140`, `GO:0061629`, `GO:0042803`, plus the three named dimer complexes).\n- **`GO:0043161` removed** (IDA, PMID:17872950). The paper maps the N-terminal determinant of CHOP's *own* proteasomal turnover; the row annotates CHOP as `involved_in` proteasome-mediated catabolism. Being consumed by a process is not participation in it. Recorded as a `WRONG_TERM` source defect against GOA_EBI; the turnover result itself is sound and is already a UniProt PTM statement.\n- **`GO:0005737` `is_active_in` (IBA)** kept as a location but flagged as a `WRONG_QUALIFIER` source defect against GO_CENTRAL: the cytoplasmic pool is real (UniProt, PMID:29083303), but CHOP's activity is nuclear.\n- **Five vascular rows over-annotated** (`GO:0060840`, `GO:1904738`, `GO:1990874`, `GO:0009611`, `GO:0036119`) plus `GO:0010467`. All six reach human by Ensembl Compara from mouse rows annotated out of one ENPP1-deficiency neointimal-hyperplasia paper (PMID:24530784) in which CHOP expression was a readout. `GO:0001955` is *kept* as non-core by contrast, because it rests on a dedicated mouse loss-of-function study with an identified mediator (eNOS, PMID:22265908).\n- **`GO:2000016` and `GO:0009948` over-annotated**: both derive from CHOP mRNA injection into Xenopus embryos (PMID:16434966). The informative claim from that paper is Wnt/TCF inhibition, kept as `GO:0090090`.\n- **`GO:0140537` kept, not removed.** It initially looked like a keyword artefact, but the mouse IDA behind it is PMID:10523647 (\"CHOP enhancement of gene transcription by interactions with Jun/Fos AP-1 complex proteins\") and PMID:18534616 reports the same direction of effect in human cells.\n- **`GO:0005770` late endosome** marked over-annotated rather than removed: the source is a single mouse IDA from an anthrax-toxin/MLN64 study, and the mouse full text was not read.\n\n**Two abstract-only rows** (PMID:33384352) were ACCEPTed rather than left UNDECIDED, with the limitation stated in `reason` and in `reference_review`: the cached record does not mention CHOP, but five other IDA rows establish the same claims and the curator read the full text.\n\n**Knowledge gaps** (two, both wet-lab): what selects the adaptive versus terminal outcome of CHOP induction, and which bZIP partner occupies each target promoter — the second anchored on the ChIP observation that CHOP sits on SNAT2, VEGF and CAT-1 without changing their expression.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- `just deep-research human DDIT3 --provider perplexity` fails here (`Provider 'perplexity' not available. Available: claude_code`), so the literature synthesis is in `DDIT3-notes.md` with inline citations, not in a `-deep-research-<provider>.md` file.\n- Draft, because the ISR module and project-page refresh that #3951 also asks for are not included.\n\n`just validate human DDIT3` passes (schema + terms + ontology checks + references + best practices, no warnings). History record added and validated.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","headRefName":"curation/isr-ddit3","number":5855,"title":"Review human DDIT3 (CHOP), the last missing ISR gene","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5855"},{"body":"Bumps [anthropics/claude-code-action](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action) from 1.0.183 to 1.0.264.\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/releases\">anthropics/claude-code-action's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v1.0.264</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>docs: use claude-plugins-official in plugin examples by <a href=\"https://github.com/mhegazy\"><code>@​mhegazy</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/pull/1932\">anthropics/claude-code-action#1932</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.263...v1.0.264\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.263...v1.0.264</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.263</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.262...v1.0.263\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.262...v1.0.263</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.262</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>chore: bump Bun to 1.4.4 by <a href=\"https://github.com/dylan-conway\"><code>@​dylan-conway</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/pull/1916\">anthropics/claude-code-action#1916</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New Contributors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dylan-conway\"><code>@​dylan-conway</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/pull/1916\">anthropics/claude-code-action#1916</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.261...v1.0.262\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.261...v1.0.262</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.261</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.260...v1.0.261\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.260...v1.0.261</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.260</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Pin the PR version a run checks out by <a href=\"https://github.com/OctavianGuzu\"><code>@​OctavianGuzu</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/pull/1915\">anthropics/claude-code-action#1915</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.259...v1.0.260\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.259...v1.0.260</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.259</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.258...v1.0.259\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.258...v1.0.259</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.258</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.257...v1.0.258\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.257...v1.0.258</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.257</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.256...v1.0.257\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.256...v1.0.257</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.256</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.255...v1.0.256\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.255...v1.0.256</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.255</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.254...v1.0.255\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.254...v1.0.255</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.254</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.253...v1.0.254\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.253...v1.0.254</a></p>\n<h2>v1.0.253</h2>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.252...v1.0.253\">https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/v1.0.252...v1.0.253</a></p>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/d70510b174a1244958df0b03166a54912811f3e6\"><code>d70510b</code></a> chore: bump Claude Code to 2.1.312 and Agent SDK to 0.3.312</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/98a9e39fe1e0e81f39cec7f8b24f04c3e39192ed\"><code>98a9e39</code></a> docs: use claude-plugins-official in plugin examples (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/issues/1932\">#1932</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/8041f1d17e0e7d2f2cfe35002962e9f26237c0bb\"><code>8041f1d</code></a> chore: bump Claude Code to 2.1.311 and Agent SDK to 0.3.311</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/925afb53776cf1a2b738c6bdbed37b4d4000ef13\"><code>925afb5</code></a> chore: bump Bun to 1.4.4 (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/issues/1916\">#1916</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/16f9f71ab9c490faf367f94dd294897edc25be33\"><code>16f9f71</code></a> chore: bump Claude Code to 2.1.310 and Agent SDK to 0.3.310</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/8d70a0dd265122bd65b8e09d3d959e65373ae2a1\"><code>8d70a0d</code></a> Pin the PR version a run checks out (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/issues/1915\">#1915</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/ae68417a745a5df4b2124873632bd73a2255e6f6\"><code>ae68417</code></a> chore: bump Claude Code to 2.1.309 and Agent SDK to 0.3.309</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/6b6870e649b5ff408753befeaee5fc7989d4e6a9\"><code>6b6870e</code></a> chore: bump Claude Code to 2.1.308 and Agent SDK to 0.3.308</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/aa8a7738e48bf30a40ee499ce818bef081c5ab5e\"><code>aa8a773</code></a> chore: bump Claude Code to 2.1.307 and Agent SDK to 0.3.307</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/commit/a5a423f75c251dcbff8a74e99f83b2cb03c72bfa\"><code>a5a423f</code></a> chore: bump Claude Code to 2.1.306 and Agent SDK to 0.3.306</li>\n<li>Additional commits viewable in <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/compare/be7b93b1907a4abad570368f3c74b6fe3807510b...d70510b174a1244958df0b03166a54912811f3e6\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=anthropics/claude-code-action&package-manager=github_actions&previous-version=1.0.183&new-version=1.0.264)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by commenting on this PR:\n- `@dependabot rebase` will rebase this PR\n- `@dependabot recreate` will recreate this PR, overwriting any edits that have been made to it\n- `@dependabot show <dependency name> ignore conditions` will show all of the ignore conditions of the specified dependency\n- `@dependabot ignore this major version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this major version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this minor version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this minor version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this dependency` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this dependency (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n\n\n</details>","headRefName":"dependabot/github_actions/anthropics/claude-code-action-1.0.264","number":5853,"title":"Bump anthropics/claude-code-action from 1.0.183 to 1.0.264","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5853"},{"body":"Bumps [actions/upload-artifact](https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact) from 4 to 7.\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/releases\">actions/upload-artifact's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v7.0.0</h2>\n<h2>v7 What's new</h2>\n<h3>Direct Uploads</h3>\n<p>Adds support for uploading single files directly (unzipped). Callers can set the new <code>archive</code> parameter to <code>false</code> to skip zipping the file during upload. Right now, we only support single files. The action will fail if the glob passed resolves to multiple files. The <code>name</code> parameter is also ignored with this setting. Instead, the name of the artifact will be the name of the uploaded file.</p>\n<h3>ESM</h3>\n<p>To support new versions of the <code>@actions/*</code> packages, we've upgraded the package to ESM.</p>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Add proxy integration test by <a href=\"https://github.com/Link\"><code>@​Link</code></a>- in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/754\">actions/upload-artifact#754</a></li>\n<li>Upgrade the module to ESM and bump dependencies by <a href=\"https://github.com/danwkennedy\"><code>@​danwkennedy</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/762\">actions/upload-artifact#762</a></li>\n<li>Support direct file uploads by <a href=\"https://github.com/danwkennedy\"><code>@​danwkennedy</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/764\">actions/upload-artifact#764</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New Contributors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Link\"><code>@​Link</code></a>- made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/754\">actions/upload-artifact#754</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/compare/v6...v7.0.0\">https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/compare/v6...v7.0.0</a></p>\n<h2>v6.0.0</h2>\n<h2>v6 - What's new</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[!IMPORTANT]\nactions/upload-artifact@v6 now runs on Node.js 24 (<code>runs.using: node24</code>) and requires a minimum Actions Runner version of 2.327.1. If you are using self-hosted runners, ensure they are updated before upgrading.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Node.js 24</h3>\n<p>This release updates the runtime to Node.js 24. v5 had preliminary support for Node.js 24, however this action was by default still running on Node.js 20. Now this action by default will run on Node.js 24.</p>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Upload Artifact Node 24 support by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/719\">actions/upload-artifact#719</a></li>\n<li>fix: update <code>@​actions/artifact</code> for Node.js 24 punycode deprecation by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/744\">actions/upload-artifact#744</a></li>\n<li>prepare release v6.0.0 for Node.js 24 support by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/745\">actions/upload-artifact#745</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/compare/v5.0.0...v6.0.0\">https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/compare/v5.0.0...v6.0.0</a></p>\n<h2>v5.0.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<p><strong>BREAKING CHANGE:</strong> this update supports Node <code>v24.x</code>. This is not a breaking change per-se but we're treating it as such.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Update README.md by <a href=\"https://github.com/GhadimiR\"><code>@​GhadimiR</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/681\">actions/upload-artifact#681</a></li>\n<li>Update README.md by <a href=\"https://github.com/nebuk89\"><code>@​nebuk89</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/712\">actions/upload-artifact#712</a></li>\n<li>Readme: spell out the first use of GHES by <a href=\"https://github.com/danwkennedy\"><code>@​danwkennedy</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/727\">actions/upload-artifact#727</a></li>\n<li>Update GHES guidance to include reference to Node 20 version by <a href=\"https://github.com/patrikpolyak\"><code>@​patrikpolyak</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/725\">actions/upload-artifact#725</a></li>\n<li>Bump <code>@actions/artifact</code> to <code>v4.0.0</code></li>\n<li>Prepare <code>v5.0.0</code> by <a href=\"https://github.com/danwkennedy\"><code>@​danwkennedy</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/pull/734\">actions/upload-artifact#734</a></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/cf430e030ddbb5b0abf93d22962f4752f3646cd9\"><code>cf430e0</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/822\">#822</a> from actions/tunc-d-prepare-upload-v7-0-2</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/c2acdedd35a8824a1552c7137f8c18288c3ea469\"><code>c2acded</code></a> chore: prepare v7.0.2 release</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/4687c1bbaa0a58abb517d90b4bbade6754c80c1a\"><code>4687c1b</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/821\">#821</a> from actions/tunc-d-upload-artifact-package-bump</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/8cbe1bebdb858105d8fdd88f81404d4de4595719\"><code>8cbe1be</code></a> Bump <code>@​actions/artifact</code> to 6.3.1</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/043fb46d1a93c77aae656e7c1c64a875d1fc6a0a\"><code>043fb46</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/797\">#797</a> from actions/yacaovsnc/update-dependency</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/634250c1388765ea7ed0f053e636f1f399000b94\"><code>634250c</code></a> Include changes in typespec/ts-http-runtime 0.3.5</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/e454baaac2be505c9450e11b8f3215c6fc023ce8\"><code>e454baa</code></a> Readme: bump all the example versions to v7 (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/796\">#796</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/74fad66b98a6d799dc004d3353ccd0e6f6b2530e\"><code>74fad66</code></a> Update the readme with direct upload details (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/795\">#795</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/bbbca2ddaa5d8feaa63e36b76fdaad77386f024f\"><code>bbbca2d</code></a> Support direct file uploads (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/764\">#764</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/commit/589182c5a4cec8920b8c1bce3e2fab1c97a02296\"><code>589182c</code></a> Upgrade the module to ESM and bump dependencies (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/762\">#762</a>)</li>\n<li>Additional commits viewable in <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/compare/v4...v7\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=actions/upload-artifact&package-manager=github_actions&previous-version=4&new-version=7)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by commenting on this PR:\n- `@dependabot rebase` will rebase this PR\n- `@dependabot recreate` will recreate this PR, overwriting any edits that have been made to it\n- `@dependabot show <dependency name> ignore conditions` will show all of the ignore conditions of the specified dependency\n- `@dependabot ignore this major version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this major version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this minor version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this minor version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this dependency` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this dependency (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n\n\n</details>","headRefName":"dependabot/github_actions/actions/upload-artifact-7","number":5852,"title":"Bump actions/upload-artifact from 4 to 7","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5852"},{"body":"## Summary\n- `modules/phenylacetate_catabolism.yaml` moves to `status: COMPLETE` and is generalized to Bacteria (`scope: CONCRETE`, NCBITaxon:2).\n  - E. coli K-12 representatives are added beside P. putida KT2440.\n  - The PaaABCE complex gets GO:0062077.\n  - New evidence from Teufel 2010, Grishin 2011 and Grishin 2012, with verbatim quotes.\n  - Leaf molecular functions are refined (GO:0016651, GO:0016863, GO:0016803, GO:0033721).\n- PANTHER groundings from #5076 were checked against panther.obo, the PANTHER 19.0 member index, UniProt and InterPro, then refined to the Paa subfamilies.\n  - PaaB is grounded to NCBIfam TIGR02157.\n  - The rat Echs1 representative from #5076 is not adopted, because it does not perform this step.\n  - Two members have conflicting PANTHER and UniProt assignments, documented in the module notes and family reviews: PaaG (PTHR43459:SF1 vs PTHR11941:SF133) and PaaJ (PTHR43853:SF2 vs PTHR18919:SF12).\n- Family reviews:\n  - New: PTHR43439, PTHR30458, PTHR47354, PTHR43459, PTHR43111, PTHR11941, PTHR48075.\n  - Updated: PTHR43853, and PTHR18919 (merged with #5347's PcaF curation).\n  - All PAINT nodes are assessed, and every reference protein has a COMPLETE gene review.\n- Gene reviews:\n  - 10 new COMPLETE E. coli K-12 paa reviews.\n  - 10 PSEPK paa reviews completed: unvetted UniProt quotes replaced, harmonized with the E. coli orthologs, `record_section` backfilled.\n  - Source defects recorded for UniProt/EcoCyc.\n- Module and gene pages are rendered, with 30 history records.\n\n## Validation\n- `linkml-validate -C ModuleReview`: no issues found.\n- `module_validator`: passed. Locally, its only warnings are PANTHER member-index lookups.\n- module_qc: 100% recommended-field compliance, no conformance violations, 20/20 member reviews complete.\n- `just validate`: clean for all 20 genes.\n- Family reviews:\n  - schema, term and reference checks pass;\n  - family_gene_crosscheck passes;\n  - the residue validator run on the 9 touched files gives 251 pass, 0 fail.\n- All history records validate, and the branch merges cleanly against current `main`.\n- #5076 edits the same module lines, so whichever PR merges second needs a conflict resolution.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_016p6NCPLmxKu9HCtbWVsUDT)_","headRefName":"claude/pensive-darwin-e7gi2m-phenylacetate-catabolism","number":5851,"title":"Promote phenylacetate catabolism module to COMPLETE (bacteria-wide, E. coli + P. putida exemplars)","url":"https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/5851"}]
